������� ��������� ��� ��� ������������ ����\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������� �������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������ ��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��� ���� ���� ������������ ������������������������� ����� ��� ���������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n���������������������������������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |222", "t": "Digi Yatra Facility", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 222\n(To be answered on the 22nd December 2022)\n \nDIGI YATRA FACILITY\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has introduced Digi Yatra for digital travel experience based on\nfacial biometrics on airports in the country and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(b) the names of airports in which such a facility is currently being provided, airport-wise;\n \n(c) the names of the domestic and international airlines which would provide Digi Yatra facility\nto the passengers in the country, airlines-wise;\n \n(d) whether the Government has developed a Digi Yatra app to facilitate the passengers and if\nso, the details thereof; and\n \n(e) the steps being taken by the Government to start Digi Yatra in all the airports across the\ncountry to facilitate the passengers and reduce the burden of cumbersome check-in and\nboarding process along with the time by which such facility would be operational? \n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*222. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in the reply to Lok Sabha Starred (*) Question No. 222 for 22.12.2022\nregarding \"Digi Yatra\"\n \n(a) & (b) Yes, Sir. Digi Yatra has been launched on 01.12.2022 at Delhi, Bangalore and Varanasi\nAirports for providing passengers seamless and hassle-free experience based on facial\nbiometrics.\n \n(c) Digi Yatra Facility is only for domestic airlines. Presently, all domestic airlines at Delhi and\nBangalore Airports are providing this facility. At Varanasi Airport, the facility is currently\nprovided by Indigo, Vistara and Air India.\n \n(d) Yes, Sir. Digi Yatra App has been developed to facilitate the passengers. The same is\navailable on android as well as iOS platform.\n \n(e) The implementation of Digi Yatra under phase I is planned at Kolkata, Pune, Vijayawada\nand Hyderabad Airports by March 2023. It is to be implemented across various airports in the\ncountry in phased manner. \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |223", "t": "Impetus to MSMEs in Industrial Manufacturing Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 223 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2022 \n \nDOUBLING WORK OF JHANSI-MANIKPUR RAILWAY LINE  \n \n������������������������������������������������������ \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the doubling work of Jhansi -Manikpur and Banda -Kanpur railway \nlines is being carried out under North Central Railway; \n(b) if so, the time by which the said work is likely to be started and  planned to \nbe completed; \n(c) whether the doubling work of Jhansi -Manikpur railway line under North -\nCentral Railway has already been taken up; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to  (d) Yes , Sir.  North Central Railway has taken up doubling of Jhansi -\nManikpur and Khairar -Bhimsen (425 Km).  Sanctioned Cost of project is �� 4330 \nCrore.  Works on the project have been taken up.  So far an expenditure of �� 151 \nCrore has been incurred on the  project.  An outlay of �� 400 Crore has been \nprovided for the project for financial year 2022-23.  \n \nThe completion of any Railway project(s) depends on various factors like quick \nland acquisition by State Government, forest clearance by officials of forest  \ndepartment, shifting of infringing utilities, statutory clearances from various \nauthorities, geological and topographical conditions of area, law and order \nsituation in the area of project(s) site, number of working months in a year for \nparticular project site due to climatic conditions etc. and all these factors affect \nthe completion time of the project(s).  With these constraints, Railways are \nmaking all efforts to complete these projects at the earliest. \n ***** �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |224", "t": "Conversion of SHs into NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA                                                                                                            \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA                                                                                                                                 \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 224                                                                                                           \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2022 \n \nK-RAIL (SILVERLINE) PROJECT \n \n224.  SHRI HIBI EDEN : \n \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has noticed that without proper and legal \nsanction for the Silverline semi-high speed rail project, the Government of \nKerala is proceeding with the works of the same, if so, the facts and details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government of Kerala has submitted new amplifications or \nmagnifications for the K -Rail (Silverline) project for approval and permission, on \nor after 20.07.2022 and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Kerala Rail Development Corporation Limited (K -Rail) has not \nyet given technical documents and other details, including the alignment of the \nSilverline project, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has re -examined or scrutinized the project on or \nafter 20.07.2022 and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) whether the consideration of the project is likely to depend upon soil \ncondition, natural drainage, env ironmental issues, interoperability, debt \nserviceability with mainly passenger traffic of the project, if so, the details of all \npre-conditions that may be considered before the Union Government grants \npermission/issue NOC; and \n \n(f) whether the Government is aware of the delay that occurred in issuing \npermission/NOC in the case of the K-Rail Silverline Project and if so, the stand of \nthe Government in this regard? \n \n \n   ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \n ELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n   (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW)  \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f ) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 224 BY SHRI HIBI EDEN TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON \n07.12.2022 REGARDING K-RAIL (SILVER LINE) PROJECT. \n \n(a): No, Sir. Kerala Rail Development Corporation Ltd.(KRDCL) a Joint \nVenture Company of State Govt. of Kerala (51%) and Ministry of Railways \n(49%) has taken up the work of Silver Line (Semi High Speed Rail) from \nThiruvananthapuram to Kasaragod (530.6 Km.) for development. In Principle \nApproval (IPA) of Railway Board has been granted for this project for taking \nup pre-investment activities. Granting of IPA means go ahead for preparation \nof DPR etc which brings out the complete detail s of the project including \nfinancials. \n(b) to (d):  No, Sir. DPR is under examination by Ministry of Railways. \nSufficient details for technical feasibility are not available in the DPR. \nTherefore, KRDCL has been advised to provide detailed technical docume nts \nsuch as alignment plan, particulars of railway land and private land, \ncrossings over existing railway network, duly depicting affected railway \nasset through Zonal Railway for detailed examination of the project and to \narrive at conclusion about feasibi lity of project, which is awaited from \nKRDCL. \n(e): Financial viability will be examined and firmed up after finalisation of \ntechnical parameters. Consideration of the project will depend upon techno -\neconomic viability of the project. \n(f): The Project is no t yet sanctioned.  Therefore, question of delay does not \narise. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |225", "t": "Privatisation of Airports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 225\n(To be answered on the 22nd December 2022)\n \nPRIVATISATION OF AIRPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to privatize more airports as part of\nproposed monetisation;\n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n \n(c) the amount of revenue that the Government expects to earn therefrom along\nwith the steps taken to ensure job safety of existing employees serving in the\nairline?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*225. PROF SOUGATA RAY\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement in reply to parts (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred (*) Question No. 225\nfor answer on 22.12.2022 regarding \"Privatisation of Airports\"\n \n(a) to (c): As per National Monetization Pipeline (NMP), 25 Airports Authority of\nIndia (AAI) airports namely Bhubaneshwar, Varanasi, Amritsar, Trichy, Indore,\nRaipur, Calicut, Coimbatore, Nagpur, Patna, Madurai, Surat, Ranchi, Jodhpur,\nChennai, Vijayawada, Vadodara, Bhopal, Tirupati, Hubli, Imphal, Agartala,\nUdaipur, Dehradun and Rajahmundry have been earmarked for leasing over the\nyears 2022 to 2025, considering proceeds of Rs. 10782 crore. However, the exact\nvalue of proceeds that the Government would receive is dependent upon many\nfactors including transaction timing, market conditions, investor appetite,\ntransaction terms etc.\n \nInterests of the AAI employees deployed at the airports brought under PPP have\nbeen fully protected. As per the Concession Agreement with the concessionaires of\nrecently leased six AAI airports under PPP, the employees up to the level of\nAssistant General Manager will continue to be posted at respective Airport  for 3\nyears (i.e. 1 year Joint Management period followed by 2 years deemed deputation\nperiod). Also, the Concessionaire is liable to extend the offer of appointment to\nminimum 60% of employees on terms and conditions not inferior to the existing\nterms. Subsequently, employees have the option to join the Concessionaire or to\nreturn to AAI.\n \nThere is however no impact of leasing of airports on the job safety of existing\nemployees serving in airlines.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |226", "t": "Maintenance of National Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n       \nLOK SABHA \n   STARRED QUESTION NO.226  \n   ANSWERED ON 22ND DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nMAINTENANCE OF NATIONAL HIGHWAYS \n†*226.SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA: \n SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has conducted any survey or study \nregarding sharp turns, potholes, steep grades, narrow/single lanes or \nother accident prone points on National Highways (NHs) in Bhuj, \nAbdasa, Gandhidham, Rapar, Mandvi and Anjar districts of Kutch Lok \nSabha Constituency and in Madhya Pradesh including Panaa, Katni \nand Chhatarpur districts; \n(b)if so, the details thereof; \n(c)whether the Government has initiated/proposes to initiate any \ncorrective measures to make these NHs safe for commuters; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (d)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n  \n\n STATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 226 ANSWERED ON  \n22.12.2022 ASKED BY SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA & SHRI VISHNU DATT \nSHARMA REGARDING “MAINTENANCE OF NATIONAL HIGHWAYS” \n(a)&(b) Design, construction and maintenance of National Highways \n(NHs) are carried ou t as per Indian Roads Congress (IRC) \nstandards/guidelines, guidelines issued by Ministry of Road \nTransport & Highways (MoRTH), relevant international standards \nand good industry practices . Road safety audit is conducted at \nvarious stages of project impleme ntation like project preparation, \nconstruction and operation following such relevant \nstandards/guidelines. During road safety audit, deficiencies in the \ngeometrics and other features of a road including sharp turns, steep \ngrades, and accident prone points impacting safety of road users \nare identified and necessary corrective measures are taken. \nMinistry and its executing agencies follow standard bidding \ndocuments such as (i) Engineering Procurement Contract (EPC) (ii) \nHybrid Annuity Mode Concession Agreemen t and (iii) \nBOT(Toll/Annuity) Concession Agreement. Such \ncontract/concession agreements have provisions to make the \ncontractor/concessionaire responsible for safety auditing at design \nstage, during construction, before opening to traffic and during \noperations and taking necessary corrective measures which are \nbeing followed in all NH projects. Details of road safety audits \nconducted  in Bhuj, Abdasa, Gandhidham, Rapar, Mandvi and Anjar \ntalukas of Kutch Lok Sabha Constituency  in Gujarat and Panna, \nKatni & Chhatarpur districts in Madhya Pradesh are at Annexure -I. \n \n(c)&(d) Corrective measures from safety requirements are taken as \npart of widening/upgradation projects or on standalone basis \ndepending upon site conditions. Details of the widening/upgradation \nworks and standalone basis road safety works being \nundertaken/completed in Bhuj, Abdasa, Gandhidham, Rapar, Mandvi \nand Anjar talukas of Kutch Lok Sabha Constituency  in Gujarat and \nPanaa, Katni and Chhatarpur districts in Madhya Pradesh are at \nAnnexure-II. Besides, in order to make the NHs pothole free, regular \nrepair and maintenance activities are carried out.  \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE I \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN PARTS (a) AND (b)   OF STATEMENT \nREGARDING REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 226 \nANSWERED ON 22.12.2022 ASKED BY SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA & \nSHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA “MAINTENANCE OF NATIONAL \nHIGHWAYS” \n  \nROAD SAFETY AUDITS CONDUCTED   ON NHs IN BHUJ, ABDASA, \nGANDHIDHAM, RAPAR, MA NDVI AND ANJAR TALUKAS OF KUTCH \nLOK SABHA CONSTITUENCY \nSl \nNo. \nLok Sabha \nConstituency \nNH No. Length (Km) Road Safety Audit \nConducted in \nLength (km) \n1   \nKutch \nConstituency \n341 162.70 97.5 \n2 754K 264.00 80.00 \n3 927C 57.34 0 \n4 41 243.65 164.67 \n5 141 13.98 8.98 \n6 27 88.30 88.30 \n  Total   829.97 439.25 \n  \n  \nROAD SAFETY AUDIT CONDUCTED ON NHs IN CHATTARPUR, \nPANNA & KATNI DISTRICTS OF MADHYA PRADESH \n          \nSl. No District NH no. Length (km) \nRoad Safety \nAudit conducted \nin Length (km)  \n1 \nChattarpur \n339 B 10 10 \n2 339 8 0 \n3 39 6 6 \n4 43Ext 76 31 \n5 12A Ext 10 10 \n6 \nPanna \n39 92 92 \n7 43Ext 79 70 \n8 943 43 43 \n9 Katni 43Ext 49 49 \nTOTAL 373 311 \n  \n\nANNEXURE II \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN PARTS (c) AND (d)   OF STATEMENT \nREGARDING REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 226 \nANSWERED ON 22.12.2022 ASKED BY SHRI VINOD L. CHAVDA & \nSHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA “MAINTENANCE OF NATIONAL \nHIGHWAYS” \nDistrict/Lok \nSabha \nConstituency \nWidening/Upgradation/Standalone \nRoad Safety  works \nStatus \n  \n  \n  \n  \nKutch \nConstituency \nBhuj- Khavda- Dharamshala on \nNH-341 \nIn progress \n  \nLakhpat- Hajipir- Dholavira- \nSantalpur  on NH-754K \nWork in progress in \n80 km length. In \nbalance 202 km \nlength DPR is in \nprogress \nMandavi Bypass on NH41 In progress \n  \nMandavi-Narayan Sarovar on NH -\n41 \nBidding stage. \nKm 74/200 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |227", "t": "Operational Criteria for Aircraft", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 227\n(To be answered on the 22nd December 2022)\n \nOPERATIONAL CRITERIA FOR AIRCRAFT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that many of the private airlines\noperating in the country are technologically archaic and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) whether the Government has any criteria for determining the airworthy\nconditions of aircraft including its lifespan and if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether several old aircraft of various airlines were unable to meet basic\noperational criteria and if so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n*****\n*227. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question\nNo. 227 regarding \"Operational Criteria for Aircraft\" to be answered on\n22.12.2022\n(a) & (b) The Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has not\nprescribed guidelines specifying life for an aircraft to fly in India. Aircraft is\nconsidered airworthy provided the maintenance is as per approved schedule\nlaid down by the manufacturer. Aircraft registered in India may operate as\nlong as the aircraft is under maintenance support provided by manufacturer\nfor the continuous operation of the aircraft.\nThe aircraft may be withdrawn from operation by an operator, in case it\nbecomes 'beyond economic repair' or is 'permanently withdrawn from use'\ndue to any reason such as unavailability of spares, etc.\nHowever, DGCA has laid down age criteria for an aircraft to be imported into\nIndia as under:\n(1) Aircraft intended to be imported and used in passenger services\n(Scheduled/ Non-Scheduled) and General Aviation\n(i) Pressurized aircraft restricted to 18 years of age or 65% of designed\neconomic life in terms of pressurization cycle, whichever is earlier\n(ii) Non Pressurized aircraft (intended to import in India) allowed up to 20\nyears of age on case to case basis agreed by DGCA.\n(2) Aircraft to be used for air cargo operations restricted to 25 years in age or\n75% of its designed economic life in terms of pressurization cycles, whichever\nis earlier.\n(c) No sir. No aircraft shall fly without having valid Airworthiness Review\nCertificate (ARC) as per Rule 15 of the Aircraft Rules, 1937. The\nresponsibility of the operator is to keep the aircraft continuous airworthy and\nin a controlled environment. The aircraft operating in India are periodically\ninspected for compliance with DGCA regulations.The aircraft can also be\nwithdrawn by the airline from flying when the spare parts and consumables\nto maintain the aircraft are not available from OEM & Manufacturer to keep\nit airworthy.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |228", "t": "Fund Sharing under AMRUT Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 228 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 22, 2022 \n \nFUND SHARING UNDER AMRUT SCHEME \n \nNO. 228.   SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of Atal Mission for Rejuvenation an d Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT) scheme including fund sharin g pattern \nbetween Centre and States; \n(b)  the details of the cities where AMRUT scheme i s running in the \ncities of Jharkhand;  \n(c)  the details of fund allocation and utilization  thereof in the State of \nJharkhand under the scheme, project and district-wise; \n(d)  whether there exists specific guidelines to mo nitor and supervise \nthe implementation of the scheme at district and State level; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof along with the moni toring committees \nconstituted for the purpose and the number of such committees’ \nmeetings held during the last five years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the  House. \n***** \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PART (a) TO (e) O F THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION No. *228 DUE FOR ANSWER IN T HE LOK \nSABHA ON 22 DECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING “FUND SHARING UNDER \nAMRUT SCHEME”: \n \n(a):   Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformat ion (AMRUT) \nwas launched on 25 th June 2015 for a period of 05 years i.e. Financial \nYear 2015-16 to 2019-20with a total Central outlay of ₹50,000 crore in \n500 selected cities across all the States and Union  Territories (UTs) in \nthe country. The Mission focuses on the development  of basic urban \ninfrastructure in the sectors of water supply; sewe rage and septage \nmanagement; storm water drainage; non-motorized urb an transport \nand development of green spaces & parks. Mission al so executed a \nset of 11 reforms to improve governance of Urban Lo cal Bodies (ULBs) \nand Capacity Building activities for the ULBs.  \n Under AMRUT, total approved plan size of all the S tates and UTs \nis ₹77,640 crore, which includes committed Central Assistance of \n₹35,990 crore. Through this, the Mission envisions to provide 139 lakh \nwater tap connections to achieve universal coverage  of water supply \nand 145 lakh sewer connections to enhance substanti al sewerage \nnetwork coverage in AMRUT cities. AMRUT has now bee n subsumed \nunder Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Trans formation 2.0 \n(AMRUT 2.0) launched on 01 \nst October 2021. Accordingly, all ongoing \nand eligible projects of AMRUT shall receive Centra l funding till \n31 st March 2023. \n\n Fund sharing pattern between Centre and States for  AMRUT \nprojects is as under: \nSl. \nNo.  \nCategory of \nState / Union \nTerritory \nSector  Category of \nAMRUT city in \nterms of \npopulation (as \nper 2011 census) \nMaximum \nCentral \nShare \nagainst \nproject \ncost* \nState/ULB/ \nOther’s \nshare \nagainst \nproject \ncost \ni. \nFor all Union \nTerritories \nAll Sectors  For a ll AMRUT \ncities \nirrespective of \npopulation \n100%  - \nii. \nFor 08 North -\nEastern \nStates and \nHimalayan \nStates \n(Himachal \nPradesh and \nUttarakhand)  \nAll Sectors  For a ll AMRUT \ncitiesirrespective \nof population \n90%  10%  \niii. \nAll remaining \nStates \nWater \nSupply, \nSewerage \nand Septage \nManagement, \nStorm Water \nDrainage and \nUrban \nTransport \n(non-\nmotorized) \nCities having \npopulation up to \n10 lakhs \n50%  50%  \niv. \nCities having \npopulation above \n10 lakhs \n33.33%  66.67%  \nv. Parks/ Green For all AMRUT \ncities \n50%  50%  \n\nSpaces  irrespective of \npopulation \n* Limited to maximum committed Central Assistance. \n \n(b):  List of 7 cities selected under AMRUT in the State of \nJharkhand is as under: \nS.No.  Name of the City \n1 Adityapur \n2 Chas \n3 Deoghar \n4 Dhanbad \n5 Giridih \n6 Hazaribag \n7 Ranchi \n \n(c):  Under AMRUT, Central funds are not allocated project or \ndistrict-wise. For the State of Jharkhand, State An nual Action Plans \n(SAAPs) amounting to ₹1,246crore, including committ ed Central \nAssistance of ₹566 crore have been approved for the  entire Mission \nperiod. So far, ₹552crore has been released to the State as Central \nfunds for implementation of projects under the Miss ion, against which \nState Government has submitted Utilisation Certific ates for ₹386 \ncrore. District-wise details of projects taken up u nder AMRUT in the \nState of Jharkhand and expenditure reported so far has been placed \nas ANNEXURE-I. \n \n\n(d) & (e): Under AMRUT, States have been empowered to select, \napprise, approve and implement projects within the broad framework \nof AMRUT Mission. Further, AMRUT guidelines have sp ecific provisions \nfor formation of State High Powered Steering Commit tee (SHPSC), \nheaded by the Chief Secretary of the State, to moni tor and supervise \nthe implementation of the scheme at the State level . Apart from this, a \nState Level Technical Committee (SLTC) headed by th e Secretary, \nUrban Development & Housing Department als"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |229", "t": "Financial Assistance from CRIF", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 229 \nANSWERED ON 22ndDECEMBER, 2022 \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n*229. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK:   \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n (a) whether each district has been included in the financial \nassistance given to Jharkhand under the Central Road and \nInfrastructure Fund (CRIF); \n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise; and \n(c) the targets set for development and maintenance of roads in the \nState in the coming three years? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on Table of the House.  \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 229 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n22.12.2022, ASKED BY SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK  REGARDING \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n(a) AND (b) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways (MoRTH) \napproves works on the State Roads under the Central Road and \nInfrastructure Fund (CRIF) scheme as per recommendations of the \nconcerned State Government. Works on State Roads under CRIF are \napproved based on accrual under CRIF and proposals received from \nState Governments. The scheme earlier started as Central Road Fund \n(CRF) in the year 2000.  Since year 2000, this Ministry has approved  \n40 numbers of works amounting to Rs. 1368.64 Crores in the State of \nJharkhand. Out of total 24 districts of Jharkhand, CRIF works has \nbeen sanctioned in 17 districts.  The details of works approved on \nstate roads by MoRTH under CRF are at Annexure-I. \n(c) This Ministry is primarily responsible for development a nd \nmaintenance of the National Highways (NHs) in the country. MoRTH \nhas identified about 1573 km length of NHs with an estimated cost of \nRs.  39097 Crore for development of NHs during the next three years \nin Jharkhand state. Details are at Annexure-II.   \nMaintenance of the NHs is a continuous process which is taken \nup as per condition of the existing road, traffic density, inter -se \npriority and availability of funds to keep the NHs in the traffic-worthy \ncondition.   \n  \n\n  \n  \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) AND (b) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 229 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n22.12.2022, ASKED BY SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK  REGARDING \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FROM CRIF \n  \n(Completed Road works under CRIF since year 2000 in Jharkhand) \n  \nSl No. \nName of work  District Cost       (Rs. \nin Crore) \n1 Widening & strengthening of Chaibasa-Jaintgarh Road from \nKM 35.00 to 58.00 in Hatgamharia Jaintgarh (Chaibasa-\nDiv.) \nWest \nSinghbhum 6.41 \n2 Widening & Strengthening of Gola-Muri road km 0.00 to \n27.00  (Hazaribagh-Div) \nRamgarh & \nRanchi 5.35 \n3 Widening &Strengthening of Balipur-Patla Bari from KM \n1.00 to 5.00 Chirkunda  Patchet Road KM 1.00 to 7.20 \n(Dhanbad-Div.) \nDhanbad 4.12 \n4 Widening & Strengthening of Khunti-Tamar road from \nKM  1.00 to 44.00 km (Kunti-Div) Khunti 9.91 \n5 Construction of Approaches and Protection  work to Jumar \nBridge in KM 119.00 of NH-33 (NH Ranchi -Div.) Ranchi 0.97 \n6 Strengthening of Daltonganj-Hariharganj Road from KM \n0.00 to 71.40 (Daltonganj-Div.) Palamu 12.41 \n7 Widening & Strengthening of Deoghar-Madhupur via \nMohanpur Road from KM 1.00 to 29.00 (Deoghar-Div.)  Deoghar 9.82 \n8 \nWidening & Strengthening of Kunti - Torpa Kolebira- Road \nfrom km 13.00 to 56.00 & 56 to 81  \nKhunti & \nGumla 33.16 \n9 Widening & Strengthening of Dhab-Pihra-Kalidih Road \n(Koderma Div) Koderma 11.43 \n10 Widening & Strengthening of Barabhoom-Bandoyan Road \nfrom km  0.00 to 10.80 (Jhamshedpur Div) East Singhbhum 8.80 \n11 Widening & Strengthening of Hata -Tring Road from km \n0.00 to 13.165(Jamshed pur-Div.) \nSaraikela-\nKharsawan 9.19 \n12 \nWidening & Strengthening of Jamshedpur -Hata Road from \nkm 0.00 to 18.100 (Jamshedpure- Div.) \nEast Singhbhum \n& Saraikela-\nKharsawan \n19.16 \n13 Construction of PCC Road (350M) including Drain culvert \netc. in Firayalal Chowk to Karamtoli Chowk (Ranchi-Div.)  Ranchi 0.70 \n14 Construction of H.L. RCC Bridge Over Banai River in 8th \nkm of Khunti-Topra-Kolebira Road (Khunti Div.) Khunti 1.62 \n15 Widening & Strengthening of Sarath-Palajori road \n(Deoghar- Div.) Deoghar 15.97 \n\n16 Widening & Strengthening of Godda-Pirpaity Road (km \n9.00 to km 16) (Godda Div) Godda 12.54 \n17 Widening & Strengthening of Godda-Pirpaity Road (km \n17.00 to km 24) (Godda Div) Godda 11.94 \n18 Widening & Strengthening of Godda-Pirpaity Road (km \n25.00 to km 32) (Godda Div) Godda 11.37 \n19 Widening & Strengthening of Godda-Pirpaity Road (km \n33.00 to km 42) (Godda Div) Godda 12.55 \n20 Widening & Strengthening of Godda-Pirpaity Road (km \n43.00 to km 52) (Godda Div) Godda 12.13 \n21 Widening & Strengthening of  Godda - Pirpaity Road (km \n53.00 to km 61.875) (Godda Div) Godda 12.56 \n22 Construction of High Level R.c.c. Bridge (with foothpath ) \nover Kajhia Ri"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |23", "t": "Diversion of Flood Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *23 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \nDIVERSION OF FLOOD WATER \n*23 SHRI MALOOK NAGAR \n SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to divert the surplus water from flood prone areas to the drought \nhit areas in order to address the annual crisis of recurring floods and droughts in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to provide relief from flood and ensure proper use of rain \nwater and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI    \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 23 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 08.1 2.2022 REGARDING \n“DIVERSION OF FLOOD WATER” ASKED BY SHRI MALOOK NAG AR AND SHRI \nVIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR:, HON’BLE M.Ps. \n \n(a) & (b)  The large temporal and spatial variation in availa bility of water in country leads to \nrecurrent floods in some parts, while some other pa rts experience drought. Interlinking of Rivers \n(ILR) programme has been envisaged to reduce region al imbalance in the availability of water. \nNational Perspective Plan (NPP) for Water Resources  Development was formulated by \nGovernment of India in 1980 for providing storages and transfer of surplus waters to water \ndeficit regions so as to mitigate the effect of ann ually recurring floods and also to minimize the \nincidence of droughts.  \nThe National Perspective Plan would give additional  benefits of 25 million hectares of irrigation \nfrom surface waters, 10 million hectares by increased use of ground water, 34,000 MW of hydro-\npower generation and other benefits like augmenting  domestic & industrial water supply, \nnavigational facilities, employment generation, fis heries, salinity control, pollution abatement, \netc. National Water Development Agency (NWDA) under  the Ministry of Jal Shakti has \nidentified 16 links under Peninsular Rivers Compone nt and 14 links under Himalayan \nComponent of NPP for inter basin transfer. Pre-Feas ibility Reports (PFRs) of all the 30 links \nhave been completed and Feasibility Reports (FRs) o f 24 links and Detailed Project Reports \n(DPRs) of eight links have been completed. The Govt . of India has approved the implementation \nof Ken- Betwa Link Project (KBLP), one of the link under Peninsular Rivers Component, in \nDecember, 2021 with an estimated cost of Rs 44605 C rores with central support of Rs 39317 \nCrores through a Special Purpose Vehicle viz; Ken B etwa Link Project Authority (KBLPA). \nFurther, under the intra-State link projects, NWDA received 49 link proposals from the 10 \nStates, out of which PFRs of 39 link projects and D PRs of six link projects were completed and \nsent to concerned States.  \n \n(c)   Central/State Governments have accorded high prio rity to the optimum utilisation of flood \nwater through construction of storage and diversion  projects. Water resources projects are \nprimarily planned, funded, executed and maintained by the State Government as per their own \nneed, priority and available resources. In order to  supplement their efforts, Government of India \nprovide technical and financial assistance to State  Governments to encourage sustainable \n\ndevelopment and efficient management of water resou rces through various schemes and \nprogrammes such as Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP), Har Khet ko Pani \n(HKKP), etc. under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY). Many of the projects \nunder PMKSY store flood water during monsoon season  in their reservoirs and multi-purpose \nstorages of these projects have helped greatly in m oderating the intensity of flood in the \ndownstream flood plain area. To ensure proper use o f rain water in the country, Central Ground \nwater Board (CGWB) has prepared a master Plan for A rtificial Recharge to Groundwater - 2020 \nin consultation with State Government /Union Territ ories which is a macro level plan indicating \nvarious structures for different terrain conditions  of the country. Ministry of Jal Shkati has \nlaunched “Jal Shakti Abhiyan: Catch the Rain” with the tagline “Catch the Rain where it falls, \nwhen it falls” to nudge states and stakeholders to create appropriate Rain Water Harvesting \nstructures(RWHS), suitable to the climatic condition and sub soil strata of the area, with people’s \nactive participation, during the pre monsoon and mo nsoon period. With a view to conserve water \nfor the future, the Union Government launched “Miss ion Amrit Sarovar” on 24 th  April 2022 \naimed at developing and rejuvenating 75 water bodies in each district of the country.  \nTo strengthen the structural measures of flood mage ment, Ministry had implemented during XI \n& XII Plan Flood Management"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |230", "t": "Setting up of Solar Parks", "head": "�\n�\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������� �\n� �\n��������������������������������������������� �\n�������������������� � �������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n������������ ����\n�������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������� ������������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ������ ���� ������ ��� ����������� ��������� ��� ��������