�� ���\n� � ��� ���������������� ������������������������� �\n�\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������� ������������������������������� �\n�\n�\n�� ������������������������������� ����������������������������������������\n���� ����� ����� �������� ��� ����������� ��� ���� ��� ��� ��������� ��� ������\n�������� ����������� ��� ���������� � ����� �������� ��� ����������� ���� ����\n������������ � �������� ������������ ������� ����� �������� ������� ����\n�������������������� ����������� ��������������������������������������� ��\n�\n�\n�������������������������������������������� �\n�\n�\n������ ����� ���������� �������� ���������� ������ ��� ������ ��������� ����� ������\n������ ���������� ���������� ��������� ������ �������� ���������� �������\n���������� �������������� ��������� ���������� ����� � ������ ����� �����\n���������� �������� � ��� ���� � �� � ���� � ��� ���� ��� ������ ����� �������� �������\n������� ������ �������������������� ���� ���� ���������� ��� ���������� �����\n����������� ���� ���� ���� ��� ������� ���� ���������� ������� ������� �� ���� ����\n�������������������������������� ����\n�\n����������������� �������� ��� ������ ��������� ���� ���� ������� ���� �����������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������\n�\n�\n������������������������������������������������ ���� �������������� ���\n����� �\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |231", "t": "Civic Amenities in Slums", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 231 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 22, 2022 \n \nCIVIC AMENITIES IN SLUMS \n \n \nNO. 231. SHRIMATI PRATIMA MONDAL:  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a) whether the Government has allocated funds to provi de civic \namenities in slums and for development of slums in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof during the last two year s and the current \nyear, State/UT-wise; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to conduct a survey  regarding \nslums in the urban areas; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) whether the Government proposes to take any concret e steps to \nprovide basic amenities such as healthcare to the p eople living in \nthe slums in the metropolitans of the country and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the  House. \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 231* FOR 22.12.2022 REGARDING ‘CIVIC AMENITIES IN \nSLUMS’ \n \n(a) & (b): ‘Land’ and ‘Colonisation’ are State subjects . Schemes related \nto development of the slums including providing civ ic amenities are \nimplemented by States/Union Territories (UTs).  How ever, Central \nGovernment supplements the efforts of States/UTs to  develop \nslums and provide civic services to slum dwellers t hrough various \nMissions, such as Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urba n (PMAY-U), \nAtal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformat ion (AMRUT \n2.0), Swachh Bharat Mission - Urban (SBM-U 2.0).  \n \nUnder PMAY-U, Central Assistance is provided to Sta tes/UTs for \nconstruction of houses for all eligible families/beneficiaries in urban \nareas including slum dwellers. The details of Centr al Assistance \nreleased during the last two years and the current year, State/UT-\nwise is at Annexure-I.  \n \nUnder SBM-U, funds are released to States/UTs for c onstruction of \nCommunity/ Public Toilets and Individual Household Latrines on \nneed basis alongwith Solid Waste Management compone nt in urban \nareas including slums. The State/UT-wise details of  Central Share \nfunds released under SBM-U during the last two year s and current \nyear is at Annexure-II.  \n \nUnder AMRUT Scheme, State Annual Action Plans (SAAP s) \namounting to ₹77,640 crore including committed Cent ral \nAssistance of ₹35,990 crore have been approved for projects in all \nStates/UTs for the entire Mission Period.  So far, Central Assistance \namounting to ₹31,198 crore have been released to St ates/UTs for \nAMRUT projects for water supply, sewerage & septage  \nmanagement, storm water drainage, green spaces & parks and non-\nmotorized urban transport. The State/UT-wise detail s of Central \nfunds released under AMRUT during the last two year s and current \nyear is at Annexure-III. \n \nRecently, AMRUT 2.0 and SBM 2.0 have been launched to make \ncities garbage free and water secure during the mis sion period. \nUnder AMRUT 2.0, so far State Water Action Plans (SWAP) have  \n \n \n\nbeen approved by MoHUA for 4,830 projects worth ₹93 ,800 crores \nsubmitted by States/UTs. Under SBM 2.0, Mission all ocation is \n₹33,993.60 crore against which, ₹1877.23 crore have  been \nreleased.  \n \n(c) & (d): Census of India conducts enumeration of population \nincluding slums on decadal basis in the country. Th e last census \nwas conducted in 2011, according to which, a total of 6.54 crore \npeople were living in 1,08,227 slums across the country .  \n \n(e): Apart from PMAY-U, SBM-U and AMRUT, to provide  civic amenities, \nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare under Nationa l Health \nMission provides financial and technical support to  States/UTs to \nstrengthen the health care systems to provide affor dable \nhealthcare to all the citizens including slum areas . Further, under \nAyushman Bharat, Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana ( AB-PMJAY) \nhealth coverage up to ₹5 lakh per family per year i s provided to all \neligible households including slum dwellers and est ablishing \nHealth and Wellness Centres (HWCs) to provide free and quality \nhealthcare closer to people’s homes including slums.  \nFurther, Central Government is implementing SAUBHAG YA - \nPradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar Yojana, Pradhan  Mantri \nUjjwala Yojana (PMUY) and UJALA scheme in all urban  areas \nincluding slums, amongst others.  \n*****  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I referred in reply to LS SQ No. 231 due for answer on 22.12.2022 \n \nState/UT- wise details of Central Assistance releas ed during last two years and current \nyear (FY 2020-21 to 2022-23) under PMAY-U \n[as on 12 \nth December 2022] \n \nS. No.  Name of the  \nState/ UT  \nCentral Assistance Released during last \ntwo years and current year (₹ in Crores)  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 8,935.71 \n2 Bihar 675.20 \n3 Chhattisgarh 1,568.33 \n4 Goa 46.96 \n5 Gujarat 8,220.95 \n6 Haryana 480.29 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 81.09 \n8 Jharkhand 801.94 \n9 Karnataka 2,184.82 \n10 Kera"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |232", "t": "Scholarship to Minority Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n232\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 0\n8\n.\n12\n.2022\n \n \nDIGITIZATION OF MSMEs\n \n \n232.\n \n \nSHRIMATI APARAJITA SARANGI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the schemes for digitization of MSMEs to boost business potential and to \ncompete with other e\n-\ncommerce large business have positively benefitted MSMEs;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details for the reported number of MSMEs that have b\neen digitized through \nthe existing schemes for digitization of MSMEs\n;\n \n(c) \n \nthe data that indicates status of revenue and profitability of MSMEs after introduction \nof schemes to digitize MSMEs;\n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government plans to introduce schemes that all\neviate digital security \nconcerns of MSMEs to incentivize more number of MSMEs to build an online presence and \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n(e) \n \nwhether the Government is promoting any unified technology to ease digitization of \nMSMEs and if so, the detai\nls thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) & (b): \n \nThe Government runs various programmers/schemes to boost business \npotential and to compete with other e\n-\ncommerce large business entities through various \ndigitalization initiatives for MSMEs such as Udyam portal, MSME Champions Portal, \nGovernment e\n-\nMarketpl\nace\n \n(GeM), \nTrade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS), \nmsmemart.com\n. In addition,\n \nMSME SAMBANDH for monitoring of procurement by Central \nPublic Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) from Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) and MSME \nSAMADHAAN portals for filling appl\nications regarding delayed payments.\n \nAs on date 1.24 \ncrore MSMEs are registered in Udyam Registration portal and further benefitted through \nvarious programmes. Procurement from Central Ministries / Departments / CPSEs made \npurchases about Rs. 95576 Cr. fro\nm MSEs under Public Procurement Policy (PPP) for MSEs \nand 21,360 \napplications filed by \nMSMEs \nhave been \ndisposed by MSEFC council.\n \n(c): \n \nThe information with respect to the \nstatus of revenue and profitability of MSMEs \nis \nnot centrally maintained\n \nby Ministry\n \nof MSME.\n \n(\nd\n)\n \n& (e): \n \nUnder the MSME Champions Schemes ‘Digital MSME’ component has been \nincluded for digital empowerment of MSMEs in the country.\n \nThe Ministry of MSME propose  \nto  support MSMEs through\n \n“Digital MSME” \na component of MSME Champions Scheme\n \nto \nbring in digitization and digitalization and to \nmake MSMEs digitally empowered and \nmotivate them to adopt digital tools, applications and technologies in their production & \nbusiness processes with a view to i\nmprove their competitiveness.\n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |233", "t": "Financial Assistance for Revival of MSMEs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *233\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 22.12.2022\n \n \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE FOR REVIVAL OF MSMES\n \n \n \nNo. *233 \n \n                 \nSHRI SHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.:\n \n                                       \n  \nSHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND:\n \n \nWill the Minister of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government is aware of the fact that millions of people employed in Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterpr\nises (MSMEs) in the country have lost their jobs due to various reasons like \ndemonetisation, cash crunch, import\n-\nexport crisis and escalating price rise of raw materials during the \nCovid pandemic;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Gove\nrnment to restore the jobs of people rendered \njobless and improve the status of MSMEs;\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government has provided any financial assistance for the revival of MSMEs in the \ncountry, particularly in Tamil Nadu and Pollachi constituency and if so, th\ne details thereof; and\n \n \n(d) the measures taken or proposed to be taken by the Government to protect the interests of both \nemployer and employees of MSME sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) \nto (d): \n \nA \nstatement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *233 FOR ANSWER ON 22.12.2022.\n \n \n(a) to\n \n(c):   The Government has taken a number of recent initiatives vide programmes, schemes and \ndeclarations for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) in the country, including Tamil \nNadu. Some of these include:  \n \n \ni\n.\n \nRs. 5 lakh crore Emergency Credit Line\n \nGuarantee Scheme (ECLGS) for business, including \nMSMEs.\n \nii\n.\n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through Self\n-\nReliant India Fund.\n \niii\n.\n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \niv\n.\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores.\n \nv\n.\n \n“Udyam Registration” for MS\nMEs, for Ease of Doing Business\n \nvi\n.\n \nLaunching of an online Portal “Champions” in June, 2020 to cover many aspects of e\n-\ngovernance including redressing grievances and handholding of MSMEs.\n \nvii\n.\n \nInclusion of Retail and Wholesale traders as MSMEs w.e.f. 02.07.2021.\n \nviii\n.\n \nNo\nn\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of MSMEs. \n \n \nAdditionally, MSMEs have also been supported by the ongoing financial assistance schemes of \nthis Ministry i.e. \n \n(i)  \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme\n \n(PMEGP) which is a major credit\n-\nlinked \nsubsidy programme aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment; and \n \n(ii) \nCredit Guarantee Scheme (CGS) to strengthen credit delivery system and to facilitate the flow of \ncredit to the Micro and Small Enterprise sector withou\nt the hassles of collateral and third party \nguarantee. \n \n \nFor the above schemes, constituency wise data are not maintained, however, as per the data \nfurnished by National Credit Guarantee Trustee Company Ltd. (NCGTC), which is the implementing \nagency of ECL\nGS, as on 30.11.2022 since its inception, a total \nnumber\n \nof \n8,72,849  \nguarantees have \nbeen issued with \nl\noan amount guaranteed for Rs.\n26,497.14 crore in the State of Tamil Nadu. At the \ncountry level for the same period, number\n \nof \n1,13,47,107 \nguarantees have been issued with \nl\noan \namount guaranteed for Rs.\n2,37,702.81 crore to MSMEs. \n \n \nAs per the data furnished by Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) under PMEGP, \nas on 30.11.2022 since 2020\n-\n21, a total 14,336 micro units have been assis\nted with Rs.396.02 crore \nmargin money disbursed \nin the state of Tamil Nadu. At the country level for the same period \n2,19,403\n \nmicro units have been assisted with Rs.6,507.36 crore margin money disbursed.\n \n \n \nAs per the data furnished by Credit Guarantee Fund\n \nTrust for Micro and Small Enterprises \n(CGTMSE) under CGS as on \n30.11.2022, since 2020\n-\n21, a total number 1,42,196  of guarantees have \nbeen issued amounting Rs.11,519 crore \nin the state of Tamil Nadu. Similarly, for the same period, at \nthe country level \nnu\nmber \n22,60,449\n  \nof guarantees have been issued amounting Rs.1,53,448 crore.\n \n \n(d):   In the Union Budget 2022 the Government has announced the integration of Ministry of \nMSME’s Udyam portal with Ministry of Labour & Employment’s e\n-\nShram and National Career \nService (NCS) portals and Ministry of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship’s Aatmanirbhar \nSkilled Employee Employer Mapping (ASEEM) portals. \n \n \nFurther, as\n \ninformed by Directorate General of employment, Ministry of Labour & \nEmployment, Aatmanirbhar Bharat R\nojgar Yojana (ABRY) has been launched in October, 2020 as \npart of Aatmanirbhar Bharat package 3.0 to incentivize employers for creation of new employment \nalong with social security benefits and restoration of"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |234", "t": "Cochin Smart City Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 234 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 22, 2022 \n \nCOCHIN SMART CITY PROJECT \n \nNO. 234.    SHRI HIBI EDEN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to include chi ld-friendly \n features in all Cochin Smart City projects; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the decis ions, project list, \nestimated funds and challenges in this regard; and \n \n(c)  whether resting places for children would be c onstructed on \nfootpaths of major roads under the project and if s o, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n(a) to (c) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PARTS (a) to (c) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *234 (14 TH POSITION) FOR ANSWER \nON 22.12.2022 REGARDING COCHIN SMART CITY PROJECT. \n \n(a) to (c): Government of India launched the Smart Cities Mission \n(SCM) on 25 June 2015.  100 Smart Cities, including  Kochi, have been \nselected through 4 rounds of competition from Janua ry 2016 to June \n2018. \n \nProjects of Kochi Smart City are being implemented by Cochin Smart \nMission Limited (CSML), a Special Purpose Vehicle ( SPV) created for \nthe purpose.  CSML aims to enhance core infrastruct ure of the city \nwith primary focus on promoting sustainable and inc lusive \ndevelopment in the densely populated areas of the c ity benefitting \nlarger populations including young children and their caregivers.  \nNurturing Neighborhoods Challenge is a 3-year old i nitiative of the \nMinistry of Housing & Urban Affairs (MoHUA) that ai ms to enable cities \nadopt an early childhood lens in designing neighbor hood-level \nimprovements that promote health and well-being of young children \nand their caregivers. A cohort of 25 cities was ini tially selected for \ncapacity building to implement actions under the Ch allenge of which \n10 cities viz. Bengaluru, Hubbali-Dharwad, Indore, Jabalpur, Kochi, \nKakinada, Kohima, Rourkela, Vadodara, and Warangal were finally \ndeclared winners. \nInspired by the Nurturing Neighborhoods Challenge, CSML is \nincorporating young children and caregiver-friendly  lens in a variety of \nits projects such as development of outdoor play sp aces in Early \nChildhood Development (ECD) facilities, enhancing e xisting parks and \npublic open spaces to suit the needs of young children and caregivers. \nCSML, along with the Kochi Municipal Corporation (K MC), have also \nsigned a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with \nWRI India  to help \nscale up young children and caregiver-friendly proj ects in Kochi. A \nNurturing Neighbourhoods Cell consisting of members  from CSML and \nKMC has been formed to formulate and implement such  projects. This \nCell reviews all ongoing initiatives, facilitates i nter-departmental co-\nordination, collection of relevant data, and ensure s regular evaluation \n\nand monitoring of those projects. Details of projec ts benefitting young \nchildren and caregivers which have either been comp leted or are \nongoing in Kochi Smart City are given below:- \n \n(₹ in Crore) \nS.No.  Project Project \nCost \nStatus of \nProject \n1. Development of Smart Roads in Ernakulam \nABD  \n61.53  \nCompleted  \n2. Open Space Corridor Linking from DH \nGround to Mangalavanam \n7.84 \n3. Development of Open Spaces, Playgrounds \nand Museum of Freedom Struggle in West \nKochi  \n4.23 \nTotal 73.60 \n4. Development of Parks & Open Spaces in \nErnakulam Area Package-4 \n4.84 \nWork in \nProgress \n \n5. Upgradation of Anganwadi’s in West Kochi \nunder SCM, Kochi  \n2.18 \n6. Place-making and Beautification of \nPoovath Road and Durbar Hall Ground in \nErnakulam ABD Area under SCM, Kochi \n1.40 \nTotal 8.42 \n \nKochi Smart City is creating robust operational mod els for such \nprojects and is trying to formulate city-level poli cies with needs of \nyoung children and caregivers at the core. It is al so working to \nenhance capacity of relevant stakeholders to deal with such issues.  \n \nAs part of ‘Development of Smart Roads in Ernakulam  & West Kochi \narea’, all footpaths are being made child-friendly by installation of \nmulti-heighted seating spaces, without obstructing movement of \npedestrians.  These benches made from 100% recycled  plastic have \nbeen installed on the pedestrian walkway, wherever feasible, along \nGandhi Square at Marine Drive. In addition, CSML is  installing child-\nfriendly street furniture across the city. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |235", "t": "Losses to Aviation Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 235\n(To be answered on the 22nd December 2022)\n \nLOSSES TO AVIATION SECTOR\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Aviation Industry is estimated to report a net loss of Rs.\n15,000 to Rs. 17,000 crore during the financial year 2022-2023 and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(b) the details of loss incurred by the industry during the last three years,\nyear-wise along with the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the Government has proposed any sops to make aviation industry\nprofitable and create more employment opportunities in the sector;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government in this\nregard; and\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) and (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n \n*****\n*235. SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU:\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (A) TO (E) OF THE LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION  NO 235 FOR 22.12.2022 REGARDING LOSSES TO\nAVIATION SECTOR\n \n(a) The actual figure of profit / losses to the aviation industry shall be known\nonly when the audited account are available at the end of the financial year\n2022-23.\n \n(b) The details of losses incurred by the industry during the last three years\nare: FY 2019-20 Rs.4770 crore FY 2020-21 Rs.12479 crore and FY 2021-22\nRs. 11658 crore. The losses to the industry were primarily because of\ndisruption during COVID -19 pandemic across the globe, currency\ndepreciation (USD/INR), high operating cost environment, especially due to\nincrease in ATF prices which constitute a major portion of the operational\ncost of Airline, increase in crude oil prices in international market, VAT,\nExcise duty and Ukraine - Russia War. Airlines were unable to pass full\nimpact of cost increases to the passenger. \n \n(c) to (e) The airlines and major airports are operated by the private sector\nand they evolve their own SOPs for cost minimisation and profitability.\nHowever, Government has taken various steps to facilitate the airlines.\nThe UDAN Scheme of the Government of India is a game-changer for the\naviation industry. The UDAN Scheme or Ude Desh Ka Aam Nagrik, is a\nregional connectivity scheme that seeks to make air travel accessible and\naffordable for the masses.  The enhanced fiscal support in the form of VGF\n(Viable Gap Funding), concession on fuel rates, landing/parking charges and\nthe infrastructural development of un-served airports have not only boosted\nthe operations of giant airline companies but has also lead to the participation\nof regional start-up airlines such as M/s Star Air and M/s IndiaOne Air, and\nM/s Flybig which are operating exceptionally well. Other measures taken by\nthe Government include:\n \nI) The reduction in Value Added Tax (VAT) on Aviation Turbine Fuel (ATF)\nwas taken up with the state Government / Union Territories   levying high\nVAT on ATF. As a result 16 states have reduce VAT on ATF in the range of\n1- 4% by  (i) Andaman & Nicobar Islands, (ii) Uttarkhand, (iii) Jammu &\nKashmir, (iv) Ladakh, (v) Himachal Pradesh, (vi) Tripura, (vii) Madhya\nPradesh, (viii) Haryana, (ix) Uttar Pradesh, (x) Dadra & Nagar Haveli and\nDaman & Diu, (xi) Arunachal Pradesh, (xii) Manipur, (xiii) Jharkhand and\n(xiv) Mizoram reduced VAT on ATF below 5% and (xv) Gujarat and (xvi)\nKarnataka have reduced VAT on ATF 5% and 18% respectively in their\nStates.\nII) Goods and Services Tax on (GST) rate has been reduced from 18% to 5 %\nfor domestic Maintenance, Repair and Overhaul (MRO) services.\nIII)  Airports Authority of India (AAI) and other Airport Developers have\ntargeted capital outlay of approximately Rs.   98,000 crore in airport sector in\nthe next five years for expansion and modification of existing terminals, new\n\nterminals and strengthening of runways, among other activities.\nIV)  Government has approved Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme\n(ECLGS) to aviation sector. Based on the industry demand, the scope of\nECLGS has been enhanced to provide credit support to these companies upto\n100% of their total credit outstanding (both fund based and non-fund based\noutstanding) as on reference dates, subject to a cap of Rs. 1500 crore per\nborrower, whichever is lower.\n \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |236", "t": "Safety of Bridges on National Highways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA�\nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS�\nLOK SABHA�\nSTARRED QUESTION NO *236�\nANSWERED ON 22nd DECEMBER, 2022�\n �\nSAFETY OF BRIDGES ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS�\n �\n*236.   SHRI BENNY BEHANAN�\n            SHRI MANISH TEWARI�\n �\nWill the Minister of Road Transport and Highways�\n��\n�������������������������������������������������\n��\nbe pleased to state:�\n �\n(a) whether the Government has implemented stringent measures to \nensure the maintenance and upkeep of old road infrastructure as the \nbridges on NHs in the country have a twenty-five per cent mortality rate \nand if so, the details thereof;�\n(b) whether  the Government proposes to introduce safeguards to \nprevent untrained/unskilled bidders from obtaining public sector \ncontracts and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;�\n(c) whether the Government proposes to devise a framework/policy  to \nevaluate the status and the age of every bridge in the country and if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;�\n(d) whether the Government proposes to introduce a monitoring \nmechanism to ensure structural integrity of road infrastructur e, \nespecially old bridges; and�\n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?�\nANSWER�\nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS�\n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI)�\n \n(a) to (e)    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.�\n         � �\n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *236 ANSWERED ON 22.12.2022 \nASKED BY SHRI BENNY BEHANAN  AND SHRI MANISH \nTEWARI REGARDING SAFETY OF BRIDGES ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS�\n �\n(a) Maintenance and development of National Highways (NHs) including \nbridges thereon is a continuous process. Accordingly, repair / \nrehabilitation / reconstruction of existing NHs including old/ \ndistressed  bridges thereon ar e taken up on annual basis. Repair / \nrehabilitation / reconstruction of old / distressed bridges are taken up \neither on standalone basis or as part of improvement/ upgradation of \nsections of NHs. The repair / rehabilitation treatment are decided based \non n ature and extent of different distresses, functional requirement, \npresent loading vis -à-vis loading for which a particular bridge \nwas designed, etc. \n�\n(b) The bidding documents for procurement of \ncontractor/concessionaire for NH projects under different mod es \nof  implementation (EPC/HAM/BOT) clearly specify the technical and \nfinancial qualification requirements of the bidders. Such qualification \nrequirements are decided based on cost and size of NH projects \nincluding specific project components like bridges / tunnels.  Further, to \nensure construction of roads as per standards and specifications laid \ndown in contract/concession agreement and to avoid project delays, \nMinistry vide circular dated 06.10.2021 and dated 04.01.2022 has issued \nSOP to debar/penalize/d eclare as Non -Performer the \ncontractor/concessionaire in NHs and other centrally sponsored road \nprojects.�\n �\n(c ) to ( e)  Year of construction/approximate age of a bridge is generally \nknown except some old bridges. Ministry follow a well -established \nsystem of inspection/maintenance protocol for periodical assessment \nof physical condition of bridges. Indian Roads Congress(IRC) has \npublished manual / detailed guidelines for inspection and condition \nsurvey of bridges. MoRT&H has also issued guidelines for this purpose. \nMoRT&H has collected inventory and condition data of all bridges on \nNHs through consultants between 2015 to 2019. Bridge inspection was \nprincipally carried out using Mobile Bridge Inspection Unit for close \naccess to different critical components of the bridges. Recently, \nMoRT&H has engaged a consultant for development of Bridge \nManagement System (BMS) for NHs, which will provide an online \nplatform for monitoring health of all bridges on NHs in the country.�\n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |237", "t": "Contribution of MSME Sector in Production and Export", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 237 \nANSWERED ON 08TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nBAN ON USE OF OLD VEHICLES \n  \n237.  SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n         SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \n         SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n   \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government proposes to impose ban on use of vehicles \nwhich are older than 15 years and also proposes to impose any tax/fine \non such vehicles in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the total number of vehicles in the country as on date, State and \nvehicle type-wise; \n(c) whether the quantum and levying of such tax/fine would be uniform \nthroughout the country, if so, the details thereof and if not, the details \nof rates prescribed therefor, State-wise; and \n(d) whether the Government has made any impact assessment of \nimposing tax or banning of older vehicles and if so, the details thereof \nalong with other measures  taken by the Government in this regard to \nprotect the interest of such vehicle owners? \n  \nANSWER \n THE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI)      \n\n  \n(a), (c) and (d) 1. The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has \nissued GSR Notification 845 (E) dated 24.11.2022, which provides for \nnon-renewal of Certificate of Registration of government vehicles after \nthe lapse of fifteen years.  \n  \n2. The Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in WP No. 13029/1985 (MC Mehta \nvs Union of India), vide o rder dated 29.10.2018, has directed the \nTransport Departments of NCR that all diesel vehicles more than 10 \nyears old and petrol vehicles more than 15 years old shall not ply in \nterms of order of NGT dated 07.04.2015.  \n  \n3. Further, Ministry of Road Transpo rt and Highways has formulated \nthe Vehicle Scrapping Policy based on incentives/ dis -incentives and \ncreating an ecosystem to phase out older, unfit polluting vehicles. In \norder to enforce provisions of the policy, rules have been issued/ \namended under the framework of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 and \nCentral Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989. The following notifications have \nbeen issued and uploaded on the Ministry's website: - \n  \n(i) GSR Notification 653 (E) dated 23.09.2021 provides the Motor \nVehicles (Registration and Functions of Vehicle Scrapping Facility) \nRules, 2021 for establishment of Registered Vehicle Scrapping Facility \n(RVSF). The notification has come into force with effect from 25th \nSeptember, 2021. \n  \n(ii) GSR Notification 652 (E) dated 23.09.2021 provides for recognition, \nregulation and control of Automated Testing Stations. The notification \nhas come into force with effect from 25th September, 2021. \n  \n(iii) GSR Notification 714 (E) dated 04.10.2021 provides for upward \nrevision of registration fee, fitness testing fee and fitness certification \n\nfee of vehicles. The notification has come into force with effect from \n1st April, 2022. \n  \n(iv) GSR Notification 720 (E) dated 05.10.2021 provides for concession \nin the motor vehicle tax for the vehicle r egistered against submission \nof \"Certificate of deposit\". The notification has come into force with \neffect from 1st April, 2022. \n  \n(v) GSR Notification 166(E) dated 28.02.2022 provides for the manner \nin which validity of fitness certificate and registratio n mark of the \nmotor vehicle shall be exhibited on the vehicles. \n  \n(vi) GSR Notification 272(E) dated 05.04.2022 provides for mandatory \nfitness of motor vehicles only through an Automated Testing Station, \nregistered in accordance with rule 175 of the Centra l Motor Vehicles \nRules 1989, as under –  \n  \n(i)      For Heavy Goods Vehicles/Heavy Passenger Motor Vehicles \nwith effect from 01st April 2023 onwards, and  \n(ii)     For Medium Goods Vehicles/Medium Passenger Motor \nVehicles and Light Motor Vehicles (Transpor t) with effect from \n01st June 2024 onwards. \n  \n(vii) GSR Notification 695(E) dated 13.09.2022 provides for \namendments in Motor Vehicles (Registration and Functions of Vehicle \nScrapping Facility) Rules, 2021 earlier published vide GSR 653(E) dated \n23.09.2021. The notification has come into force with effect from 13th \nSeptember, 2022. \n  \n(viii) GSR Notification 797(E) dated 31.10.2022 provides the Central \nMotor Vehicles (Seventeenth Amendment) Rules, 2022 for \namendments in the rules for recognition, regulation and control of \n\nautomated testing stations. The notification has come into force with \neffect from 31st October, 2022. \n  \n(b) The total number of vehicles in the country as on date, State and \nvehicle type-wise are provided in the Annexure. \n  \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 237 ANSWERED ON \n08th DECEMBER, 2022 ASKED BY SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA, SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND AND SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA \nREGARDING BAN ON USE OF OLD VEHICLES. \n  \nSr. \nNo. State Name \n \n2WIC       2WN        2WT        3WN        3WT        LMV    "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |238", "t": "Allocation of Funds under NRDWP", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *238 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.12.2022 \n \nAllocation of Funds under NRDWP \n*238. SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of funds allocated/ released/ utilized by Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Jharkhand, \nChhattisgarh, Assam and Karnataka under National Rural Drinking Water Programme \n(NRDWP) during the last three years, year-wise;  \n(b)  the number of projects completed under the scheme in the said States, particularly in Latur \ndistrict of Maharashtra, Sikar, Alwar, Karauli, Dholpur and Bharatpur districts of Rajasthan, \nLatehar and Chatra districts of Jharkhand, Bilaspur , Mungeli and Gaurela -Pendra-Marwahi \ndistricts of Chhattisgarh, Darrang district of Assam and Bellary district of Karnataka during \nthe said period;  \n(c)  the number of project proposals pending under the scheme in the said States particularly in \nthe above-mentioned districts; and  \n(d)  the fresh steps taken by the Government for approval and implementation of all the pending \nproject proposals under the scheme? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n  \n\n2 \n \nStatement referred in reply of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *238 to be answered on \n22.12.2022 \n(a) to (d) Since August, 2019, Government of India in partnership with States, is implementing \nJal Jeevan Mission (JJM) – Har Ghar  Jal after subsuming the then National Rural Drinking \nWater Programme (NRDWP) to provide tap water connection to every rural household by 2024. \nDetails of allocation of Central fund  allocated/ released/ utilized by Maharashtra, Rajasthan, \nJharkhand, Chhatti sgarh, Assam and Karnataka  under JJM, in 2019 -20, 2020-21, 2021-22 & \n2022-23 (as on 15.12.2022) are annexed. \nDrinking Water being a State subject, it is States, including Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Jharkhand, \nChhattisgarh, Assam and Karnataka , who plan, design, approve and implement drinking water \nsupply schemes. Government of India supplements the efforts of the States by providing \ntechnical and financial assistance. As such State/ UT and district -wise details of individual \nprojects/ schemes for rural water supply, including proposals and approval, are not maintained at \nthe Government of India level.  \nHowever, number of steps have been taken to plan and implement JJM in the whole country \nincluding Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Jharkhand, Chhattisgar h, Assam and Karnataka with speed, \ninter alia, which includes joint discussion and finalization of annual action plan (AAP) of States/ \nUTs, regular review of implementation, workshops/ conferences/ webinars for capacity building \nand knowledge sharing, fiel d visits by multi -disciplinary team to provide technical support, etc. \nA detailed Operational Guideline for the implementation of JJM; Margdarshika for Gram \nPanchayats & VWSCs to provide safe drinking water in rural households and Guidelines on a \nspecial campaign to provide piped water supply in anganwadi  centres, ashramshalas and schools \nhave been shared with States/ UTs, to facilitate planning and implementation of Jal Jeevan \nMission. For online monitoring, JJM –Integrated Management Information System (IM IS) and \nJJM–Dashboard has been put in place. Provision has also been made for transparent online \nfinancial management through Public Financial Management System (PFMS). \n***** \n  \n\n3 \n \nAnnex \nAnnex referred to in Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *238 to be answered on 22.12.2022 \nDetails of allocation of Central fund  allocated/ released/ utilized by Maharashtra, Rajasthan, \nJharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Assam and Karnataka  under JJM, in 2019 -20, 2020-21, 2021-22 & \n2022-23 (as on 15.12.2022) \n (Amount in Rs. Crore) \n   Maharashtra \n \nS. No. Year \nCentral share State \nExpenditure  Opening \nBalance \nFund \nallocated \nFund \nreleased \nAvailable \nfund Expenditure \n1. 2019-20 248.12 847.97 345.28 593.40 308.04 428.14 \n2. 2020-21 285.35 1,828.92 457.23 742.58 473.59 324.16 \n3. 2021-22 268.99 7,064.41 1,666.64 1,935.63 379.18 477.98 \n4. 2022-23 1,557.65 7,831.25 0.00 1,557.65 817.38 845.44 \n \n   Rajasthan \nS. No. Year \nCentral share \nState \nExpenditure  Opening \nBalance \nFund \nallocated \nFund \nreleased \nAvailable \nfund Expenditure \n1. 2019-20 313.67 1,301.71 1,301.71 1,615.38 620.31 698.54 \n2. 2020-21 995.07 2,522.03 630.51 1,625.58 762.04 789.05 \n3. 2021-22 863.53 10,180.50 2,345.08 3,208.61 1,920.23 1,664.02 \n4. 2022-23 1,288.46 13,328.60 2,749.65 4,038.11 2,553.88 1,375.12 \n \nJharkhand \nS. No. Year \nCentral share State \nExpenditure  Opening \nBalance \nFund \nallocated \nFund \nreleased \nAvailable \nfund Expenditure \n1. 2019-20 75.79 267.69 291.19 382.97 114.58 119.71 \n2. 2020-21 268.39 572.24 143.06 411.45 286.62 177.73 \n3. 2021-22 124.83 2,479.88 512.22 637.05 437.21 510.99 \n4. 2022-23 199.83 2,8"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |239", "t": "Contribution of MSME Sector in GDP", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT \nOF \nINDIA\n \nMINISTRY \nOF \nMICRO, SMALL \nAND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *239\n \nTO BE \nANSWERED \nON\n \n22.12.2022\n \n \nCONTRIBUTION OF MSME SECTOR IN GDP\n \n \n*239.         SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:\n \n                  \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH:\n \n \n \nWill \nthe \nMinister \nof \nMICRO, SMALL \nAND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES \nbe pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) sector contributes one\n-\nthird to the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) of the country and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether t\nhe Ministry has recently organised the Global MSME Summit to promote the \nvisibility of Indian MSMEs and encourage international market linkages and if so, the details \nthereof;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to double the contribution of MSME sector to the\n \neconomy by realising its full potential and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether there are many internal bottlenecks towards doubling the contribution to the \neconomy; and\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nif so, the steps taken by the Government to resolve these bottlenecks and the success\n \nachieved in this regard so far?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER\n \nOF \nMICRO, SMALL \nAND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI\n \nNARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) to (e): \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (e) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED \nQUESTION No. *239  FOR ANSWER ON 22.12.2022\n \n \n(a)\n:\n      \nAs per the latest information received from Ministry of Statistics & Programme \nImplementation, the Share of Gross Value Added (GVA) of MSME sector in All India GDP \nduring 2020\n-\n21 \nwas \n26.83% \n.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \n \n  \n  \n  \nConfederation of Indian Industry (CII), New Delhi has organised the 19\nth\n \nedition \nof the Global MSME Business Summit with a theme \"India@100 with Future Ready \nMSMEs\" on 30\nth\n \nNovember, 2022 at New Delhi, in which Ministry of MSME has provided \nlogo suppor\nt.\n \n \n(c) to (e)\n:\n    \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises implements various \nschemes for the growth and development of MSME sector in the country in areas of credit \nsupport, technological assistance, infrastructure development, skill developme\nnt and training \nand market assistance to MSMEs.  The schemes/programmes inter alia include Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Micro and Small Enterprises\n-\nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), Scheme of Fund for Regeneration of \nTra\nditional Industries (SFURTI), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and Small Enterprises \n(CGTMSE) and A Scheme for Promoting Innovation, Rural Industry & Entrepreneurship \n(ASPIRE).\n \n \nThe Government has taken a number of recent initiatives to support MSMEs in t\nhe \ncountry, which inter alia include;\n \n(\ni\n)\n \nRs. 5 lakh \nEmergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS)\n \nfor business, \nincluding MSMEs.\n \n(\nii\n)\n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through MSME Self\n-\nReliant India Fund.\n \n(\niii\n)\n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \n(\niv\n)\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores.\n \n(\nv\n)\n \n“Udyam Registration” for MSMEs, for Ease of Doing Business.\n \n(\nvi\n)\n \nLaunching of an online Portal “Champions” in June, 2020 to cover many aspects of e\n-\ngovernance including grievance redress and handholding of\n \nMSMEs.\n \n(\nvii\n)\n \nInclusion of Retail and Wholesale trades as MSMEs w.e.f. 02\nnd\n \nJuly, 2021.\n \n(\nviii\n)\n \nNon\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of \nMSMEs. \n \n \n      \nSince the launch of Udyam Portal on 01.07.2020, 1.27 crore MSMEs have registered\n \non the portal as on 18.12.2022.\n \n \n***\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |24", "t": "International Air Services from Coimbatore Airport", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 24\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nINTERNATIONAL AIR SERVICES FROM COIMBATORE AIRPORT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to increase the number of international\nair services from Coimbatore International Airport and if so, the details\nthereof; and\n \n(b) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) and (b) A statement is laid on the table of the house.\n \n*****\n*24. SHRI SHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) to (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Question\nNo. 24 regarding \"Increase of Flights\" to be answered on 08.12.2022.\n \n(a) and (b) Indian designated carriers are free to mount scheduled operations\nto/from any international airport in India including Coimbatore under the\nambit of bilateral Air Service Agreements concluded by India with foreign\ncountries.\n \nThe start or increase of international air services from any point in India is\npurely a commercial decision of the airlines on the basis of economic viability\nof the route and other associated factors. Any designated foreign airline can\noperate scheduled services to/from an airport in India if it is designated as a\npoint of call in the ASA.\n \nCoimbatore is already available as a point of call to the designated carriers of\nSingapore, Sri Lanka, UAE-Dubai, UAE-Sharjah, UK and USA. Currently,\ndue to significant imbalance in the number of points of call in favour of\nforeign carriers, the Government of India does not grant any non-metro\nairport as a new point of call to any foreign carrier.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |240", "t": "Urbanisation Programme", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.240 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 22, 2022 \nURBANIZATION PROGRAMME  \n \nNO. 240.  SHRI ANIL FIROJIYA:  \nWILL THE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS BE \nPLEASED TO STATE: - \n(a) whether the Government has started/undertaken the w orld’s \nlargest planned urbanization programme to achieve t he \nEconomic and Sustainable Development Goals; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the roles being played by the cities to achieve the said goals? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the table of the  House. \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT AS REFERRED TO IN PART (A) TO (C) IN REPLY TO LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 240* DUE FOR ANSWER ON 2 2 \nDECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING ‘URBANIZATION PROGRAMME’. \n \n(a) to (c): Urban Planning is a State subject. As p er the 12 \nth  schedule \nof the Constitution, urban planning is the function  of Urban Local \nBodies/ Urban Development Authorities subject to the transfer of the \nfunctions by the State Government. Government of In dia has only an \nadvisory role in the matter. Ministry of Housing an d Urban Affairs \n(MoHUA) provides schematic and programmatic support  for urban \ndevelopment in the country. \n In order to support State and cities to achieve su stainable \nplanned urban development, MoHUA has taken following initiatives: \ni. MoHUA provided schematic support through various na tional \nmissions. \na. Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformat ion \n(AMRUT) addressed the water, sewerage, storm water drain, \nurban transport, and parks in cities with total out lay of Rs. \n1,00,000 crores for 500 cities (485 after merger). 5,873 \nprojects worth ₹82,223 crore have been grounded.  \nb. AMRUT 2.0focuses on universal coverage of water sup ply in all \nstatutory towns and universal coverage of sewerage and \nseptage management in AMRUT citieswith total outlay  of \n₹2,77,000 crore.So far, 4,830 projects worth ₹93,381 crorehave \nbeen approved.  \nc. Swachh Bharat Mission (Urban) provides for basic \ninfrastructure for Sanitation, Municipal Solid Wast e (MSW) and \n\nUsed Water Management (UWM) for improving infrastru cture in \nUrban Local Bodies (ULBs). Under the mission, 62.79  lakh \nIndividual Household Latrines (IHHLs) and 6.27 lakh  \nCommunity Toilets/ Public Toilets (CT/ PT) seats ha ve been \nconstructed till 30.11.2022. For scientific management of MSW \nin urban India, the waste processing plants have be en \nestablished. Door to door collection of waste has b een \nachieved in 88,150 out of 91,041 wards (96.82%), so urce \nsegregation of waste in 80,385 wards (88.29%) and s cientific \nwaste processing achieved for 74% of the total wast e \n(1,51,745 Tonnes Per Day). \nd. Swachh Bharat Mission 2.0 (Urban) is working for ‘G arbage \nFree Cities’ with total outlay of ₹1,41,678 crore.  \ne. Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana- Urban (PMAY-U) has been  \nproviding Central Assistance to States/UTs for addr essing the \nhousing requirement. ₹2,03,427 crore has been sanct ioned as \nCentral Assistance, out of which, ₹1,27,584 crore h as been \nreleased. \nf. Smart Cities Mission’s objective is to promote citi es that \nprovide core infrastructure, clean and sustainable environment \nand give a decent quality of life. 100 Smart cities  have \nproposed to execute a total of 5,151 projects worth  ₹2,05,018 \ncrore. 2,794 projects worth ₹89,130 crore are under  \nimplementation and 4,932 projects worth ₹92,220 cro re have \nalready been completed. \ng. In order to reduce poverty and vulnerability of urb an poor \nhouseholds on a sustainable basis, “Deendayal Antyo daya \n\nYojana- National Urban Livelihood Mission (DAY-NULM )” is \noperating in all the statutory towns. Under the mis sion, more \nthan 13 lakh urban poor have been imparted skill tr aining to \nenhance their employability, out of which more than  6.78 lakh \nskill trained have been placed under self and/or wa ge \nemployment \nh. Urban transport development works for improving urb an \nmobility including metro and other projects are sup ported by \nthe Ministry through policy measures and fund suppo rt. At \npresent, about 824 kilometers of metro rail lines a re \noperational in the country and 1,010 kilometers of metro lines \nare under construction. \nii. Under AMRUT, ‘Formulation of GIS-based Master Plan’  is a 100% \ncentrally funded sub-scheme with a total outlay of ₹515 crores.  \n461 cities (each above 1 lakh population) are imple menting this \nsub-scheme. Ministry is providing technology and ca pacity-\nbuilding support to cities for preparation of maste r plans. 152 \ncities have approved final GIS-based master plans a nd 132 cities \nhave formulated draft plans. GIS database has been finalized for \n396 cities and it is at draft stage for another 53 cities. 2,606 \nofficials have been trained through 73 training pro grams.  Under \nAMRUT 2.0, a sub-scheme on the formulation of GIS-based master \npla"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |25", "t": "Work on National Highway-730C", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 25 \nANSWERED ON 8TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nWORK ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS-730C \n \n*25. SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR: \n                    \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the work of rehabilitation and upgradation of a two lane road \nwith paved shoulder configuration of Allahganj – Miranpur Katra \nsection on Bareilly -Farrukhabad-Madnapur-Katra-Jalalabad National \nHighway (NH) 730C is being undertaken;  \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has received requests from the public \nrepresentatives to investigate the alleged irregularities in the \nconstruction works and quality assessment of the building materials \nused in the construction works of the said NH by the Central Technical \nCommittee; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the action taken or likely to be taken by the government in this regard? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n  \n(a) to (e)   A STATEMENT is laid on the Table of the House. \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.25 ANSWERED ON 8TH DECEMBER, 2022 ASKED BY \nSHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR, REGARDING “WORK ON NATIONAL HIGHWAYS-\n730C” \n \n(a) and (b) Yes Sir, The work of rehabilitation and upgradation to 2-lane with \npaved shoulders of Allahganj – Miranpur Katra section (km 52.770 to km \n114.000) of NH-730C in the State of Uttar Pradesh  has been taken up under \nGreen National Highways Corridor Project (GNHCP) with loan assistance of \nWorld Bank on EPC mode. The work has been awarded by Ministry at a  cost \nof Rs. 195.07 crore and the appointed date for the project has been fixed as \n08/07/2022.  Overall physical progress is about 20%. \n(c)  to (e)  Yes Sir,   the Government has received requests from the public \nrepresentatives regarding quality issues.  A   central independent expert has \nbeen appointed to investigate the alleged irregularities in the construction \nworks and quality assessment of the construction materials in the \nconstruction works. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |26", "t": "LPG Connections under PMUY", "head": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nLPG Connections under PMUY \n \n†*26.  DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:  \nSHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of LPG connections provided under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \nsince the inception of the scheme till November, 2022, State/UT-wise, especially in the Jhalawar \nand Baran districts of Rajasthan, classified as active and inactive;  \n(b) the number of refillings done under Ujjwala Yojana during the last two years including in \nJhalawar and Baran districts of Rajasthan;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to run any campaign to increase this number and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n(d) the details of refillings done under the said scheme during the last one year in Madhya \nPradesh and Rajasthan categorized as one refill, two refills, three refills and four or more refills? \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000) \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS (SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d)   A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 BY DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN AND SHRI DUSHYANT \nSINGH TO BE ANSWERED ON 8 th DECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING ‘LPG \nCONNECTIONS UNDER PMUY’.  \n \n(a)State/UT-wise details of LPG connections released under Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana(PMUY)  \nsince inception of the scheme till November, 2022 are at Annexure. Details of LPG connections \nreleased under PMUY since inception of the scheme till November, 2022 in the districts of Jhalawar \nand Baran of Rajasthan are as under :-  \nDistrict Number of LPG connections released \nunder PMUY (in lakh) \nJhalawar 2.03 \nBaran 1.60 \n \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \nAll PMUY connections are in active status \n(b)   Details of number of refills consumed by PMUY beneficiaries in 2020-21 and 2021-22 across \nthe country  including Jhalawar and Baran  districts of Rajasthan are as under :-  \nDistrict Number of  refills (in terms of 14.2 kg refills)  (in lakh) \n2020-21 2021-22 \nJhalawar 9.37 8.98 \nBaran 5.96 5.04 \nRajasthan State 277.52 267.27 \nAll India 3511.47 3126.23 \n \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n \n(c)  To improve LPG usage amongst PMUY beneficiaries, Government and Oil Marketing \nCompanies (OMCs) have organized LPG panchayats, social media campaigns, public events for the \ndistribution of connections and public awareness campaigns etc. across the country. Conducting LPG \nPanchayats across the country is an ongoing process and so far 381 and 285 LPG Panchayats have \nbeen conducted in Baran and Jalawar districts of Rajasthan respectively. \nFurther, Government has taken several other steps to encourage consumption of LPG by PMUY \nbeneficiaries, which include subsidy of ₹ 200/- per 14.2 Kg refill upto 12 refills/year for PMUY \nbeneficiaries for year 2022-23, option of 5 Kg Double Bottle Connection (DBC), swap option from \n14.2 Kg to 5 Kg, upto 3 free refills to PMUY beneficiaries under Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan \nPackage from April to December 2020 etc. \n(d)   As on 31.03.2021, there were 71.43 lakh  and 63.59 lakh PMUY beneficiaries in the States of \nMadhya Pradesh and Rajasthan respectively. Details of number of  PMUY customers who have taken \none refill, two refills, three refills, four or more refills during the last year i.e. 2021-22 in the States of \nMadhya Pradesh and Rajasthan are as under :-  \nState one refill \n(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nTwo refills \n(including \ninstallation refill (in \nlakh) \nthree refills \n(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nFour or more \nrefills(including \ninstallation refill \n(in lakh) \nMadhya Pradesh 18.85 15.74 11.54 22.84 \nRajasthan 7.64 10.68 11.49 33.02 \nSource : Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n------- \n\nAnnexure  \nAnnexure referred in part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 26  to be answered on \n08.12.2022 asked by Dr. Dhal Singh Bisen and Shri  Dushyant Singh regarding \"  LPG \nConnections under PMUY” \nState/UT \nTotal number of LPG connections released \nunder  PMUY as on 30.11.2022 \nAndaman and Nicobar Islands 13,447 \nAndhra Pradesh 5,11,579 \nArunachal Pradesh 49,118 \nAssam 43,79,144 \nBihar 1,07,10,910 \nChandigarh 562 \nChhattisgarh 34,90,375 \nDadar & Nagar Havelly and Daman \n& Diu 15,016 \nDelhi 1,41,042 \nGoa 1,147 \nGujarat 38,40,339 \nHaryana 7,66,461 \nHimachal Pradesh 1,40,672 \nUT of Jammu and Kashmir 12,44,016 \nJharkhand 36,42,058 \nKarnataka 37,48,565 \nKerala 3,41,040 \nUT of Ladakh 11,091 \nLakshadweep 310 \nMadhya Pradesh 82,20,240 \nMaharashtra 48,76,309 \nManipur 2,00,398 \nMeghalaya 2,09,800 \nMizoram 32,734 \nNagaland 90,464 \nOdisha 53,19,513 \nPuducherry 14,821 \nPunjab 12,83,863 \nRajasthan 69,24,719 \nSikkim 13,667 \nTamil Nadu 36,98,293 \nTelangana 11,52,103 \nTripura 2,81,825 \nUttar Pradesh 1,74,88,703 \nUttarakhand 4,97,374 \nWest Bengal 1,23,45,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |27", "t": "New Airports under RCS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION \nLok Sabha \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 27 \n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  8th December 2022 )  \nNEW AIRPORTS UNDER RCS \n27.    SHRI DILIP SAIKIA  \n                       \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION \nbe pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government proposes to start twenty airports, heliports \nand water aerodromes under the  Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS) \nby the year 2024-25; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Assam; \n(c) the names of the ports i dentified for the purpose and the present \nstatus thereof; \n(d) the time by which the said airports are likely to be set up; \n(e) whether air travel from Mangaldoi is proposed to be started under \nthe scheme and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) the time by which the flights are likely to be started therefrom? \nANSWER \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION      (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia) \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 27 \nREGARDING \"NEW AIRPORTS UNDER RCS\" TO BE ANSWERED ON \n08.12.2022.; ; (a) to (d): Details of the 68 identified aerodromes for \nrevival/upgradation under UDAN by 2024, are at Annexure.   The \nairports/heliports/water aerodromes identified for revival/upgradation \nunder UDAN in the State of Assam are Guwahati River Front (WA), \nNagaon (H), Rupsi and Umrangso Reservoir (WA).  ; (e) & (f): Mangaldoi \nairstrip is not available in the UDAN scheme document. However, if the \n\nState Government of Assam requests to include the same under UDAN, \nit shall be considered as per the provisions of scheme document. ; ; The \nSelected Airline Operators (SAOs) under UDAN are obligated to \ncommence RCS flight operations on a RCS route within a period of six \nmonths from the issuance of Letter of Award or within two months from \nthe readiness of airport, whichever is later, unless any extension is \ngranted by the Implementing Agency.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 27 for answer on 08.12.2022 \n \nList of 68 identified RCS Aerodrome, to operationalise by 2024 \nS. No. Name of State Airport Name Airport/Heliport \n(H)/ Water \nAerodrome \n(WA) \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar Campbell Bay Airport \n2.  Andaman & Nicobar Car Nicobar Airport \n3.  Andaman & Nicobar Havelock (W) WA \n4.  Andaman & Nicobar Long Island (W) WA \n5.  Andaman & Nicobar Neil Island (W) WA \n6.  Andaman & Nicobar Shibpur Airport \n7.  Andhra Pradesh Parkasam Barrage (W) WA \n8.  Arunachal Pradesh Daparizo (H) Heliport \n9.  Arunachal Pradesh Itanagar (H) Heliport \n10.  Arunachal Pradesh Tuting (H) Heliport \n11.  Arunachal Pradesh Walong (H) Heliport \n12.  Arunachal Pradesh Yinghoing (H) Heliport \n13.  Arunachal Pradesh Ziro (H) Heliport \n14.  Assam Guwahati River Front (W) WA \n15.  Assam Nagaon (H) Heliport \n16.  Assam Umrangso Reservoir (W) WA \n17.  Chhattisgarh Ambikapur Airport \n18.  Daman&Diu Daman Airport \n19.  Haryana Ambala Airport \n20.  Himachal Pradesh Baddi (H) Heliport \n21.  Himachal Pradesh Manali (H) Heliport \n22.  Himachal Pradesh Shimla(H) Heliport \n23.  Jharkhand Bokaro Airport \n24.  Jharkhand Dumka Airport \n25.  Jharkhand Hazaribagh Airport \n26.  Jharkhand Jamshedpur Airport \n27.  Ladakh Kargil Airport \n\n28.  Ladakh Thoise Airport \n29.  Lakshadweep Kavaratti WA \n30.  Lakshadweep Minicoy WA \n31.  Maharashtra Amravati Airport \n32.  Maharashtra Ratnagiri Airport \n33.  Maharashtra Solapur Airport \n34.  Manipur Jiribam (H) Heliport \n35.  Manipur Moreh (H) Heliport \n36.  Manipur Parbung (H) Heliport \n37.  Manipur Tamenglong (H) Heliport \n38.  Manipur Thanlon (H) Heliport \n39.  Odisha Rourkela Airport \n40.  Odisha Utkela Airport \n41.  Rajasthan Kota Airport \n42.  Rajasthan Uterlai Airport \n43.  Tamil Nadu Neyveli Airport \n44.  Tamil Nadu Ramnad Airport \n45.  Tamil Nadu Tanjore Airport \n46.  Tamil Nadu Vellore Airport \n47.  Telangana NagarjunaSagar (W) WA \n48.  Uttar Pradesh Aligarh Airport \n49.  Uttar Pradesh Ayodhya (Faizabad) Airport \n50.  Uttar Pradesh Azamgarh Airport \n51.  Uttar Pradesh Chitrakoot Airport \n52.  Uttar Pradesh Fursatganj Airport \n53.  Uttar Pradesh Ghazipur Airport \n54.  Uttar Pradesh Jhansi Airport \n55.  Uttar Pradesh Meerut  Airport \n56.  Uttar Pradesh Moradabad Airport \n57.  Uttar Pradesh Muirpur (Korba) Airport \n58.  Uttar Pradesh Sarsawa (Saharanpur) Airport \n59.  Uttar Pradesh Shravasti Airport \n60.  Uttarakhand Dharchula (H) Heliport \n\n61.  Uttarakhand Joshimath (H) Heliport \n62.  Uttarakhand Mussoorie (H) Heliport \n63.  Uttarakhand Nainital (H) Heliport \n64.  Uttarakhand Haridwar (H) Heliport \n65.  Uttarakhand Ramnagar (H) Heliport \n66.  West Bengal Burnpur Airport \n67.  West Bengal Cooch Behar Airport \n68.  West Bengal Kalaikunda Airport \n \nThe above list of 68 RCS Aerodromes is as on 30.11.2022. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |28", "t": "New Flights to Lakshadweep", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 28\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nNEW FLIGHTS TO LAKSHADWEEP\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has received any proposal from other airline\ncompanies except Air India to start new flights to Lakshadweep;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) whether the Government  has taken further initiatives to expand air\ntravels to promote tourism in Lakshadweep and if so,  the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****\n*28. SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.\n28 REGARDING \"NEW FLIGHTS TO LAKSHADWEEP\" TO BE\nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022.\n \n(a) to (c): As per ongoing Winter Schedule-2022, M/s Alliance Air is operating\n7 flights per week on Kochi-Agatti route. With the repeal of the Air\nCorporation Act,1953, in March 1994, the Indian domestic aviation market\nwas deregulated. Consequently, airlines are free to induct capacity with any\naircraft type for selecting whatever markets and networks they wish to service\nand operate across the country. Thus, it is up to the airlines to provide air\nservices to specific places depending upon the traffic demand and their\ncommercial viability, in compliance with the relevant guidelines in this\nmatter.\n \nMinistry of Civil Aviation has launched Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS)\n- UDAN (Ude Deshka Aam Nagrik) on 21-10-2016 to stimulate regional air\nconnectivity and making air travel affordable to the masses which includes\nairports/heliports/water aerodromes.In order to expand air travels to promote\ntourism in Lakshadweep, Ministry of Civil Aviation introduced a new mode of\ntransportation i.e. seaplane operations from water aerodromes under the\nUDAN scheme. \n \nTill the completion of four rounds of bidding, twenty four (24) routes\ninvolving eight (8) water aerodromes have been identified/awarded in\nLakshadweep (UT) for the seaplane operation which are Kavaratti, Minicoy,\nAgatti, Kalpeni, Bitra, Kadmat, Kiltan, & Bangaram. \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |29", "t": "Greenfield Airport", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 29\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nGREENFIELD AIRPORT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has recently inaugurated country’s first\nGreenfield ‘Donyi Polo’ airport in Arunachal Pradesh;\n(b) if so, the details and the salient features thereof;\n(c) the details of the proposals received for setting up of Greenfield Airports\nfrom the State Governments along with the NOC given by Union Government\nfor the purpose during each of the last three years and the current year,\nState/UT-wise;\n(d) the details of the criteria fixed by the Government for setting up of\nGreenfield Airports in the country; and\n(e) the details of the funds allocated and utilized for setting up of Greenfield\nAirports during the said period?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n (a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n*****\n*29. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\nSHRI RAVI KISHAN\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement in reply to parts (a) to (e) of the Lok Sabha Starred (*) Question\nNo.29 for answer on 08.12.2022 regarding \"Greenfield Airport \"\n \n(a) & (b): Donyi Polo Airport, Itanagar,  the first Greenfield Airport of\nArunachal Pradesh has been inaugurated on 19.11.2022. The Donyi Polo\nairport has a Terminal Building capable of handling 300 passengers during\npeak hours. With a 2300 meter long runway, the airport is suitable for the\noperation of B-737/A-320 type of aircraft. Along with a suitable apron for\nparking of three B-737/A-320 type of aircraft, the airport also has  a special\napron to accommodate four Mi-17 helicopters. The airport is operational\nsince 28.11.2022.\n \n(c) & (d):  Development of a Greenfield airport is governed under the\nGreenfield Airports (GFA) Policy, 2008. As per the Policy, a State\nGovernment or an Airport Developer, willing to establish an airport, is\nrequired to send a proposal to the Ministry of Civil Aviation (MoCA) for 2-\nstage approval i.e. 'Site-Clearance' followed by 'In-Principle' approval. Such\nproposals are considered by MoCA as per the procedure stipulated in the\nGFA Policy.\n \nAs of now, Government of India (GoI) has accorded 'In-Principle' approval\nfor setting up of 21 Greenfield Airports across the country. Out of these, 9\nGreenfield airports viz. Durgapur, Shirdi, Kannur, Pakyong, Kalaburagi,\nOrvakal (Kurnool), Sindhudurg, Kushinagar and Donyi Polo, Itanagar have\nbeen operationalized.\n \nGovernment of India has also granted the first stage clearance i.e. site\nclearance for construction of three Greenfield airports namely Alwar in\nRajasthan, Singrauli in Madhya Pradesh and Mandi in Himachal Pradesh.\nDuring the last  three years, Mandi Airport project has been granted 'Site\nClearance' in 2022.\n \n(e): The project cost of nine (09) operationalized airports is: Durgapur-Rs.670\ncrore, Shirdi-Rs.320 crore, Pakyong- Rs. 553.53 core, Kannur- Rs.2342 crore,\nKalaburagi- Rs.175.57 crore, Orvakal  (Kurnool)- Rs.187 crore, Sindhudurg-\nRs.520 crore, Kushinagar-Rs.448 crore and Donyi Polo, Itanagar-Rs.646\ncrore.\n \nAs per Greenfield Airports Policy, the responsibility of implementation of\nairport projects including funding of the projects rests with the concerned\nairport developer including the respective State Government (in case the State\nGovernment is the project proponent). \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |3", "t": "Extension/Implementation of PMGKAY", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 03 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nEXPORT OF BROKEN RICE \n \n03.  SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री)be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)   whether it is not true that there has been a huge  demand for broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo-political reasons; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; \n(c)   whether it is also not true that export of broken  rice has increased by more than 40 \ntimes in the last four years and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether 21.03 lakh tonnes of broken rice was  exported between April -August, 2022; \nand \n(e)   if so, the reasons for banning broken rice exports from the country? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a) & (b):  Yes Sir. There has been sudden increase in demand for Indian broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo -political reasons. India’s exports of broken rice have increased \nfrom 17.86 Lakh MT in 2021 -22(April- September) to 23.82 Lakh MT in 2022 -23 (April -\nSeptember) registering a growth of 33.37%.  \n \n(c):  No Sir. The export of broken rice from India has increased about three time s in the last \nfour years i.e from 12.21 Lakh MT in 2018 -19 to 38.90 Lakh MT in 2021 -22 in volume and in \nvalue terms i.e USD 369.58 Million in 2018 -19 to USD 1132.94 Million in 2021 -22. However, \n\n2 \n \nexport of broken rice witnessed a decline in 2019 -20 due to th e pandemic and domestic policies \nof major importing countries in Africa. The details are given in the Table below: - \n \nYear Quantity in MT Value in Million USD \n2018-19 12,21,617 369.58 \n2019-20 2,70,338 82.48 \n2020-21 20,64,562 595.69 \n2021-22 38,90,866 1132.94 \n2021-22 \n(April-September) \n17,86,435 516.89 \n2022-23 * \n(April-September) \n23,81,657 764.81 \nSource: DGCIS, Kolkata.   \n* Figures for 2022-23 are provisional and subject to change. \n \n(d): A total of 21.64 Lakh MT of broken rice was exported from India during the period \nbetween April-August in 2022. \n \n(e):   Broken rice is majorly used for ethanol production and as poultry & cattle feed.  As \nevident from Table at Para -(c) above, there has been sudden rise in demand for Indian broken \nrice due to geo -political situation around. Further, as per the first Advance Estimates for 2022 -\n23,released by Directorate of Economics and Statistics, Department of Agriculture and Farmers’ \nWelfare, the production of rice is estimated to be lower than the target fixed for the  current year. \nTherefore, in order to ensure adequate availability of broken rice in domestic market,the \nGovernment has prohibited export of broken rice w.e.f 9thSeptember, 2022.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |30", "t": "DPR of Inter-linking of Rivers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *30 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \nDPR OF INTER-LINKING OF RIVERS \n*30  SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has prepared draft DPR f or five river linking projects in the country and \nmentioned in the recent budget 2022-23 that once th e consensus is reached among the beneficiary States , \nthe Government would provide for its implementation and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether any States have specifically objected o r have expressed any reservations which are causing  \ndelay in implementation of these river linking projects and if so, the details thereof including the nature of \nthe objections; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to include in t hese five draft DPR the other pending projects on t he \nrespective rivers which are going to be linked toge ther including river-basin area relating to ground water \nrecharge/conservation/irrigation project(s) especially for Par-Tapi-Narmada river linking project; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI    \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*30 TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 REGARDING “DPR OF INTER-LINKING OF \nRIVERS”.  \n(a) & (b)  The five river linking projects for which draft Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) were completed, \nas mentioned in the speech of Union Minister of Fin ance on 01.02.2022 for Union Budget for financial \nyear 2022-23, include Damanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tap i-Narmada link projects apart from the three \ncomponents of the Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Gr and Anicut) link project, viz; Godavari \n(Inchampalli)-Krishna (Nagarjunasagar), Krishna (Na garjunasagar)-Pennar (Somasila) and Pennar \n(Somasila)-Cauvery (Grand Anicut) links.These links  projects are among the 30 link projects identified  \nunder the National Perspective Plan (NPP) prepared by the Government of India. \nDamanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tapi-Narmada link project s are twin link projects concerning the States of \nMaharashtra and Gujarat.  DPR of Damanganga-Pinjal link was circulated to the States in 2014 and \naccorded techno-economic clearance in 2016 while re vised DPR  of Par-Tapi-Narmada link was \ncirculated to the States in 2017. Various consultat ion meetings have been held by the Government of \nIndia.  \nAs envisaged under the NPP, Godavari is to be augme nted by transfer of surplus waters from Mahanadi \nand Brahmaputra basins, for further diversion of wa ters to Krishna, Pennar and Cauvery basins. Pending  \nconsensus on Mahanadi-Godavari and Godavari (Inchampalli)-Krishna link projects, alternative studies to \ndivert surplus waters available in Godavari basin and unutilized waters in Indravati sub-basin of Godavari \nbasin through Godavari-Cauvery link project were ca rried out by National Water Development Agency \n(NWDA). This link project traverses through Godavar i, Krishna, Pennar, Palar and Cauvery basins and \nlies in the States of Telangana, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.  \nThe DPRs of Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Grand An icut) link project were prepared in consultation \nwith the party States and circulated to them in Apr il, 2021. The party States have raised concerns rel ated \nto surplus waters availability in Godavari basin an d quantum of allocation of waters to the States. \nConsultation meetings with party States/Union Terri tory (UT) have been held and as decided during the \nmeeting held on 18.02.2022, a Technical Feasibility  Report (TFR) has also been prepared by NWDA to \nrecast the proposal limiting the transfer of waters  from 7,000 Million Cubic Metres (MCM) to about \n4,000 MCM from Godavari basin, in the interim phase , along with combining the proposal for \nsupplementation in Krishna basin through Bedti-Vard a link. The consultation meeting was last held on \n18.10.2022 and efforts made for the party States to  arrive at a consensus for implementation of the li nk \nproject. \nThe implementation of the link projects depends upon the party States to arrive at a consensus. \n  \n(c) & (d)  No such proposal is under consideration. \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |31", "t": "Startup Companies", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *31\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n08.12.2022\n \n \nSTARTUP COMPANIES\n \n \n*31.        \nSHRI S. VENKATESAN:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it has come to the notice of the Government that 90 to 95 per \ncent of startup companies have failed;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the percentage of failures among Micro, Small and M\nedium \nEnterprises;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the statistics with the Government corroborate with the findings \nof these studies on failure of startups; and\n \n(d) \n \nif not, the failure rate as per the details available with the Government? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL\n \nAND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) \nto (d): \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *31 FOR 08.12.2022 REGARDING STARTUP \nCOMPANIES\n \n \n \n(a) \nto (d): \n \nAs per Allocation of Business Rules, 1961, matters relating to \nstartups come under the purview of the Department for Promotion of Industry \nand Internal Trade (DPIIT), Ministry of Commerce and Industry. As per the \ninformation provided by DPIIT, the Governme\nnt of India has taken a \ncomprehensive approach under the Startup initiative which has resulted in the \ncountry's Startup ecosystem maturing into the third largest startup ecosystem\n. \nT\nhe Government has \nalready \nrecognized \nmore than 84\n,000 Startups that are \nsp\nread over 640 districts with 45% of them\n \nbeing from\n \ntier 2 and 3 cities. The \nrecognized Startups have reported creation of over \n8.4\n \nlakh jobs. Further, more \nthan 100 India Startups have achieved unicorn status (entity valued over USD l \nbillion) as \non\n \ndate.\n \n \n \nWhereas, r\negular businesses \nare often\n \nmeasured by success or failure in a \nspecific number of years of operation\n,\n \nStartups and scale ups are more \naccurately measured by failure or success in a particular phase\n \nmaking it \ndifficult to put together a statis\ntic covering all types of new businesses and their \nfailure rate with any level of accuracy.\n \n \n \n \nThe information with respect to the failure of Startups is not centrally \nmaintained by the Government. \n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |32", "t": "Women-led Enterprises", "head": "1 \n \n������������������� �\n������������������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�\n��������� �\n����������������������� ���\n������������������ �������������������� �\n�\n�\n���� �\n����� ���������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n����������������������� �\n����������������� ���������� �\n��������������������� �\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n������������������� �\n����������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������������������\n����������\n����� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������\n���� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������