 PROJECTS IN \nNORTH EASTERN STATES” \n  \n(a) & (b) Yes Sir, the Government has approved Nine  National Highway \n(NH) projects of 56 km. for an amount of Rs. 258 Cr in North Eastern \nStates including Manipur during the current year. All projects are at \nbidding stage. The details are as per Annexure-I. \n  \n  \n\n Annexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) & (b) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 38 FOR ANSWER ON 08.12.2022 \nASKED BY SHRI LORHO S. PFOZE REGARDING NH PROJECTS IN \nNORTH EASTER STATES.  \n  \nDetails of National Highway (NH) projects approved  in North Eastern \nStates including Manipur during the current Financial year \n \nSr. \nno Name of Work NH Length(In \nKm) \nCost  \n(In Crores) \nARUNACHAL PRADESH       \n1 \nStrengthening and road safety of \nShantipur – Meka road (NH -115) \nsection from km 0.000 to km \n19.500 of NH-115. \n115 19.50 71.09 \n  Total   19.50 71.09 \nASSAM       \n2 \nProviding Slope Protection Works \nto the embankment of existing NH-\n515 from Km 11.443 to Km 11.590 \nwith Geobags for protection \nagainst perennial submergence of \nNH-515 by Jalakiasuti Channel \nnear Demow in Dhemaji District. \n515  0 4.24 \n3 \nStrengthening of existing NH -\n129(New)/NH-39(old) from Phulbari \nKm 96/00(near Bokajan) to Gautam \nbasti Km 103/00(near Lahorijan) \nalong with RCC drain near \nKhatkhati. \n129 7.00 16.46 \n4 \nWidening of existing intermediate \nlane to 2L+PS from 224th Km of NH \n44 to 1st Km of NH 151(Missing \nlink). \n151 4.00 25.95 \n\nSr. \nno Name of Work NH Length(In \nKm) \nCost  \n(In Crores) \n5 \nRepair and rehabilitation of ROB at \n20.50Km on NH -37(New)/NH-\n53(Old)(Silchar-Jiribam Section). \n37  0 0.99 \n6 \nStrengthening from Km 879/000 to \nkm 888/000 (Total length = \n9.095km) on NH -31.  \n31 9.09 11.57 \n  Total   20.09 59.21 \nMANIPUR       \n  Total   0.00 0.00 \nMEGHALAYA       \n7 \nRestoration and protection works \nwith safety works by providing:   1) \nConstruction of Retaining wall& \nBreast wall, pucca side drains, \ncrash barriers,road signages etec \nfrom 23rd km to 63rd km   and 2) \nConstruction of Breast wall at \nlocation at vulnerable lo cation at \nTura town portion from 96th km to \n101st km and improvement of \njunction at 97th km of NH -51 (New \nNH-217) \n217 0.00 9.23 \n  Total   0.00 9.23 \nMIZORAM       \n8 \n2-Laning of Bairabi-Kolasib section \nfrom Jn. km 89.00 to km 105.00 \ncorresponding to Km 58/00 to Km \n42/00 on NH -154.  \n6 16.00 86.81 \n  Total   16.00 86.81 \nNAGALAND       \n  Total   0.00 0.00 \nSIKKIM       \n9 Replacement of existing Steel \nBridge at km 6+780 , km 23+660 ,  310A 0.00 31.68 \n\nSr. \nno Name of Work NH Length(In \nKm) \nCost  \n(In Crores) \nkm 26+935 with pre -fabricated \nmodular steel bridges and \nConstruction of bailey suspension \nbridge at km 52+500 on NH -310A \n(Tashi view point to Mangan Road). \n  Total   0.00 31.68 \nTRIPURA     \n  Total   0.00 0.00 \n  \nTOTAL FOR NORTH EASTERN \nREGION   56 258  \n  \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |39", "t": "Nai Roshni Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 39 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2022 \n \nEXTENSION OF RAILWAY LINE FROM CHANDKHIRA TO KANHMUN \n \n39. SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH:  \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that there is an urgent public \ndemand for extension of railway line from Chandkhira to \nKanhmun (Mizoram) via Lowairpoa and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is also aware that the people living in \nthe remote area of his Constituency Karimganj and Hailakandi \ndistrict bordering Mizoram are totally deprived from getting the \ncheapest and safest transportation facilities of train and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government is aware that the survey had already \nbeen conducted by the department concerned earlier for the \nsaid railway line but no action has been taken so far and if so, \nthe details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) whether the Government has fixed any time limit regarding \nextension of this railway line and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 39 BY SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2022 REGARDING EXTENSION \nOF RAILWAY LINE FROM CHANDKHIRA TO KANHMUN \n \n(a) to (d):  Chand Khira Bagan  is already connected with Karimganj \nvia Patharkandi by Railway Network. Further, a survey for \nconstruction of new Broad Gauge (BG) line from Patharkandi to \nKanhmun via Loirpowa (43.15 km) has been completed in 2015. As \nper survey report, the project could  not be taken forward due to low \ntraffic projections. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |4", "t": "Extending 4G Facility to Puducherry", "head": " \n \n1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \n DEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 04  \nFOR ANSWER ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2022  \n  \nEXTENDING 4G FACILITY TO PUDUCHERRY \n \n*4.  SHRI VE. VAITHILINGAM:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether priority will be given in extending 4G facility to Puducherry and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n \n(b)  the time by which the 4G service is expected to be given to Puducherry? \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS & \n ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(a) & (b) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n2 \n \nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF LOK SABHA IN RESPECT OF \nPARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION  NO. 04 FOR 7th \nDECEMBER, 2022 REGARDING “EXTENDING 4G FACILITY TO \nPUDUCHERRY” \n \n  \n(a) & (b) 4G services have already been provided by Telecom Service Providers (TSPs) in \nUnion Territory of Puducherry as per following details. \n \n \nSl No. Telecom Service Provider Commencement of 4G Services \n1. Bharti Airtel Limited August, 2015 \n2. Reliance Jio Infocomm Limited September, 2016 \n3. Vodafone Idea Limited January, 2017 \n \n \n \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |40", "t": "Flying Training Institutes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.40 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2022 \n \nNEW CARGO TERMINALS \n \n  \n40. SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether any proposals have been received from the industry for \nnew cargo terminals after the launch of Gati Shakti Multi -Modal \nCargo Terminal; \n \n(b)  if so, the number of new cargo terminals proposed, applications \nand firm proposals received;  \n \n(c) the number of new players who are likely to be selected; \n \n(d) whether any guidelines have been framed in this regard and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n \n(e) the revenue that is expected to accrue to the Railways \nconsequent upon operationalisation of the new cargo terminals; and \n \n(f) the number of people who are likely to be given direct \nemployment? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (f):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRE D TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f ) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 40 BY SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 07.12.2022 REGARDING NEW CARGO \nTERMINALS \n \n(a):  Yes, Sir. \n \n(b) and (c):    Under the Gati Shakti Cargo Terminal (GCT) Policy , it \nhas been targeted to develop 100 GCTs  in three years i.e. 2022-23, \n2023-24 & 2024-25.  22 GCTs have already been commissioned.  125 \napplications for development of Cargo Terminals under the GCT \nPolicy have been receiv ed and 79 In Principle Approvals have been \ngranted. \n \n(d):  For GCTs to be developed on non -Railway land, the GCT \nOperators will identify the location and will construct the terminal \nafter obtaining necessary approval. For GCTs to be developed either \nfully or partially on Railway land, the land parcels will be identified by \nRailway, and the GCT Operator for construction and operation of \nTerminal will be selected through open tendering process. \n \n(e) and (f):  Data regarding any estimate of revenue and employment \nafter operationalisation of the GCTs is not maintained. \n****** \n \n  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |41", "t": "Setting up of New Medical Colleges", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.41 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07/12/2022 \n  \nCMDLD \n  \n41. SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n           \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps to ameliorate lung diseases collectively termed \nCoal Mine Dust Lung Disease (CMDLD) including Coal Workers’ Pneumoconiosis, silicosis, \nmixed dust pneumoconiosis, dust -related diffuse fibrosis and chronic obstr uctive pulmonary \ndisease; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nAnswer \n  \nMINISTER OF  PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) & (b) CIL and its subsidiaries have taken required steps to ameliorate coal mine dust lung \ndiseases (CMDLD) including Coal Workers' Pneumoconiosis (CWP). These are as under: \n1. Adequate measures are taken to prevent generation, propagation and control of air \nborne coal dust in the mines and its surroundings as per various provisions made \nunder relevant statutes. \n2. Appropriate site specific dust suppression systems are also adopted in mines to keep \nmine environment dust free. \n3. PME (Periodic Medical Examination) is done for all employees at regular interval at \nvarious PME centers of different subsidiaries of CIL. \n4. Some patients with respiratory problems are treated at hospitals of CIL and other \ntertiary level empaneled hospitals. \n5. Various camps are organized at regular interval at area level of subsidiaries to screen \nany Lungs disease in its early stage.  \n6. Preventive measures like distribution of anti-dust mask and other protective gears are \ndistributed among employees regularly. \n7. Provision of spirometry and chest x-ray imaging is available at various PME centers \nof the subsidiaries.  \n8. Time to time counseling and mental health sessions are organized for employees. \n9. Pneumoconiosis board is being conducted in a regular interval on the suspected cases \ntaking the help of specialist doctors (pulmonologists and radiologists) and ILO \nrecommended X-ray plates are referred. \n10. On confirmation of pneumoconiosis, the patients are offered alternative jobs and they \nare observed for disease progression in a regular interval. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |42", "t": "Passport Melas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.42\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022\nPASSPORT MELAS\n42. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to organize passport melas and passport\nseva camps  in Gujarat in order to speed  up  passport  issuance  and meet high or\nseasonal demand for passports; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise; \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(d) the details of funds allocated towards organizing the passport melas during the\nlast three years, State-wise including Gujarat?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER FOR EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n (DR. SUBRAHMANYAM JAISHANKAR)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n1\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE\nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 42 REGARDING \"PASSPORT\nMELAS” FOR ANSWER ON 09.12.2022.\n(a) to (c) The Ministry has been working continuously to speed up Passport\nissuance and meet high or seasonal demand for Passports by taking various\nsteps including organizing of Special Drives or Melas on weekends. \nStarting 3rd December 2022, the Ministry has decided to launch yet another\nspecial drive by opening Passport Seva Kendras (PSKs) and some Post Office\nPassport Seva Kendras (POPSKs) for next 4 Saturdays including in the state of\nGujarat. On 3 rd December 2022, the Melas were organised at various PSKs\nunder  the  jurisdictions  of  20  Regional  Passport  Offices  (RPOs)  including\nAhmadabad and Surat. Under these two RPOs in Gujarat, there are 5 PSKs and\n23 POPSKs.\n(d) There are no separate funds allocated towards organizing the Passport\nMelas. The expenditure, if any, is met out from the regular budget of the Central\nPassport Organization.\n*****\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |43", "t": "Coastal Tourism Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. -43\nANSWERED ON 09fi22022\nCOASTAL TOURISM PROJECTS\n-43. ADV. A.M. ARIFF:\nwill the Minister of poRTS, sHlpplNG AND wATERWAys be pleased to state:\nqil{, +f,qf{-rf,{ efuqeqrrf dfi\n(a) whether lhe Government has reviewed the progress of four coastal tourism projects\nincluded in the sagarmara programme in the Arappuzha parriamentary consiituency\nand if so, the detairs thereof including the current status of each of the said projects;\n(b) whether lhe Government has released/ sanctioned funds for completion of theabove-said projects and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps for compretion of the above-saidprojects in a time- bound manner; and\n(d) if so' the details thereof along with tentative deadlines for completion of each of theabove-said projects?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(sHRt SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) to (d). A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\n- )-\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO lN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d)\nsTARRED Qu ESrIo N No .43 AN swERED- oI :]: :-t::j?t\"T\nOF LOK SABHA\n2022 RAISED BY\ni riT ;\";;t-;n tt n n o \n\"' \nc \" c oA srA L r o u R I s M P R o J E c r s''\n(a) to (d) Holistic Development of Coastal Districts is a part of the Sagarmala\nProgramme of the Ministry oi pont' Shipping and Waterways formulated to bridge the\ncritical gaps in infrastructure in the coastal areas' stimulate local economy' attract\ninvestment and implement various development programme in a convergence mode to\nenrich and empower coastal communities'\nAfter multiple consultations with the concerned state governments' State\nMaritime Boards, 'nO \notn\"'- ti\"keholders representing coastal constituencies' the\nMinistry has identified a totat ot 567 projects with an estimated cost of Rs 58'704 cr\nunder the four focus \n\"\"\"- \nof Coastal infrastructure Development' Coastal lndustrial\nDevelopment, Coastal f ou'ltrn Development and Coastal Community Development'\nln the state of Kerala a total of 63 projects with an estimated cost of Rs 6'131 Cr\nhave been identified rn*\" p..\"f\"ttt aie retateO to Fishing Harbours' Lighthouses'\nSilosMarehouses,RoRo&PassengerFerryandSkillDevelopmentetc.\nln the district of Alappuzha a total of 4 tourism proiects with an estlmated \n-cost \nof\nRs.255.35 Cr. have 0\"\"'liJ\"ntiti\"o These proiects are related to Lighthouses and\nMaritime Museum. rne fisi oiproiects arong with status is provided at Annexure l'\nFor the projects identified in Holistic Development of Coastal Districts in\nAlappuzha. no funds n'ff'## '\"r\"a'eJ \nunoet Sagarmala Programme However'\nState of Kerala has 0\"\"\"\" '\"q'\"ti\"o \nio submit p|.opJt\"rt for funding' if any' under\nSaoarmata Scheme ;;\";il\";i';;r'i\"ii\"*\"0 in\"itntnor.\" prolects in Alappuzha\nDis-trict. project. or cor\"rli.,\"\"i \"i'r\"rrr, n\"i recently b\"een reviewed on 24 11 2O22'\n\nList of projects identified in\nAlappuzha\n-3-\nAnnexure I.\nHolistic Development of Coastal Districts in\nS\nNo\nManakkodam\nLighthouse\ntourism\nDevelopment\nArea\nLighthouse\ntourism\nprojects\nlmplementing\nAgency\nDirectorate\nGeneral of\nLighthouses\nand Lightships\n(DGLL)\nTotal\nProject\nCost (Rs\ncr)\nAt present the footfall at\nManakkodam Lighthouse\nis minute. DGLL is\ndeveloping sustainable\neco-friendly structures,\nsuch as souvenirs\ncounter, arts&crafts shop\nin eco-friendly structures,\nlandscaping & gazebo,\nopen cafeteria with no\nfire kitchen, movable\nseating arrangements,\nChildren play area, etc\nAlappuzha\n(Alleppy)\nLighthouse\ntourism\nLighthouse\ntourism\nprojects\nDirectorate\nGeneral of\nLighthouses\nand Lightships\n(DGLL)\nA maritime Heritage\nMuseum has been\nestablished\ndepartmentally at\nAlappuzha Lighthouse\nwhich exhibits heritage\nvalue of the Lighthouses\nand Maritime history of\nlndia. The lighthouse is\nprovided with basic\namenities for the visitors.\nln addition to the existing\nfacilities, DGLL is\ndeveloping facilities such\nas lnformation\nCentre/Souvenir\ncounter/Art &\nshops/Ayurvedic\nRejuvenation\nCentre/Coffee\nValiyazhikkal\nLighthouse\ntourism\nLighthouse\ntourism\nprojects\nDirectorate\nGeneral of\nLighthouses\nand Lightships\n(DGLL)\nDGLL is developing\nsustainable eco-friendly\nstructures, such as\nsouvenirs counter,\narts&crafts shop in eco-\nfriendly structures\n\n-q-\nlandscaPing & gazebo,\nooen cafeteria with no\nf iie kitchen, movable\nseating arrangements'\nChildren PlaV area' etc.-\n4\nrr;Covt ot rerala has\ninformed that AlaPuzha\nHeritage Proiect has\nbeen sanctioned. lt is\nI naving comPrehensive\nloroiect for linking\nI tairwaters, coastal and\nI monuments.\nAlapuzha\nHeritage\nProiect\n(Maritime\nM useum)\nPort &\nMaritime\nM useum\nTourism DePt,\nKerala\n250"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |44", "t": "National Centre of Excellence for Green Port & Shipping", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. -44\nANSWERED ON O9I12I 2022\nNATIONAL CENTRE OF EXCELLENCE FOR GREEN PORT & SHIPPING\n-44. SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT:\nSHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHtPPtNG AND WATERWAyS be pteased to state:\nqilr, d-d qft-{f,{ .1fu u-o+p1 qa\n(a) whether the country's first National centre of Excellence for Green port & shipping\n(NCoEGPS) a major initiative for providing greener solutions has been set up in the\ncountry, if so, the details thereof along with the salient features;\n(b) whether NCoEGPS will provide support on policy, research and cooperation on\nGreen shipping areas for Ports, DG shipping, cSL and other institutions and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(c) whether the ports also aim at reducing carbon emissions per ton of cargo handled by\nthem;\n(d) if so, the details of the existing carbon emissions per ton and also the reduction\ntargets fixed by various ports; and\n(e) whether the centre proposes to provide technological arms to support the port and\nshipping sector and also provide solutions to various problems being faced by industry\nthrough scientific research and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF PORTS,SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(sHRt SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) to (e). A Statement is laid on the Tabte of the House.\n\n-l-\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. -44 ANSWERED\nBY SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT\nREGARDING 'NATIONAL CENTRE OF\nSHIPPING\"\nPARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA\nON THE gTH DECEMBER,2022 RAISED\nAND SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE\nEXCELLENCE FOR GREEN PORT &\n(a) Yes, Sir.\nThe Memorandum of Agreement was signed on November 18' 2022 between\nMinistry of Ports' Shipping and Waterways (MoPSW) along with Deendayal Port\nAuthority, Kandla;Paradip Port Authority'Paradip; V O Chidambaranar Port\nAuthority,Thooth ukudi; Cochin Shipyard Limited' Kochi& The Energy & Resource\nlnstitute (TERI).\nThe details and the salient features of NCoEGPS are as follows:\n.NCoEGPSwillprovidepolicyandregulatorySupportfordevelopingregulatory\nframeworkandalternatetechnologyadoptionroadmapforGreenShippingtofoster\ncarbon neutrality and circular economy (cE) in the shipping sector in lndia'\n. The NCoEGPS will be housed at and led by'The Energy and Resources lnstitute'\n(TERI) as a knowledge and implementation partner'\n(b) Yes Sir.\nTheCentrewouldaimtoidentifythetechnologies,alternativefuels,renewable\nenergy sources and targets for the shipping and port sector to reduce GHG emissions in\nlinewiththeGoVt.oflndiacommitmentforthesector.Thelegalframeworkdevelopedby\nthe centre will support research and cooperation on green shipping andwill aid to\nformulate policy that will best suit the industry The Center will work with all ports'\nshipping companies, and maritime states to understand their problems and offer\nsolutions based on resea rch '\nP.T.O\n\n-3-\nThe broad focus areas of NCoEGPS are as follows:\ni. Policy, Regulatory and Research\nii. Human Resource development\niii. Network- Key Partners and Strategic collaborators\niv. Explore- Area ofwork, outcomes, projects and resources\nv. Engage- Past events, upcoming evenls, dissemination\n(c) & (d) Yes, Sir.\nOur lrinistry is taking a number of initiatives to contribute towards fulfillment of\nnational commitments towards achieving sustainable development goals. Maritime lndia\nVision - 2030 aims to reduce Carbon dioxide emission by 30% by the year 2030. Major\nsteps taken by the Ports to achieve the set targets include transition lo Renewable\nenergy in a big way. As on date, the instalted capacity of Solar power in the Major ports\nof the country, has gone up to around 1tB iIW. Similarly, on shore Wind Mills have been\ninsialled to generate 20.7 MW of power. Further, to cut down Carbon emission from the\nShips during idling white at Berth, the facility for Shore_to_Ship powering is being\ninstalled at a number of Major ports. Major ports have also taken initiative to deploy\nelectrically powered cranes and to convert the existing cranes also to electrical powered\nequipments. Hybrid tugs and hydrogen fuel cell powered port crafts are also being\ndeveloped to reduce carbon emissions. Similarly, the Ministry has initiated a number of\nactions lo increase transportation of Cargo and passengers through lnland WateMays\nand promoting modal shift from road and rail to Sea. A number of incentives are being\nprovided to promote coastal shipping.\nThe performance of Major ports have been consistenfly improving. Average\noutput per Ship berth-day during Fy 2022-23 has increased by 32% as compared to Fy\n2013-'14. Similarly, the average Turn Around Time of Container Vessels has improved by\n35% and has come down to only 26.5 hours an Fy 2021-22 as compared to Fy 2}]|g_14.\nThese improvements in operational efficiency have helped Major ports in reducing\ncarbon emissions by improving visibility of supply chain as also reducing idling time of\nvessels and vehicles operating in the port area.\nP.T,O\n\n-4-\n(e)"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|STARRED   |45", "t": "Cases of Cancer", "head": "������������������� �\n�������������������� �������������������� ���������� �\n��������� �\n���������������������� ����\n����������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������� �\n�\n������ ����������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������\n�\n�