4098\n \n4\n \nBANKA\n \n1575080\n \n5\n \nBEGUSARAI\n \n36298464\n \n6\n \nBHAGALPUR\n \n13938537\n \n7\n \nBHOJPUR\n \n5509563\n \n8\n \nBUXAR\n \n5708478\n \n9\n \nDARBHANGA\n \n14992077\n \n10\n \nGAYA\n \n24751513\n \n11\n \nGOPALGANJ\n \n7290851\n \n12\n \nJAMUI\n \n5400\n \n13\n \nJEHANABAD\n \n463618\n \n14\n \nKAIMUR (BHABUA)\n \n1763562\n \n15\n \nKATIHAR\n \n5581432\n \n16\n \nKHAGARIA\n \n7997822\n \n17\n \nKISHANGANJ\n \n9020306\n \n18\n \nLAKHISARAI\n \n600648\n \n19\n \nMADHEPURA\n \n1280493\n \n20\n \nMADHUBANI\n \n7412107\n \n21\n \nMUNGER\n \n5546359\n \n22\n \nMUZAFFARPUR\n \n29132229\n \n23\n \nNALANDA\n \n9188316\n \n24\n \nNAWADA\n \n2825132\n \n25\n \nPASHCHIM  CHAMPARAN\n \n14040146\n \n26\n \nPATNA\n \n229751501\n \n27\n \nPURBI  CHAMPARAN\n \n12741150\n \n28\n \nPURNIA\n \n9718249\n \n29\n \nROHTAS\n \n11602810\n \n30\n \nSAHARSA\n \n5499402\n \n31\n \nSAMASTIPUR\n \n6093673\n \n32\n \nSARAN\n \n9595351\n \n33\n \nSHEIKHPURA\n \n309100\n \n34\n \nSHEOHAR\n \n1695644\n \n35\n \nSITAMARHI\n \n6417956\n \n36\n \nSIWAN\n \n2179298\n \n37\n \nSUPAUL\n \n1187290\n \n38\n \nVAISHALI\n \n25751319\n \n \n \nTotal\n \n536616602\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |3", "t": "Export of Broken Rice", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 03 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nEXPORT OF BROKEN RICE \n \n03.  SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री)be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)   whether it is not true that there has been a huge  demand for broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo-political reasons; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; \n(c)   whether it is also not true that export of broken  rice has increased by more than 40 \ntimes in the last four years and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether 21.03 lakh tonnes of broken rice was  exported between April -August, 2022; \nand \n(e)   if so, the reasons for banning broken rice exports from the country? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a) & (b):  Yes Sir. There has been sudden increase in demand for Indian broken rice in the \ninternational market due to geo -political reasons. India’s exports of broken rice have increased \nfrom 17.86 Lakh MT in 2021 -22(April- September) to 23.82 Lakh MT in 2022 -23 (April -\nSeptember) registering a growth of 33.37%.  \n \n(c):  No Sir. The export of broken rice from India has increased about three time s in the last \nfour years i.e from 12.21 Lakh MT in 2018 -19 to 38.90 Lakh MT in 2021 -22 in volume and in \nvalue terms i.e USD 369.58 Million in 2018 -19 to USD 1132.94 Million in 2021 -22. However, \n\n2 \n \nexport of broken rice witnessed a decline in 2019 -20 due to th e pandemic and domestic policies \nof major importing countries in Africa. The details are given in the Table below: - \n \nYear Quantity in MT Value in Million USD \n2018-19 12,21,617 369.58 \n2019-20 2,70,338 82.48 \n2020-21 20,64,562 595.69 \n2021-22 38,90,866 1132.94 \n2021-22 \n(April-September) \n17,86,435 516.89 \n2022-23 * \n(April-September) \n23,81,657 764.81 \nSource: DGCIS, Kolkata.   \n* Figures for 2022-23 are provisional and subject to change. \n \n(d): A total of 21.64 Lakh MT of broken rice was exported from India during the period \nbetween April-August in 2022. \n \n(e):   Broken rice is majorly used for ethanol production and as poultry & cattle feed.  As \nevident from Table at Para -(c) above, there has been sudden rise in demand for Indian broken \nrice due to geo -political situation around. Further, as per the first Advance Estimates for 2022 -\n23,released by Directorate of Economics and Statistics, Department of Agriculture and Farmers’ \nWelfare, the production of rice is estimated to be lower than the target fixed for the  current year. \nTherefore, in order to ensure adequate availability of broken rice in domestic market,the \nGovernment has prohibited export of broken rice w.e.f 9thSeptember, 2022.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |30", "t": "PM MITRA Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *30 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \nDPR OF INTER-LINKING OF RIVERS \n*30  SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has prepared draft DPR f or five river linking projects in the country and \nmentioned in the recent budget 2022-23 that once th e consensus is reached among the beneficiary States , \nthe Government would provide for its implementation and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether any States have specifically objected o r have expressed any reservations which are causing  \ndelay in implementation of these river linking projects and if so, the details thereof including the nature of \nthe objections; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to include in t hese five draft DPR the other pending projects on t he \nrespective rivers which are going to be linked toge ther including river-basin area relating to ground water \nrecharge/conservation/irrigation project(s) especially for Par-Tapi-Narmada river linking project; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI    \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*30 TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 REGARDING “DPR OF INTER-LINKING OF \nRIVERS”.  \n(a) & (b)  The five river linking projects for which draft Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) were completed, \nas mentioned in the speech of Union Minister of Fin ance on 01.02.2022 for Union Budget for financial \nyear 2022-23, include Damanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tap i-Narmada link projects apart from the three \ncomponents of the Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Gr and Anicut) link project, viz; Godavari \n(Inchampalli)-Krishna (Nagarjunasagar), Krishna (Na garjunasagar)-Pennar (Somasila) and Pennar \n(Somasila)-Cauvery (Grand Anicut) links.These links  projects are among the 30 link projects identified  \nunder the National Perspective Plan (NPP) prepared by the Government of India. \nDamanganga-Pinjal and Par-Tapi-Narmada link project s are twin link projects concerning the States of \nMaharashtra and Gujarat.  DPR of Damanganga-Pinjal link was circulated to the States in 2014 and \naccorded techno-economic clearance in 2016 while re vised DPR  of Par-Tapi-Narmada link was \ncirculated to the States in 2017. Various consultat ion meetings have been held by the Government of \nIndia.  \nAs envisaged under the NPP, Godavari is to be augme nted by transfer of surplus waters from Mahanadi \nand Brahmaputra basins, for further diversion of wa ters to Krishna, Pennar and Cauvery basins. Pending  \nconsensus on Mahanadi-Godavari and Godavari (Inchampalli)-Krishna link projects, alternative studies to \ndivert surplus waters available in Godavari basin and unutilized waters in Indravati sub-basin of Godavari \nbasin through Godavari-Cauvery link project were ca rried out by National Water Development Agency \n(NWDA). This link project traverses through Godavar i, Krishna, Pennar, Palar and Cauvery basins and \nlies in the States of Telangana, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.  \nThe DPRs of Godavari (Inchampalli)-Cauvery(Grand An icut) link project were prepared in consultation \nwith the party States and circulated to them in Apr il, 2021. The party States have raised concerns rel ated \nto surplus waters availability in Godavari basin an d quantum of allocation of waters to the States. \nConsultation meetings with party States/Union Terri tory (UT) have been held and as decided during the \nmeeting held on 18.02.2022, a Technical Feasibility  Report (TFR) has also been prepared by NWDA to \nrecast the proposal limiting the transfer of waters  from 7,000 Million Cubic Metres (MCM) to about \n4,000 MCM from Godavari basin, in the interim phase , along with combining the proposal for \nsupplementation in Krishna basin through Bedti-Vard a link. The consultation meeting was last held on \n18.10.2022 and efforts made for the party States to  arrive at a consensus for implementation of the li nk \nproject. \nThe implementation of the link projects depends upon the party States to arrive at a consensus. \n  \n(c) & (d)  No such proposal is under consideration. \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |300", "t": "Setting up of Small Hydro Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 300 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \n \nSETTING UP OF SMALL HYDRO PROJECTS \n300. ADV. A. M. ARIFF \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps for encouraging the setting up of new Small Hydro \nProjects(SHPs) in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has stopped the Central Financial Assistance (CFA)/Grant/Subsidy for \nsetting up new SHP projects since 2017 and if so, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether it has come to the notice of the Government that the State of Kerala has proposed the \ndevelopment of ten SHP projects with a cumulative capacity of 26.9 MW and if so, the action taken \nthereon; \n(d) whether the Government proposes financial support in the form of capital subsidy of at least Rs. \nFive Crore/MW for these projects; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n                                                                        ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) Yes Sir. The Government in the past had provided Central Financial Assistance (CFA) for setting \nup of Small Hydro Power projects in the country as per the provisions made in the schemes. \n \n(b) Yes Sir. The Government has stopped Central Financial Assistance/Grant/Subsidy for setting up \nof SHP projects since September 2017 as there was no Scheme to provide financial support for such \nprojects.   \n \n(c) This Ministry has not received any formal request regarding the proposal for development of ten \nSHP projects with a total capacity of 26.9MW from Government of Kerala. \n \n(d) & (e) No Sir. At present, there is no scheme in this Ministry to provide financial support for \nsetting up of Small Hydro Power projects. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |301", "t": "Free Electricity to Farmers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.301 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \n \nFREE ELECTRICITY TO FARMERS \n \n†301.  SHRI RODMAL NAGAR: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether free electricity is being provided to  Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe \nfarmers having  land upto one hectare and agricultural pumps upto  five HP in Madhya \nPradesh; \n \n(b)  if so, the quantum of funds spent on it  during the last year and the current year;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government has any plan for  redressal of complaints related to \nirregular electricity  supply, burnt faulty transformers, power line  obstructions and \nerroneous bills in urban and rural  areas within the time limit in the interest of electricity  \nconsumers; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) & (b)  :     As per information received from Government of Madhya Pradesh , free \nelectricity is being provided to Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe farmers having land \nupto one hectare and agricultural pumps upto five HP in Madhya Pradesh.  \n \nSubsidy claims of SC/ST farmers having land upto 1 hectare and agriculture \npump upto 5 HP for last year and current year are mentioned below: \n  \nYear FY 2021-22 FY 2022-23 (upto August 2022) \nAmount Rs 4506.87 Cr Rs. 1403.38 Cr \n  \n(c) & (d)  :  The Electricity (Rights of Consumers) Rules, 2020 and its subsequent \namendments in 2021 and 2022 has provisions for having a call centre for registering and \ntracking of service requests and complaints received from the consumers, compensation \nto consumers for not meeting standards of performance specified by the Commission. \nThese Rules also provide for es tablishment of Consumer Grievance Redressal Forum \n(CGRF) at different levels to cater the needs of the sub -division, division, circle, zone, \ncompany level. \n************ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |302", "t": "Long Term Purchase Agreement", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 302 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08th December, 2022 \nLong Term Purchase Agreement  \n302. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government oil marketing companies have entered into a Long-Term \nPurchase Agreement (LTPA) for upcoming dedicated ethanol plants across the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the Government has undertaken any other steps to promote public sector \nparticipation in setting up dedicated ethanol plants; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000 वर तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL \nGAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a) to (d): Government Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have entered into a Long \nTerm Offtake Agreement (LTOA) with 131 project proponents for setting up \ndedicated ethanol plants under Ethanol Blended Petrol (EBP) Programme across the \ncountry, which includes both public and private project proponents. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |303", "t": "NH Projects in Karnataka", "head": "   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  No. 303 \nANSWERED ON 08th DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nNH PROJECTS IN KARNATAKA \n  \n303.    SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n  \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्गमंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details and the present status of ongoing National Highway (NH) \nprojects in the State of Karnataka as on date;  \n(b) the funds allocated, released and utilized on each of the said \nprojects as on date; and  \n(c) the present status of Shiradi Ghat Tunnel, strengthening of Bantwal-\nBangalore Section of   NH-275, widening of Thirthahalli -Malpe section \nand construction of four -lane highway from Parkala to Malpe on NH -\n169A? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(Shri NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI)  \n \n  \n(a) & (b) The details of ongoing National Highway (NH) projects including \ntheir present status/ expenditure incurred in the State of Karnataka as \non date are at Annexure I. \n  \n(c) The present status of Shiradi Ghat Tunnel, strengthening of Bantwal-\nBangalore Section of NH -275, widening of Thirthahalli -Malpe section \nand construction of four -lane highway from Parkala to Malpe on NH -\n169A are at Annexure II. \n  \n\n ANNEXURE-I \n  \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY to PART (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 303 FOR ANSWER FOR 08.12.2022 ASKED \nBY SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL REGARDING NH PROJECTS IN \nKARNATAKA. \n  \nDetails of ongoing National Highway (NH) projects including their \npresent status/ expenditure incurred in the State of Karnataka as on \ndate. \nSl. 