m \n0.000 to km 29.960) \n29.960 Yes \n7   4-lane bypass on western side \nof Gwalior (Gwalior ring road) 28.8 Yes \n\nMadhya \nPradesh \nJabalpur Ring Road(4 pkgs) 90.35 \nSagar Link Road - Berkhedi-\nSagar 25.8 \n8   \nJ&K \n4 lane Jammu Ring Road of \nlength 58.255 Km \n  \n58.255 10:90 basis by State \n/Union Government \n4 lane Srinagar Ring Road of \nlength 42.10 Km (Phase-I) \n42.10 \nDevelopment of existing \nSumbal – Wayul NH-1 to four-\nlane NH Standards  to complete \nsemi ring road around Srinagar \n(Srinagar Ring Road Phase – II) \n24 \n9 Bihar Patna Ring Road 140 No \n10  Telangana The details of outer ring roads \non National Highways under the \njurisdiction of NHAI, RO \nHyderabad in the State of \nTelangana is NIL. However, the \ndetails of Regional Ring Road \nunder the jurisdictoin of NHAI, \nRO Hyderabad in the State of \nTelangana is mentioned below:  \na) Northern Portion:  \nSangareddy (on NH -65) – \nNarsapur – Toopran – Gajwel – \nJagdevpur - Bhongir-Choutuppal \n(on NH-65) - 158.600 Km (design \nlength) - (Declared as NH-161AA \nin Ju ne 2017).  \n \nHowever, MoRTH, New Delhi \nvide letter no.RW/NH -\n12027/13/2020-TS/Zone-III, \ndated 05/03/2021 (copy \nenclosed), decided to take up \nthe development of Northern \nPortion of Regional Ring Road \naround Hyderabad City starting \nfrom its junction with NH No.161 \nnear Sangareddy and \nconnecting Narsapur, Toopran, \n158.60 \nThe Government of \nTelangana. \nhas given undertaki\nng to share 50% of \nthe land acquisition \ncost. \n\nGajwel, Pragnapur, Jagdevpur, \nBhongir and terminating at its \njunctions with NH -"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |41", "t": "CMDLD", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.41 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07/12/2022 \n  \nCMDLD \n  \n41. SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n           \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps to ameliorate lung diseases collectively termed \nCoal Mine Dust Lung Disease (CMDLD) including Coal Workers’ Pneumoconiosis, silicosis, \nmixed dust pneumoconiosis, dust -related diffuse fibrosis and chronic obstr uctive pulmonary \ndisease; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nAnswer \n  \nMINISTER OF  PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) & (b) CIL and its subsidiaries have taken required steps to ameliorate coal mine dust lung \ndiseases (CMDLD) including Coal Workers' Pneumoconiosis (CWP). These are as under: \n1. Adequate measures are taken to prevent generation, propagation and control of air \nborne coal dust in the mines and its surroundings as per various provisions made \nunder relevant statutes. \n2. Appropriate site specific dust suppression systems are also adopted in mines to keep \nmine environment dust free. \n3. PME (Periodic Medical Examination) is done for all employees at regular interval at \nvarious PME centers of different subsidiaries of CIL. \n4. Some patients with respiratory problems are treated at hospitals of CIL and other \ntertiary level empaneled hospitals. \n5. Various camps are organized at regular interval at area level of subsidiaries to screen \nany Lungs disease in its early stage.  \n6. Preventive measures like distribution of anti-dust mask and other protective gears are \ndistributed among employees regularly. \n7. Provision of spirometry and chest x-ray imaging is available at various PME centers \nof the subsidiaries.  \n8. Time to time counseling and mental health sessions are organized for employees. \n9. Pneumoconiosis board is being conducted in a regular interval on the suspected cases \ntaking the help of specialist doctors (pulmonologists and radiologists) and ILO \nrecommended X-ray plates are referred. \n10. On confirmation of pneumoconiosis, the patients are offered alternative jobs and they \nare observed for disease progression in a regular interval. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |410", "t": "Techniques of Rain Water Harvesting", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 410 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \nTECHNIQUES OF RAIN WATER HARVESTING \n410.  SHRI HAJI FAZLUR REHMAN \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government proposes any scheme to develop new technology for rain water harvesting; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has taken adequate measures for implementation of the said scheme; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the details of the assistance provided to the States for the said scheme? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n \n(a) to (d)  Water is a State subject and the Central Government supplements the efforts of the States on water \nconservation and recharge, including rainwater cons ervation and its harvesting, through technical and \nfinancial support.  \n  \nAdoption of new and advanced technology for harvest ing of rainwater is a continuous process and \nvaries from region to region depending upon the cli mate, soil-strata and other related factors. The Mi nistry \nof Jal Shakti incentivizes usage of new and advance d technology in water conservation through the Nati onal \nWater Awards. Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urb an Transformation (AMRUT) has a component of \ntechnology sub-mission which helps in identifying p roven and potential global technologies in water se ctor \nincluding technology for harvesting of rainwater. I ndian Council of Agriculture Research (ICAR) has \ndeveloped improved water harvesting technologies. I CAR also provides technical backstopping to various  \nschemes/ programmes of the Government and imparts t rainings to farmers/stakeholders on rainwater \nconservation as and when required. Various technolo gies commonly used for rain water harvesting have \nbeen described in the “Manual on Artificial Recharg e of Ground Water” are  available at \nhttp://cgwb.gov.in/documents/Manual-Artificial-Recharge.pdf. \nGovernment of India has been implementing various s chemes for rainwater conservation and its \nharvesting. Major schemes/ programmes implemented b y the Government inter alia for promoting rainwater  \nconservation include Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS), Atal \n\nMission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation ( AMRUT) and Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sichai Yojana \n(PMKSY). Government of India has also initiated ‘Ja l Shakti Abhiyan’ (JSA) in 1,592 blocks of 256 wate r-\nstressed districts of the country during July to No vember 2019 to promote water conservation and water  \nresource management by focussing on water conservat ion & rainwater harvesting, renovation of tradition al \nand other water bodies/ tanks, reuse, and recharge of bore wells, watershed development and intensive \nafforestation. Third in the series of JSA, ‘Jal Sha kti Abhiyan: Catch The Rain’ in the current year ha s been \nlaunched on 29 March, 2022 in all districts (rural as well as urban areas) of the country with the mai n theme \n“Catch the Rain, where it falls, when it falls”. Wa ter conservation and rainwater harvesting is one of  the \nfocussed interventions of the campaign. States are converging resources from available Central schemes , \nState funds, 15th Finance Commission grants etc. to  implement the Jal Shakti Abhiyan. Details of steps  \ntaken by the Government to promote rainwater conservation and its harvesting are at Annexure-I.  \n \n(e) The State-wise details of expenditure under MGN REGS on water conservation and water harvesting for  \nthe last three years and in the current year are gi ven at Annexure-II . The State-wise details of Central \nAssistance given to various States during 2018-19 t o 2022-23 under Surface Minor Irrigation (SMI) and \nRepair, Renovation & Restoration of Water Bodies (R RR of WBs) of PMKSY are given at Annexure-III  \nand the details of Central Assistance released duri ng 2018-19 to 2021-22 under Watershed Development \nComponent of PMKSY are given at Annexure-IV.  Financial assistance of Rs. 2 lakh is released to each \ndistrict in two instalments of Rs. 1 lakh each for GIS mapping of water bodies and preparation of scie ntific \nwater conservation plans under JSA:CTR. The details  of funds released under JSA:CTR and JSA:CTR-2022 \nare given at Annexure-V. Financial assistance given to various States by t he Department of Drinking Water \nand Sanitation under 15th Finance Commission tied g rant during 2020-21 to 2022-23, which can, inter-al ia, \nbe utilized for rainwater harvesting and water recy cling is at Annexure-VI . Details of Central Assistance \ngiven to States/ UTs for project implementation und er the AMRUT during 2019-20 to 2022-23 are at \nAnnexure-VII  and the details of Central Assistance given to Sta tes/ UTs against approved State Water \nAction Plan (SWAP) and Special State Water Action P lan (SSWAP) under AMRUT 2.0 are at Annexure-\nVIII.  \n \n \n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |411", "t": "Utilization of Funds for Urban Development Schemes", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 411 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 08, 2022 \n \nUTILIZATION OF FUNDS FOR URBAN DEVELOPMENT SCHEMES \n \nNO. 411.   SHRIMATI  RAJASHREE  MALLICK:   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)   the funds allocated and utilised under urban develo pment schemes \nduring each of the last three years; \n (b)  whether a large part of funds allocated for urban development to the \nStates during the said period remains unspent; \n (c) if so, the details thereof along with the reas ons therefor, State and \nscheme/project-wise including Odisha; \n (d)  whether there is any mechanism to monitor the  utilisation of funds \nallocated to the States;  \n (e) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken /likely to be taken to \nstrengthen this mechanism; and \n  (f)  the steps taken by the Government to make Ur ban Local Bodies \n(ULBs) more efficient and empower them in order to make demand \nfor funds and utilise them along with the details o f steps taken to \ninsure the financial stability of ULBs? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  The details of funds released and utilized on the u rban development \nschemes - Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban T ransformation \n(AMRUT), Smart Cities Mission (SCM), Swachh Bharat Mission-Urban \n(SBM-U), Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Urban (PMAY-U),  North-\nEastern Region Urban          Development Programme  (NERUDP) and \n10% Lumpsum Scheme of the Ministry of Housing and U rban Affairs \n(MoHUA)  are given in Annexure-I.  \n...2/- \n\n \n \n \n-2- \n \n(b)&(c) Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs approve s the overall State \nplans and releases the central financial assistance  under its \nmissions.  The detailed projects are conceived, app raised, \napproved and implemented by the States / Union Terr itories (UTs).  \nThe urban infrastructure projects generally require  three years for \nexecution and completion after selection of cities / projects, \napproval of plans and setting up of implementation structures.  \nUtilisation of funds released under the missions / schemes depends \nupon these aspects.  Moreover, utilization certific ates are \nsubmitted by the States / UTs for seeking subsequen t instalments, \nwhen it has reached upto the threshold for the next  release.   \nHence, there is a gap between actual utilization on  ground and \nutilisation reported by the States / UTs.  Details are given in \nAnnexure-II. \n \n(d)&(e) In order to monitor progress of schemes and  funds allocated to \nthe States / UTs, various institutional frameworks,  like submission \nof periodic progress reports, review meetings, vide o conferences, \nfield visits have been put in place.  Instalments o f central \nassistance are released keeping in view the reports  of the \nIndependent Review and Monitoring Agencies (IRMA) /  Utilisation \nCertificates issued by the State Government authorities as required \nunder the respective scheme guidelines.  \n \n(f) Central and State Governments provide support t o Urban Local \nBodies finances as per the requirements for the imp lementation of \nschemes / missions. In order to enhance capacity of  ULBs Ministry \nis also providing assistance for establishing State  Level Technical \nCell (SLTC) and City Level Technical Cell (CLTC). \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I as referred to in reply to part (a) of L ok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo 411 regarding ‘Utilization of Funds for Urban De velopment Schemes’ for \nanswer on 08 December, 2022. \n  \nStatus of funds allocation and utilization under Urban Development Scheme \n                                                                    (₹  in crores) \nSl No  Name of the \nMission/ \nScheme \n \nAllocation Utilization \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2019-20 to \n2021-22 \n1. AMRUT &  \nAMRUT 2.0 \n6210.18 6382.99 7197.83 14968.95 \n2. SCM 3450.00 3400.00 6600.00 13339.87 \n3. SBM-U \n& SBM-U 2.0 \n1251.56 968.92 1932.56 1103.69 \n4. PMAY-U 19068.25 27282.21 22158.54 66265.69 \n5. NERUDP* 259.99 125.00 98.05 456.71** \n6. 10% Lump \nSum Scheme \n$ \n \n121.00 18.56 Nil 128.73 \n* Scheme closed on 31.03.2022. \n** the funds were released on the basis of reimbursement.   \n$  Scheme closed on 31.03.2021. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-II as referred to in reply to part (b) & ( c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No 411 \nregarding ‘Utilization of Funds for Urban Developme nt Schemes’ for answer on 08 \nDecember, 2022. \nAtal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) \nState-wise details of UC pending/unspent balance du ring last three financial years and \ncurrent year under AMRUT mission as on 30.11.2022  \nS.No.  Name of State / UT (Figures in ₹ crore) \nFY 2019-20 FY 2020-21 FY 2021-22 Total \n1 Andaman and Nicobar 0.01 0.01 4.32 4.34 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 36.80 0.00 0.00 36.8 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0.00 0.00 56.44 56.44 \n4 Assam 0.40 1.49 40.19 42.08 \n5 Bihar "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |412", "t": "Road Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 412 \nANSWERED ON 08TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nROAD PROJECTS \n \n412. SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE: \n        DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \n        SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \n        DR. AMOL RAMSINGH KOLHE: \n        SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has sanctioned various road construction \nprojects in Maharashtra and Andaman and Nicobar Islands during each of \nthe last three years and the current year;  \n(b) if so, the name of each project along with the criteria fixed by the \nGovernment for sanctioning    road projects;  \n(c) the funds sanctioned and utilized for each project, till date; \n(d) the time frame fixed by the Government for completion of each project;  \n(e) whether any delay has been reported in completion of the said \nprojects, if so, the details there of and the reasons therefor; and  \n(f) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to complete the said \nprojects in a time bound manner? \n\nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)to(d)  Ministry of Road Transport & Highways is primarily responsible \nfor development and maintenance of National Highways (NHs) . Works \non NHs are taken up from time to time  based on traffic density, inter-\nse priority and availability of funds .The details  of NH projects \nsanctioned in the State of Maharashtra during each of the last three \nyears and the current year along with funds utilized and time frame for \ntheir completion are at annexure. No work was sanctioned during last \nthree years and current year in Andaman & Nicobar Islands. \n \n(e) & (f) Some of the above projects are delayed mainly due to delay \nin  land acquisition, delay in utility shifting, non -availability of \nsoil/aggregate, poor performance of contractors  delays in  \nenvironment/forest/wildlife clearances, delays in approvals for  ROBs & \nRUBs, public agitations for additional facilities, arbitration/contractual \ndisputes and covid pandemic etc. To fast-track these projects, various \nsteps such as streamlining of land acq uisition &environment /forest \nclearances, close coordination with other Ministries, revamping of \ndispute resolution mechanism, frequent review meetings with project \ndevelopers/ State Government  authorites and contractors at various \nlevels etc., are being taken up. \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 412 FOR ANSWER ON 08.12.2022 ASKED BY \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE AND OTHERS REGARDING ROAD \nPROJECTS. \n \nDetails of NH projects sanctioned in the States  of Maharashtra during \neach of the last three years and the current year along with funds \nutilized and time frame for their completion. \n \nSl. \nNo. Project Name NH \n \nSanction\ned \ncost(Rs. \nin Cr.) \nExpenditure \n(Rs. in Cr.) \nLikely date \nof \nCompletion  \n2019-2020 \n1 \n4 laning Km 0.000 to Km \n110.542 Solapur Vijayapur \nsection of  NH-13 \n13 1576.79 1880.37 19.04.2022 \nCompleted \n2 \n4 laning Km  99.400 to Km \n138.352 Akkalkot Solapur \nsection of NH-150E. \n150\nE 807.00 722.26 31.12.2022 \n3 \nBorgaon – Watambare – II \nfrom Km. 224.000 to \nKm.276.000 Section of \nNH- 166 On HAM Basis \n166 982.35 747.88 31.12.2022 \n4 \n4 laning  Km. 276.000 to \nKm.321.600 Watambare – \nMangalwedha – III on NH \n166 \n166 869.44 789.08 31.12.2022 \n\n5 \n4 laning of Mangalwedha – \nSolapur  - IV from Km. \n321.600 to Km. 378.100 \nSection of NH- 166  on \nHAM \n166 1101.89 772.18 31.12.2022 \n6 \nSangli – Borgaon – \nPackage - I from Km. \n182.556 to Km. 224.000 \nSection of NH- 166 - On \nHAM Basis \n166 1009.33 747.93 31.03.2023 \n7 \nMohol-Wakhari  Package - \nI from Km. 0+000 to Km. \n44+700 (Saint \nDyaneshwar Palakhi Marg \nof NH- 965) \n965 731.89 554.07 31.03.2023 \n8 \nWakhari - Kudus Package \n- II from Km. 44+700 to \nKm. 77+800 (Saint \nDyaneshwar Palakhi Marg \nof NH- 965) \n965 362.46 321.92 28.02.2023 \n9 \nKhudus to Dharmapuri \nPackage - III from Km. \n77+800 to Km. 115+350 \n(Saint Dyaneshwar \nPalakhi Marg of NH- 965) \n965 694.75 441.80 31.03.2023 \n10 \nBalance work of Khed \nGhat realignment \n(km.46/981 to km.51/400) \nand Narayangaon Bypass \n(km.76/300 to km.81/200) \n(total length- 9.319 km) in \nKhed –Sinnar section of \nNH-50 (new –NH-60) On \nEPC mode \n60 72.89 70.29 20.11.2021 \nCompleted \n11 \nConstruction of new six \nlane tunnel at Khambataki \nGhat Section at NH-4 \n4 493 212.76 14.03.2023 \n\n12 \nIntegrated Infrastructure \nand Road Development on \nNH-4 at Chandani Chowk \n4 397.61 228.78 31.01.2023 \n13 \n6/8 laning of Vadape to \nThane (Bhiwandi Bypass) \nsection of NH-3 (new NH-\n848) \n3 1182.87 99.43 31.05.2024 \n14 \nConstruction of New 4-\nLane Bridge across \nVarsova Creek and its \napproaches with \ninterchange from Km \n496+100 to Km 498+350 \nbetween Surat-Dahisar \nSection of NH-48 \n48 157.49 142.25 31.08.2023 \n15 Bal.Work of Fagne -MH/Guj \nBor"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |413", "t": "New Airports under UDAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 413\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nNEW AIRPORTS UNDER UDAN SCHEME\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to establish new airports under UDAN\nScheme in the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof including the places selected in Tamil Nadu;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up small airport in Palani which\nis one of the Hindu pilgrimage centre in Tamil Nadu;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Civil Aviation has launched Regional Connectivity\nScheme (RCS) - UDAN (Ude Deshka Aam Nagrik) on 21.10.2016 to stimulate\nregional air connectivity and making air travel affordable to the masses.\nUDAN is a market driven scheme. An airport which is included in the\nawarded routes of UDAN and requires upgradation / development for\ncommencement of RCS operations, is developed under \"Revival of unserved\nand underserved airports\" scheme. The UDAN scheme does not envisages to\nestablish new airports.\n \nIn the state of Tamil Nadu, five airports namely Salem, Neyveli, Tanjore,\nVellore and Ramnad, have been identified for up-gradation for the operation\nof flights under UDAN. Operation of RCS flights from Salem has commenced.\n \n(c) to (e): Palani airstrip is not available in the UDAN document. However, for\nthe forthcoming round of bidding under UDAN, if the State Government of\nTamil Nadu requests to include the same under UDAN, the same shall be\nconsidered as per the provisions of scheme document.\n \n413. SHRI VELUSAMY P.\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |414", "t": "Declaration of SH as NH", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 414 \nANSWERED ON 08TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nDECLARATION OF SH as NH \n \n414. SHRI GUMAN SINGH DAMOR: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether a proposal to declare Baroda, Chhota Udaipur, Alirajpur, \nKukshi, Barwani, Khargone, Khandwa State Highways (SH) as National \nHighways (NH) is pending and if so, the time by which it is likely to be \ndeclared as NH and if not, whether it is likely to be considered; \n \n(b) the time by which sanction for construction work is likely to be given \nfor SH connecting Alirajpur in Dahod which was declared as NH and the \ntime by which the construction work is likely to commence;  \n \n(c) the monitoring authority responsible for the q uality of construction \nwork of the four -lane road from Rajgarh to Dattigaon in Dhar district on \nNH-47 which was constructed in May -June 2022 and got completely \ndamaged during the rainy season; and  \n \n(d) the remedial steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)&(b) The stretch es from Chhota Udaipur to Alirajpur, Barwani to \nKhandwa and Ambua to Dahod fall on existing National Highways (NHs) \nwhich have been developed to 2L+PS/ 4 -lane/ in DPR stage. Further \naction depends on outcome of DPR for Ambua to Dahod section.  The \nStretch from Baroda to Chhota Udaipur,  Alirajpur to Barwani and \nAlirajpur to Ambua  are state roads and are not NHs.  Ministry of Road \nTransport & Highways  is primarily responsible for development and \nmaintenance of National Highways (NHs).  Ministry keeps on receiving \nproposals from various State Governments/Union Territories (UTs) for \ndeclaration of State roads as new National Highways (NHs). Ministry \nconsiders declaration of some of the State roads as new NHs from time \nto time based on requirement s of connectivity, traffic levels  inter-se \npriority and availability of funds.  \n(c) & (d) The construction work of the four -lane road from Rajgarh to  \nDattigaon in Dhar district on NH -47 was awarded  by National \nHighways Authority of India (NHAI) on 15.01.2021 at a contract price \nof Rs. 210 Cr. Appointed date of the work was 12.04.2021 and \nScheduled date of  completion was 11.04.2023. NHAI has engaged \nAuthority Engineer  for supervising the work and  for monitoring \nof quality of the aforesaid work.  The works are supervised by the \nAuthority Engineer. Apart from this the works are monitored through \ninspections at different levels of NHAI. Defects in the DBM and other \nlayers were observed  during the construction period  and the \ncontractor has been asked to rectify the defects, however, the same \nis yet to be fully rectified. Show cause notice has also been issued to \nAuthority Engineer for deficiency in services. NHAI has also engaged \nCentral Road Research Institute (CRRI) to investigate the cause of \ndamages and to suggest remedial measures to rectify the defects.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |415", "t": "LPG Pipeline in Jaunpur", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 415 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8TH DECEMBER, 2022 \nLPG Pipeline in Jaunpur \n \n415. SHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to lay a LPG gas pipeline in Jaunpur district and if \nso, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to open CNG/PNG centres in Jaunpur district and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the time by which it is likely to be opened; and  \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to promote/introduce CNG/PNG in Jaunpur district? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000  )  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a)  IHB Limited, a Joint Venture of IOCL, HPCL and BPCL is already laying 2805 km long \nLPG Pipeline from Kandla in Gujarat to Gorakhpur in Uttar Pradesh.  The capacity of Pipeline is \n8.25 MMTPA at a cost of Rs.10088 Crores.  Upon completion, the pipeline traverses about 21.7 \nKm in Jaunpur district of Uttar Pradesh. \n  \n(b) & (c)  Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) is the authority to grant \nauthorization to the entities for development of City Gas Distribution (CGD) network in \nGeographical Areas (GAs) which includes providing PNG connections and setting up of CNG \nstations. The Geographical Area of Jaunpur and Ghazipurdistricts has been authorized to Indian \nOil Adani Gas Private Limited with Minimum Work Programme (MWP) Target of 77,033 PNG \ndomestic connections and 38 CNG stations till November 2027. As on 30.9.2022, Indian Oil \nAdani Gas Private Limited has established 9 CNG stations in the GA. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |416", "t": "Business Credit Card", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 416\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n08.12.2022\n \n \nBUSINESS CREDIT CARD\n \n \n \n416\n.          \nSHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe salient features of the Business Credit Card Scheme (BCCS) under the Ministry;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nwhether the said scheme has been implemented across the country and if so, the list\n \nof \nbeneficiaries along with the eligibility; and \n \n \n \n(c)\n \n \n \n \nif not, the time by which the said scheme is likely to be implemented along with outline of the  \nBCCS?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR THE MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(\nSHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA\n)\n \n \n(a) \nto (c)\n:\n \nMinistry of MSME in association with National Payment Corporation of India (NPCI) \nhas recently launched the MSME RuPay Credit Card on pilot basis,\n \npan\n-\nIndia for Udyam registered \nMSMEs. \n \n \nThe MSME RuPay Credit Card provides a simplified payment mechanism to MSMEs to meet \ntheir business\n-\nrelated operational expenses like digital payments, utility bills payments, tax/statutory \npayments etc. and MSME bor\nrowers also takes benefit of  interest\n-\nfree credit period on their business \nspends as per the bank's policy. \n \n \n******\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |417", "t": "Extension of PM SVANidhi Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 417  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 08, 2022 \n \nEXTENSION OF PM SVANIDHI SCHEME \n \nNO. 417.  DR. VISHNU PRASAD M. K. : \n \n Will the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased \nto state:   \n(a)  whether the Prime Minister Street Vendors AtmaNirbh ar Nidhi \n(PM SVANidhi) scheme has been extended by the Gover nment \ntill December 2024; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the number of beneficiaries benefited under the sai d scheme \nso far; \n(d)  whether the vending zones have also been extended a nd if so, \nthe total number of vending zones till date; and \n(e)  the total number of vendors targeted to be provided  benefit \nunder this scheme by December 2024? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  & (b): Yes Sir. The Government of India has extende d the PM \nStreet Vendor’s AtmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) Sch eme beyond \nMarch, 2022  with the following provisions:  \n(i)  Extension of lending period till December 2024; \n(ii)  Introduction of 3rd loan of upto ₹50,000 in additio n to 1st & \n2nd loans of ₹10,000 and ₹20,000 respectively. \n(iii)  To extend ‘SVANidhi Se Samriddhi’ component for all  \nbeneficiaries of PM SVANidhi scheme across the country;  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \n(c)  As on November 30, 2022, 31.73 lakh Street Ven dors have \navailed benefit of 1st loan of ₹ 10,000 loan ; Out of these 5.81 lakh \nhave availed benefit of second loan of ₹ 20,000 loa n; Out of those \nwho availed 2nd loan, 6,926 street vendors have ava iled benefit of \nthird  loan of ₹50,000.  \n \n(d)     The subject matter relating to creation of vending zone falls \nunder the ambit of Street Vendors (Protection of Li velihood and \nRegulation of Street Vending) Act, 2014, which is b eing \nimplemented through respective State/UT.  As report ed by the \nStates/UTs, a total of 13,403 vending zones have be en identified so \nfar. \n \n(e)  42 lakh street vendors are to be provided bene fits under PM \nSVANidhi Scheme by December, 2024. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |418", "t": "Consumption of LPG", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 418 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8th DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nConsumption of LPG \n \n418. SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI:  \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n Will the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether any study has been conducted regarding the consumption/use of quantity of LPG in \nvarious Indian households and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the number of 1st, 2nd and 3rd Refills done under PMUY during the last three years, State/UTs \nwise;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has conducted any feedback or satisfaction study for beneficiaries of \nPMUY Scheme; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  रामे\u0000वर  तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d) Details regarding consumption/use of quantity of LPG in Indian households are \nreported to the Government by Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies. Consumption of \ndomestic LPG by households depends on several factors like food habits, household size, \ncooking habits, price, availability of alternate fuels etc. Per capita consumption of Pradhan \nMantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) beneficiaries has been consistently rising and has increased \nfrom 3.01 refills in 2019-20 to 3.68 in 2021-22. \nGovernment has taken several steps to encourage consumption of LPG by PMUY \nbeneficiaries, which include subsidy of Rs. 200/- per 14.2 Kg refill upto 12 refills/year for \nPMUY beneficiaries for year 2022-23, option of 5 Kg Double Bottle Connection (DBC), \nswap option from 14.2 Kg to 5 Kg, upto 3 free refills to PMUY beneficiaries under Pradhan \nMantri Garib Kalyan Package from April to December 2020 etc. \nState/UT and Year-wise details of PMUY beneficiaries who have taken one, two  and \nthree or more refills (including installation refilled) during the last three years are at \nAnnexure.  \n------- \n \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred in part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 418  to be answered on \n08.12.2022 asked by Shrimati Kanimozhi Karunanidhi  regarding \"  Consumption of LPG ” \nState/UT Financial Year 2019-20 Financial Year 2020-21 Financial year 2021-22 \nOnly One \nrefill \nOnly Two \nrefills \nThree or \nmore refills \nOnly One \nrefill \nOnly Two \nrefills \nThree or \nmore refills \nOnly One \nrefill \nOnly Two \nrefills \nThree or more \nrefills \nAndaman & \nNicobar 1,070 2,140 3,341 423 1,840 9,758 1,533 1,347 7,893 \nAndhra Pradesh 80,505 77,153 1,92,898 22,510 41,153 3,26,869 53,200 70,141 2,66,930 \nArunachal \nPradesh 6,935 7,087 23,469 2,241 6,184 36,064 7,550 7,191 26,394 \nAssam 9,16,461 5,33,132 11,33,277 6,30,763 7,82,747 19,92,161 9,78,811 6,24,232 14,98,120 \nBihar 18,35,341 13,48,867 41,53,334 5,88,114 12,28,803 65,92,201 18,59,956 17,92,240 56,69,322 \nChandigarh 3 4 74 - 1 86 11 9 60 \nChhattisgarh 6,82,464 3,45,140 5,33,842 8,38,541 7,76,163 11,68,335 9,69,487 5,07,182 7,85,959 \nDadra Nagar-\nHaveli Daman \n& Diu 2,438 2,460 7,328 895 2,773 11,452 707 1,923 12,236 \nDelhi 2,520 2,678 68,028 497 1,278 73,732 10,205 10,581 72,817 \nGoa 130 166 696 41 103 926 73 128 822 \nGujarat 4,55,494 4,41,164 16,76,880 2,21,156 3,50,275 23,00,365 4,81,482 5,00,385 22,01,477 \nHaryana 67,817 81,311 5,45,894 14,632 43,704 6,60,499 43,330 60,051 5,90,122 \nHimachal \nPradesh 24,675 26,901 71,426 1,602 11,023 1,23,168 16,809 20,198 86,551 \nJammu & \nKashmir 4,76,671 2,23,555 3,62,861 1,57,778 3,14,224 7,39,496 2,48,089 2,72,059 5,74,385 \nJharkhand 8,55,315 4,48,066 9,59,774 5,14,833 8,36,128 18,66,321 8,31,373 6,46,520 12,79,826 \nKarnataka 5,46,424 5,50,846 16,78,855 1,38,590 2,91,902 26,98,399 3,77,893 4,63,965 24,59,628 \nKerala 36,559 53,960 1,39,806 8,286 21,456 2,25,684 40,263 47,908 1,93,455 \nLadakh 4,000 1,511 4,664 1,004 2,116 7,913 1,711 2,270 6,422 \nLakshwadeep 15 18 68 30 58 204 26 24 228 \nMadhya \nPradesh 20,81,583 12,20,885 21,74,977 9,54,268 16,71,113 44,50,674 18,84,750 15,73,822 34,38,806 \nMaharashtra 9,62,231 8,17,906 21,08,471 2,23,717 5,20,439 36,39,344 5,55,163 7,13,541 31,49,617 \nManipur 16,448 17,968 1,01,331 9,095 14,597 1,30,469 22,576 18,334 1,22,511 \nMeghalaya 32,794 22,334 49,285 37,058 34,312 75,617 38,268 20,587 62,783 \nMizoram 2,288 2,645 19,542 566 1,800 25,712 2,520 1,956 22,276 \nNagaland 13,681 8,372 21,647 6,348 11,373 37,169 21,412 10,873 30,815 \nOdisha 14,43,158 7,10,815 14,10,689 4,48,211 9,51,622 32,99,968 12,17,033 9,87,119 23,22,561 \nPondicherry 698 1,212 11,638 263 547 13,565 724 1,024 12,797 \nPunjab 1,79,936 1,67,451 7,63,477 27,823 81,675 11,00,631 80,971 1,20,078 9,59,582 \nRajasthan 14,33,980 12,22,217 29,45,165 3,74,131 9,31,610 50,02,954 7,64,165 10,68,409 44,50,589 \nSikkim 1,108 778 5,671 46 986 7,708 2,779 2,091 4,829 \nTamil Nadu 5,00,463 5,29,528 17,65,821 1,57,988 3,14,586 27,44,972 3,50,597 4,68,017 23,19,886 \nTelangana 2,21,231 2,39,994 4,79,194 59,387 1,48,060 8,61,110 1,36,469 1,84,207 6,86,022 \nTripura 50,689 33,757 90,246 49,772 40,446 1,61,117 33,569 27,022 1,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |419", "t": "Flights from Gujarat", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 419\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nFLIGHTS FROM GUJARAT\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government has started any new flight from Gujarat for\ndomestic and international destination; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b): The scheduled commercial airlines including M/s Air India, Go\nFirst, Indigo, Alliance Air, Star Air, SpiceJet and Vistara , operate from 10\nairports in Gujarat.\n \nRecently, a new scheduled commercial airline namely M/s Akasa Air has\nadded 41 flights per week connecting Ahmedabad to Mumbai, Banglore and\nDelhi . All the airports in Gujarat are inter-connected with other airports in\nthe country by direct or indirect flights.\n \n*****\n419. SHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |42", "t": "Railway Infrastructure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.42\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022\nPASSPORT MELAS\n42. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to organize passport melas and passport\nseva camps  in Gujarat in order to speed  up  passport  issuance  and meet high or\nseasonal demand for passports; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, district-wise; \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(d) the details of funds allocated towards organizing the passport melas during the\nlast three years, State-wise including Gujarat?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER FOR EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n (DR. SUBRAHMANYAM JAISHANKAR)\n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n1\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE\nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 42 REGARDING \"PASSPORT\nMELAS” FOR ANSWER ON 09.12.2022.\n(a) to (c) The Ministry has been working continuously to speed up Passport\nissuance and meet high or seasonal demand for Passports by taking various\nsteps including organizing of Special Drives or Melas on weekends. \nStarting 3rd December 2022, the Ministry has decided to launch yet another\nspecial drive by opening Passport Seva Kendras (PSKs) and some Post Office\nPassport Seva Kendras (POPSKs) for next 4 Saturdays including in the state of\nGujarat. On 3 rd December 2022, the Melas were organised at various PSKs\nunder  the  jurisdictions  of  20  Regional  Passport  Offices  (RPOs)  including\nAhmadabad and Surat. Under these two RPOs in Gujarat, there are 5 PSKs and\n23 POPSKs.\n(d) There are no separate funds allocated towards organizing the Passport\nMelas. The expenditure, if any, is met out from the regular budget of the Central\nPassport Organization.\n*****\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |420", "t": "Construction of New Airport Terminal Building at Kanpur", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 420\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nCONSTRUCTION OF NEW AIRPORT TERMINAL BUILDING AT\nKANPUR\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the construction of the new airport terminal building at Kanpur\nhas been completed and if so, the current status thereof;\n(b) whether it would also have increased capacity and world class facilities\nand if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the time by which it is likely to become operational?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (c): The development/revival work of a new Civil Enclave at Kanpur\n(Chakeri) Airport is being undertaken by the Airports Authority of India\nunder the scheme for revival of airports for operations of UDAN (Ude Desh\nka Aam Nagrik) flights. The project is in advance stage of completion.\n \nThe New Terminal Building of area 6243 Sq. mtr. (Ground Floor + First\nFloor) has state of the art features, depicting local art & heritage and is\ndesigned to accommodate 300 passengers during peak hours with all modern\npassenger amenities like check-in-counters, conveyor belt, concessionaires,\nchild care room, Lounge, parking bays etc.\n \n \n*****\n420. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |421", "t": "Ultra Mega Solar Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421 \nANSWERED ON 08.12.2022 \n \nULTRA-MEGA SOLAR PROJECTS  \n \n421. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR MONDAL \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the status of development works done in West Bengal under the scheme of Solar Park and \nUltra-Mega Solar Projects during the last two years and the current year; \n(b) the number of approved solar parks and their capacity; \n(c) the implementation status along with future initiatives taken or being taken in this regard; \nand \n(d) the quantum of funds allocated for the development works under the said scheme during \nthe financial year 2021-22, State/UT-wise including West Bengal? \n \n                                                      ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) One solar park of capacity 125 MW, earlier approved to West Bengal State Electricity \nDistribution Company Limited (WBSEDCL) in West Bengal, was cancelled on 19.08.2020 \ndue to slow progress. Currently there is no solar park approved in West Bengal under the \nscheme for  Solar Park and Ultra-Mega Solar Projects \n \n(d) State/UT-wise details of funds released under the scheme during the financial year 2021 -\n22, are given at Annexure. \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (D) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421 FOR 08.12.2022 \nS. No. State Solar Park Funds released in FY \n2021-22 (Rs. in lakhs) \n1.  Gujarat \nRadhnesada Solar Park \n(External  Transmission \nSystem-PGCIL) \n2800.00 \n2.  Karnataka \nPavagada Solar Park \n(External  Transmission \nSystem-PGCIL) \n4000.00 \n3.  Madhya Pradesh \nAgar Solar Park 1723.50 \nShajapur Solar Park 1800.00 \nNeemuch Solar Park 1560.48 \n4.  Mizoram Vankal Solar Park 48.00 \n5.  Rajasthan \nNokh Solar Park  2716.04 \nBhadla Ph-III, Ph-IV and \nPhalodi Pokaran Solar \nPark (External \nTransmission System-\nPGCIL) \n6000.00 \n6.  Uttar Pradesh \nJalaun Solar Park  25.00 \nLalipur Solar Park  25.00 \nJhansi Solar Park  25.00 \nMirzapur Solar Park  10.00 \nTOTAL 20733.02 \n \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |422", "t": "Price of Petroleum Products", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8 DECEMBER, 2022 \nPrice of Petroleum Products \n422. SHRI DEEPAK BAIJ:  \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of increase/decrease in price of petrol, diesel, Petroleum Natural Gas (PNG), \nAviation Turbine Fuel (ATF) and Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) from August, 2022 to \nNovember, 2022, item, date and State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of increase/decrease in price of crude oil in international market from August, 2022 \nto November, 2022, date-wise; \n \n(c) whether the prices of above items have been in accordance with the increase/decrease of rates \nin international market during the above said period/dates; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) if not, the details of difference therein, item/date-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी   \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000 वर तेल\u0000) \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a) to (e): Prices of petrol, diesel and Aviation Turbine Fuel (ATF) have been market \ndetermined with effect from 26.06.2010, 19.10.2014 and 1.4.2001 respectively. Since then, the \nPublic Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) take appropriate decision on pricing of petrol,  \ndiesel and ATF in line with international product prices and other market conditions. \nThe details of increase/decrease in price of petrol, diesel from August, 2022 to November, 2022 \nare given at Annexure- I. \nThe monthly declared prices of Aviation Turbine Fuel (ATF) at Delhi airport for the period \nAugust 2022 to December 2022 are given at Annexure -II. \nGovernment of India has allocated domestic gas to various City Gas Distribution (CGD) entities \nfor CNG (Transport) and PNG (Domestic) purpose. Retail price of CNG/PNG is determined by \nCGD entities after considering cost of gas procured, state taxes, tariff and other components. \nThe Monthly average prices of Indian Basket of Crude Oil for the period August, 2022 to \nNovember, 2022 are given below:  \nMonth Indian Basket of Crude Oil ($/bbl) \nAugust 2022 97.40 \nSeptember 2022 90.71 \nOctober 2022 91.70 \nNovember 2022 87.55 \n   Source: PPAC \nx-x-x-x-x \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e)  of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 422 asked by Shri Deepak Baij to be answered on 08 December, 2022 \nregarding “Price of Petroleum Products” \n                      \nS.No. STATE Reference City Petrol (Rs./Litre) Diesel (Rs./Litre) \n01-Aug-\n22 \n01-Sep-\n22 \n01-Oct-\n22 \n01-Nov-\n22 \n01-Aug-\n22 \n01-Sep-\n22 \n01-Oct-\n22 \n01-Nov-\n22 \n1 ANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS PORT BLAIR                  \n84.10  \n                 \n84.10  \n                \n84.10  \n                 \n84.10  \n                 \n79.74  \n                 \n79.74  \n                \n79.74  \n                   \n79.74  \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH AMARAVATHI                  \n111.87  \n                 \n111.87  \n                \n111.87  \n                 \n111.87  \n                 \n99.61  \n                 \n99.61  \n                \n99.61  \n                   \n99.61  \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH ITANAGAR                 \n93.29  \n   