he work \n(A) Works under State PWD Kerala \n1 \nAnnual Plan 2019 -20 \nStrengthening and \ngeometric improvements \nfrom km 25/000 to 45/000 \non NH 766 in Kozhikode \ndistrict in the state of \nKerala \n27%  Contractor shall be \nterminated and work \nshall be re -arranged if \ncontractor does not \nrespond positively. \n2 \nRaised table top \npedestrian crossing on NH-\n766 b/w km 0/000 to 5/000 \nand on NH -66 b/w km \n242/000 to 246/500 \n40% Work temporarily \nsuspended due to \ntraffic issues. This \nissue is kept for \ndiscussion in the next \nroad safety meeting. \n3 \nKRSA-Traffic safety works \nbetween Pushpa Jn and \nKolathara road from km \n247/370 to 252/600 of NH \n66 \n95%  Delayed due to \nboundary disputes. The \nsame has been \nresolved now. The work \nis targeted to be \ncomplete by \n31.12.2022. \n4 \nBLACK SPOT -Providing \nshort term measures at \nidentified blackspots in \nNH66 (old NH) & NH766 in \nKozhikode district \n85% \nDelayed due to adverse \nweather. The work is \ntargeted to be \ncomplete by \n31.12.2022. \n5 \nGeneral Road Safety work-\nConstruction of drainage \nand foot path between km \n241/680-242/000 of NH 66 \n45% \n6 \nGeneral-Reconstruction of \ndamaged culvert at km \n258/900 (Near Poovannur \nPally) \n50% \n\n7 \nGeneral-Reconstruction of \nexisting damaged culvert \nat Moozhikkal km 9/620 of \nNH 766 \n50% \n8 \nTraffic Safety work -\nconstruction of side drain, \nfoothpath and improving \nshoulder at Koduvally town \nbetween km 22/000 to \n23/000 of NH 766 \n77% \n(B1) Works under NHAI (Already Awarded) \n1 \n6 Laning of Azhiyur (km \n189.200) to Vengalam (km \n232.100) section of NH-66 \n11.81% Project is delayed due \nto adverse weather \ncondition and COVID. \nRegular review meeting \nare taken to expedite \nthe progress by NHAI \nHQ and Regional office. \n2 \nConstruction of 6 Lane \nMinor Bridge (Paloli  Palam \nat Km 203.160 and Major \nBridge at (Km 204.895) on \nNH-66 \n73.65% \n3 6 Laning  of Kozhikode \nBypass section of NH-66 \n23.40% \n(B2) Works under NHAI (To be Awarded) \n1 \n4 laning of Kozhikode to \nPalakkad section (Package \n– 5, Vazahayoor -Ibingallur \nof NH-966 \nBids have \nalready been \ninvited. \n- \n  \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |431", "t": "Use of Inferior Construction Material", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †431 \nANSWERED ON 8th DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nUSE OF INFERIOR CONSTRUCTION MATERIAL \n  \n†431.   SHRI RAMSHIROMANI VERMA: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has taken legal action against agencies \nfound involved in using inferior construction materials in the \nconstruction work related to National Highways;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether such companies have been blacklisted/ proposed to be \nblacklisted by withholding their payments; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (d) All efforts are made to ensure that the National Highways (NHs) \nare constructed as per quality standards specified in the Ministry of \nRoad Transport and Highways/ Indian Roads Congress (IRC) \nspecifications and codes. Unless the roads are constructed as per the \nspecifications and codes, the contracts are not treated as complete. To \nensure the construction quality, Consultants (Authority’s Engineer/ \nIndependent Engineer) are appointed by the Ministry and its executing \nagencies for day to day supervision of the works at site. Deficiencies, if \nany, observed during such examination / supervision are brought to the \nnotice of the Concessionaire s / Contractor s for taking up necessary \ncorrective measures.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |432", "t": "Plantation of Trees along NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 432 \nANSWERED ON 08TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nPLANTATION OF TREES ALONG NHs \n  \n432. SHRI JASBIR SINGH GILL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of trees cut/uprooted to construct National Highways in \nthe country during each of the last three years; and  \n  \n(b) the funds released to various departments to plant trees in lieu \nthereof during the said period, department-wise? \n  \n   \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n  (SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) Total 14,32,294 trees have been cut/uprooted to construct National \nHighways in the country during the last three years. \n \n(b) Rs. 897.87 Cr. have been deposited in CAMPA Fund as \nCompensatory Afforestation to plant trees in lieu of trees cut during \nthe said period. \n \n \n***** \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |433", "t": "Imported Crude Oil", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 433 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8th December 2022 \n  \nImported Crude Oil \n \n433. SHRIMATI APARUPA PODDAR: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has laid down a road map for reducing country's dependency on \nimported crude oil; \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the action plan prepared and timeline for achieving \nthis target; \n(c) the steps taken to adequately increase the exploration and production of indigenous crude \noil by companies like Oil India Ltd., ONGC etc.; \n(d) the quantum of crude oil imported by the Government and expenditure incurred hereon \nduring the last five years, exporting country and year-wise; \n(e) the share of country's domestic requirement met by the imported crude oil during the last \nfive years, year-wise; and \n(f) the expected time by which the country's requirement of crude oil is likely to be fully met \nfrom indigenous sources? \n \n        ANSWER  \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  रामे\u0000 वर तेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n         \n(a) to (f) Yes Sir, Government has adopted five-pronged strategy comprising increasing \ndomestic production of oil and gas, promoting energy efficiency and conservation measures, \ngiving thrust on demand substitution, promoting biofuels and other alternate fuels/ \nrenewables, and refinery process improvements for reducing the county’s oil dependency on \nimported crude oil.  \n \nVarious steps have been taken by the Government to increase the production of domestic \ncrude oil and bring down imports. These inter-alia include Discovered Small \nField Policy, Reforms in Hydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy for enhancing \ndomestic exploration and production of Oil & Gas 2019, Natural Gas Marketing Reforms \n2020, Policy to promote and incentivize enhanced recovery methods for Oil and Gas, \nRedevelopment of existing matured fields and development of new/marginal fields, Revival \nof Sick Wells, improving recovery factors through the implementation of Improved Oil \nRecovery (IOR) and Enhanced Oil Recovery (EOR) techniques, etc. Government has also \nprovided functional freedom to national Oil Companies and promoted wider private sector \nparticipation by streamlining approval processes through electronic single window \nmechanism.  \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \nThe country imports oil and gas from various geographical regions including countries from \nthe Middle-East, Africa, Europe, North America, South America and South-East Asia. The \nyear-wise imports of crude oil and its dependency on country’s domestic requirement   during \nlast 5 years as under: - \n \nYear Quantity of Import  of \nCrude Oil (in MMT) \nValue of Import  of \nCrude Oil   (in ₹ Crore) \nImport Dependence of \nCrude Oil  (in %) \n2017-18 220.4 566450 82.9 \n2018-19 226.5 783183 83.8 \n2019-20 227.0 717001 85.0 \n2020-21 196.5 459779 84.4 \n2021-22 212.4 901262 85.6 \n \nGovernment has already resolved to make India energy independent before the completion of \n100 years of Independence.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |434", "t": "New Airports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 434\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nNEW AIRPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to open new airports in country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the State of Gujarat has been included in the proposal for the new\nairports; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b): Development of a Greenfield airport is governed under the\nGreenfield Airports (GFA) Policy, 2008. As per the Policy, a State\nGovernment or an Airport Developer, willing to establish an airport, is\nrequired to send a proposal to the Ministry of Civil Aviation (MoCA) for 2-\nstage approval i.e. 'Site-Clearance' followed by 'In-Principle' approval. Such\nproposals are considered by MoCA as per the procedure stipulated in the\nGFA Policy. The responsibility of implementation of airport projects\nincluding funding of the projects rests with the concerned airport developer\nincluding the respective State Government (in case the State Government is\nthe project proponent).\n \nGovernment of India (GoI) has accorded 'In-Principle' approval for setting\nup of 21 Greenfield Airports namely, Mopa in Goa, Navi Mumbai, Shirdi and\nSindhudurg in Maharashtra, Kalaburagi, Vijayapura, Hassan and\nShivamogga in Karnataka, Dabra (Gwalior) in Madhya Pradesh, Kushinagar\nand Noida (Jewar) in Uttar Pradesh, Dholera and Hirasar in Gujarat,\nKaraikal in Puducherry, Dagadarthi, Bhogapuram and Oravakal (Kurnool)\nin Andhra Pradesh, Durgapur in West Bengal, Pakyong in Sikkim, Kannur in\nKerala and Donyi Polo, Itanagar in Arunachal Pradesh across the country.\n434. SHRI PATEL HASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI\nSHRI RATANSINH MAGANSINH RATHOD\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n \nOut of these, 9 Greenfield airports viz. Durgapur, Shirdi, Kannur, Pakyong,\nKalaburagi, Orvakal (Kurnool), Sindhudurg, Kushinagar and Donyi Polo,\nItanagar have been operationalized.\n \n(c) & (d): GoI has granted 'In-Principle' approval to State Government of\nGujarat for development of two Greenfield Airports at Hirasar (Rajkot) and\nDholera (Ahmedabad). The State Government has collaborated with Airports\nAuthority of India (AAI) for development of the Hirasar airport at a project\ncost of Rs. 1405 crore. As regards development of Dholera Greenfield Airport\nat a project cost of Rs. 1305 crore, a Joint Venture Company comprising AAI,\nGovernment of Gujarat (GoG) and National Industrial Corridor\nDevelopment and Implementation Trust (NICDIT) holding equity in the ratio\nof 51:33:16 respectively has been formed to oversee its implementation.\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |435", "t": "Urban Employment Guarantee Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 435 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 08, 2022 \n \nURBAN EMPLOYMENT GUARANTEE SCHEME \n \n \nNO. 435  SHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA: \n         SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU: \n         DR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \na)  whether the Government has not felt the need for a employment \nguarantee scheme like NREGA for urban poor; \nb)  if so, the constraints being faced by the Governmen t to start urban \nemployment guarantee scheme for urban poor; \nc)  whether it is true that some States have already im plemented the \nscheme; \nd)  if so, the details thereof; and \ne)  the details of wages are being paid under the said scheme? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) and (b): Urban development, including urban pov erty alleviation is a \nstate subject. To complement the efforts of State/ UT governments, the \nGovernment of India is implementing various Urban M issions to alleviate \nurban poverty and provide basic urban services, inc luding Deendayal \nAntyodaya Yojana - National Urban Livelihoods Missi on (DAY-NULM), PM \nStreet Vendor's AtmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) Sch eme, Pradhan Mantri \nAwas Yojana – Urban (PMAY-U), Atal Mission for Reju venation and Urban \nTransformation (AMRUT), Swachh Bharat Mission – Urban (SBM-U) etc. \n……2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \n(c) to (e): As per information available, States th at are implementing \nurban employment scheme are Tripura (Tripura Urban Employment \nProgramme, since 2009), Kerala (Ayyankali Urban Emp loyment Guarantee \nProgramme, since 2010), West Bengal (West Bengal Ur ban Employment \nScheme, since 2010), Odisha (Mukhyamantri Karmatatp ara Abhiyaan, \nsince April, 2020), Himachal Pradesh (Mukhya Mantri  Shahri Ajeevika \nGuarantee Yojana) and Jharkhand (Mukhyamantri Shram ik Yojana, since \n14.08.2020). Rajasthan has announced the Indira Gan dhi Shahri Rojgar \nGuarantee Yojana, in its budget for FY 2022-23. The  wages being paid & \nbudget allocation for the schemes vary from State to State. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |436", "t": "Safety Norms at Airports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 436\n(To be answered on the 8th December 2022)\n \nSAFETY NORMS AT AIRPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \na) whether the Government has taken any steps for the safety, security and\nconvenience of passengers keeping in view the huge increase in passenger\ntraffic volumes at major airports of country.\n \nb) if so, the details thereof?\n \nc) whether any action has been taken against airlines for violation of safety\nnorms; and\n \nd) if so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b) A regulatory framework is in place to ensure safety and security of\npassengers during air travel. The safety and security requirement are\nstrengthened in terms of deployment and policy interventions/amendments in\nthe existing rules/regulations, in line with ICAO SARPs, from time to time on\naccount of various reasons including increase in passenger traffic volume.\nAlso, to meet the growing demand of the aviation sector, upgradation and\nmodernization of airports is a continuous process which is undertaken from\ntime to time. The airport developers including Airports Authority of India\nhave targeted a capital outlay of approximately Rs. 91,000 crores in airport\nsector in next five years for expansion and modification of existing terminals\nand strengthening of runways among other activities. DGCA has issued Air\nTransport Circular 2 of 2019 titled \"Facilities/courtesies to esteemed\ntravelling public at airports\" which lays down the general requirements for\nairlines and airports to ensure adequate facilitation of the passengers,\nparticularly senior citizens, expectant mothers, passengers with disability,\n436. SHRI RAMESH BIDHURI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nfirst time travelers, etc.\n \n(c) & (d) The details of the action taken on airline operators for violation of\nsafety norms for the year 2022 is at Annexure I.\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |437", "t": "Highway Projects in Chhattisgarh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.437 \nANSWER ON 08TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n  \nHIGHWAY PROJECTS IN CHHATTISGARH \n  \n437. SHRI ARUN SAO: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of highway construction projects passing through \nBilaspur (Chhattisgarh) during the last three years and the current \nyear; \n(b) whether any project for development/ upgradation of highways \npassing through the said region has also been taken up during the \nsaid period; \n(c) if so, the details and the present status thereof along with the \nfunds allocated/released/utilised for the said purpose; \n(d) the details of the ongoing National Highway projects in \nChhattisgarh; and \n(e) the time by which the said projects are likely to be completed? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (e) the details of  NH projects completed during the last Three \nyears and the current year on NHs passing through Bilaspur are \nplaced at Annexure -I. List of ongoing development projects  along \nwith their likely date of completion on NHs in Chhattisgarh  are at \nAnnexure-II. Funds are allocated for all NH projects state wise and \nnot project wise. Expenditure is incurred on individual NH projects \nfrom the overall allocation for the state as per the progress of the \nworks. \n\n Annexure-I \n  \nSl \nNo. \nName of Project Total Project \nCost \n(Rs in Crore) \nLength \n(in Km) \n1 Rehabilitation and upgradation of NH-200 (New \nNH=49) from km 127.500 to 178.944 (Village \nTheka/Darrighat to Village Banari Section)    to \nfour/two -lane with paved shoulders in the State \nof Chhattisgarh under NHDP -IV on EPC basis. \n(Job No. 200-CHH-NHDPIV-2015-16-33) \n512.77 33.187 \n2 4 Laning of Sargaon - Bilaspur  Section of NH -\n200 ( New NH -130) from Km 91+026 to Km \n126+525 \n535.220 35.499 \n3 4-laning of Bilaspur -Pathrapalli from Kn.0+000 \nto Km.53+300 of NH-130 \n1261.05 53.300 \n4 04 laning of Bilaspur Urga Project from Km \n0+000 to 70+200 of NH-130A \n1745.45 70.2 \n  Total 4054.490 192.186 \n  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \nSl \nNo. \nName of Project Length \n(in Km) \nTPC \n(Rs in \nCrore) \nLikely date \nof \nCompletion \n1 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-200 (New NH -49) from km \n178.944 to 241.553 (Banari village \nto Masania Kala village \nSection)  to two -lanes with paved \nshoulders in the State of \nChhattisgarh under NHDP -IV on \nEPC basis. (Job No. 200 -CHH-\nNHDPIV-2015-16-24) \n55.647 543.730 30-Jun-23 \n2 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-43 from km 130.000 to 180.000 \n(Kanker to Bedma  section) \nPackage-II, to two -lanes with \npaved shoulders in the State of \nChhattisgarh under NHDP -IV on \nEPC basis. (Job No. 43 -CHH-\nNHDPIV-2015-16-23) \n53.258 572.660 31-Mar-23 \n3 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-78 from km 373.505 to 469.300 \n(Ambikapur to \nPathalgaonSection)   to two -lanes \nwith paved shoulders in the State \nof Chhattisgarh under NHDP-IV on \nEPC basis. (Job No. 78 -CHH-\nNHDPIV-2015-16-25) \n74.630 731.500 31-Jul-23 \n4 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-78 from km 469.000 to 531.000 \n(Patthalgaon to Kunkuri) to two -\nlane with paved shoulders in the \nState of Chhattisgarh under NHDP-\nIV on EPC basis.  (Job No. 78-CHH-\nNHDPIV-2015-16-40) \n62.480 435.610 31-Dec-23 \n5 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-12A from km 246.849 to \n317.406 (Kawardha to Simga \nSection) in the State of \n71.030 394.200 31-Dec-22 \n\nChhattisgarh to two -lanes with \npaved shoulders under NHDP-IV on \nEPC basis. (Job No.12A -CHH-\nNHDPIV-2014-15-22) \n6 Rehabilitation and upgradation of \nNH-111 from km 163.400 to \n215.800 (Shivnagar to Ambikapur \nSection) in the State of \nChhattisgarh to two -lanes with \npaved shoulders under NHDP-IV on \nEPC basis. (Job No. 111 -CHH-\nNHDPIV-2015-16-17) \n52.400 335.320 31-Dec-22 \n7 Strengthening and widening of \nexisting 2 lane road to two lane \npaved shoulder from km 7.750 to \n26.542 & km. 32.093 to 45.786 of \nNH-130 on EPC mode in the state \nof chhattisgarh.(job No. 130-CG-\n2017-18-348