. DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN: \n     \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has initiated AYUSH Mission with the objective to establish \nAYUSH hospital in each district of the country;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has approved the AYUSH hospital in Bihar particularly in \nGopalganj district;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and progress made in this regard; and  \n \n(e) the amount sanctioned and utilized for establishment of AYUSH hospital so far? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) and (b) Ministry is implementing the Centrally Sp onsored Scheme of National AYUSH  \nMission (NAM) through State/UT Governments for development and promotion of Ayush \nsystem and providing financial assistance to them  for different activities  as per the proposals \nreceived in their State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs)  in accordance to NAM guidelines. Further, \nunder NAM, there is a provision for setting up of 10/30/ 50 bedded integrated AYUSH hospitals \nand State/UT Governments are eligible for financial assistance by submitting suitable  proposal \nthrough SAAPs as per the provision of NAM guidelines.  The details of approved Integrated \nAyush Hospitals in the country is furnished as Annexure. \n(c) to (e) As per the proposal submitted by the State Government of Bihar , Ministry has \napproved one new 50- Bedded Integrated Ayush Hospital in Bihar particularly in Gopalganj \ndistrict during the year 2022 -23. The Grants-in-aid would be released as soon as the conditions \nof Department of Expenditure guidelines vide no. F.N.1 (13) PFMS/FCD/2020 dated 23.03.2022 \nand corrigendum dated 21.06.2022 regarding procedure for release of funds under the Centrally \nSponsored Schemes (CSS) and monitoring utilization of the funds is met. \n \n \n\n2 \n \n \n         \nAnnexure \nThe approved Integrated Ayush Hospitals in the country under National AYUSH Mission \nfrom 2014-15 to 2021-22 \nS. No. State/UT Location of integrated Ayush Hospital \n1.  Andaman Nicobar Islands Port Blair \n2.  Andhra Pradesh Kakinada \nVisakhapatnam \n3.  Assam Goalpara \nMajuli \n4.  Bihar Patna \n5.  Chandigarh Chandigarh \n6.  Chhattisgarh \nChampa \nMahasamund \nKoria \nKorba \nKanker \nNarayanpur \nBijapur \nDantewada \nRaipur \nBilaspur \nDurg \nSarguja District Headquarter \nDalli Rajhara \n7.  Goa South Goa \n8.  Haryana Hisar \n9.  Himachal Pradesh Kullu \nMandi \n10.  Jammu & Kashmir \nKishtwar \nKupwara \nBilawar \nKulgam \nSamba \n11.  Jharkhand Ranchi \n12.  Karnataka Gadag \nMangalore \n13.  Kerala Chalakudy, Thrissur \nMattannur, Kannur \n14.  Lakshadweep Kavaratti \n15.  Maharashtra \nNandurbar \nSindhudurg \nPune \nAhmednagar \n16.  Manipur Moreh, Chandel District \n\n3 \n \nChurachandpur \nKwakeithel Konjeng Leikai \nKeirao AC, Imphal East District \n17.  Madhya Pradesh \nBhopal \nIndore \nNarsinghpur \nAmarkantak \nMandleshwar \nBalaghat \nSehore \n18.  Meghalaya Sohra (Cherapunje), CHC, East Khasi Hills \nUmtrew, Ri Bhoi District \n19.  Mizoram Aizwal \n20.  Nagaland \nNoklak, Tuensang District \nRazha, Chedema \nSapangya (Chungtia) \nYachem, Longleng \n21.  Odisha \nDhenkanal \nBehrampur \nBalasore \n22.  Puducherry Villanur \nYanam \n23.  Punjab Moga \nDayalpur Sodhia Zirakpur \n24.  Rajasthan \nBhilwara \nAjmer \nChuru \nBikaner \nJaipur \nSikar \n25.  Sikkim Kyongsa, West Sikkim \n26.  Tamil Nadu \nTheni \nThiruvannamalai \nPudukkottai \n27.  Telangana \nAnantgiri, Vikarabad, Rangareddy Distt. \nBhupalpalli (V&M), Jayashankar Bhupalpalli \nDistrict. \nSiddipet Head Quarters \n28.  Tripura Paradise Chowmuhani, Agartala \nBelonia \n29.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nBulandshahar \nBilhour, Kanpur \nWest Katli, Lucknow \nBadrasi, Varanasi \nNawab Ganj, Bareilly \nBasti \nSirathu Kaushambi \nSonbhadra \nOrai Jalaun \n\n4 \n \n \n \n \n \nUttar Pradesh \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n  \nSant Kabir Nagar \nSaharanpur \nDeoria \nLalitpur \nAmethi \nKanpur Dehat \nFirozpur, Balia District \nRaebareli \nBaghpat \nFatehpur \nShravasti \nUnnao \nHardoi \nGorakhpur \nSambhal \n30.  Uttarakhand \nHaldwani \nJakhnidhar, Tehri \nTanakpur, Champawat \n31.  West Bengal Tapsikhata, Alipurduar District \nPaschim Midnapore \n \n \n    \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |561", "t": "Scheme for Single Working Women", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT  \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.561 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022 \n  \nSCHEME FOR SINGLE WORKING WOMEN \n  \n561:   SHRI RAMCHARAN BOHRA: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the schemes introduced or proposed to be introduced to enable the \nsingle working women to maintain their families; \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to impart professional/vocational training to \nsuch women; and \n(c) the details of professional/ vocational courses being run for such women?  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \n MINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)       Under the new Mission Shakti launched by the Ministry, Working Women \nHostel(WWH) is known as Sakhi Nivas.  The scheme is meant to promote \navailability of safe and conveniently located accommodation for working  women, with \nday care facility for their children  in urban, semi urban, or even rural areas where \nemployment opportunity for  women exist, as per local needs and requirement of the \nStates/UTs.    Under the Scheme, financial assistance is provided for running of Hostel \nin rented premises for working women who may be single, widowed, divorced, \nseparated, married but husband or immediate family does no t reside in the same area \nand for those women who are pursuing higher education and training for job.  Provision \nof Day Care Centre for children of the residents of the Hostel is an important aspect of \nthe scheme. \n  \n(b)&(c) Ministry of Skill Development an d Entrepreneurship has informed that Women \nTraining under Directorate General of Training, Ministry of Skill Development & \nEntrepreneurship, takes care of providing skill training to women in the country which \naims at stimulation employment opportunities a mong women of various socio -economic \nlevels and different age groups.   \n  \nThis is aimed to provide comprehensive training in both hands -on skills and \ntraining methodology to the instructor trainees to make them conversant with techniques \nof transferring ha nds-on-skilled manpower for the industry. Out of Network of 33 \nNational Skill Training Institutes (NSTIs), 19 NSTIs are exclusively earmarked for \nwomen.   \n\n  \nThe NSTIs for Women organize skill training programmes running for current \nsession (2022 -23) under Craft Instructor Training Scheme (CITS) in the following 14 \ntrades: \n  \nSl. No. Name of Courses \n1.   Architectural Draughtsman \n2.   Computer Aided Embroidery and Designing \n3.   Computer Software Application \n4.   Cosmetology \n5.   Draughtsman Civil \n6.   Dress Making \n7.   Electronics Mechanic \n8.   Fashion Design & Technology \n9.   Information Technology \n10.   Interior Design and Decoration \n11.   Office Management \n12.   Secretarial Practice (Hindi) \n13.   Sewing Technology \n      14 Surface Ornamentation Techniques \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |562", "t": "Repeal of Obsolete and Archaic Laws", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.562 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 9TH DECEMBER, 2022 \nRepeal of Obsolete and Archaic Laws \n562.  SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether obsolete and archaic laws are causing impediments in the \nnormal life of a common man and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether more than 1500 obsolete Acts exist in the country and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the government proposes to repeal those Acts during the \ncurrent calendar year; \n \n(d)    if so, the details thereof; and   \n \n(e)  the reasons for not fixing any timelines for review of every \nlegislation made by the Parliament to avoid the issue of repealing them? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU)     \n(a) to (e) : obsolete and archaic  laws lay down unnecessary compliance \nburden on citizens. Therefore , it has been the resolve of this Government \nto reduce such compliance burden, bring reforms in the legal system and \nmake it more accessible to the common man. \n \nIn line with this resolve, the Government has repealed 1486 obsolete andn \nredundant Central Acts from May, 2014 till date. Besides, 76 Central Acts \nrelating to State subject have also been repeale d by the concerned State \nlegislature. \n  \n As the laws are reviewed on the basis of feed back received, rese arch, \nreports generated, survey and audit conducted etc. which is a tim e-\nconsuming process, it may not be possible to fix timeline for the same. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |563", "t": "New Shipping Channels", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS. SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED OUESTION NO. 563\nANSWERED ON O911212022\nNEW SHIPPING CHANNELS\n563. SHRI SAUMITRA KHAN:\nWill the l\\ilinister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqf,r, frd qf{{f,{ 3i-t ildcFf d-n\n(a) whether the Governmenl has any proposal to set up new shipping channels for\ntransportation of goods on cost efficient manner across the country especially in\nWest Bengal;\nif so, the details thereof;\nwhether the Government has received any proposals from State Government in\nthis regard; and\nif so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon?\n(b)\n(c)\n(d)\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) & (b) Yes, Sir. To promote lnland Water Transport (lWT) in the country, 111\nWaterways (including 5 existing and 106 new) spread over 24 States have been\ndeclared as National WateMays under the National Waterways Act, 2016. Based on\nthe techno-economic feasibility and Detailed Project Report (DPR) of the NWs, 26 NWs\nhave been found viable for cargo/ passenger movement as detailed at Annex.-1. Out of\nthese, NW-86, NW-97 & NW-44 fall in the state of West Bangal in addition to existing\nNW-1 (the Ganga - Bhagirathy - Hooghly river system). Development for shipping &\nnavigation has been taken up on 14 NWs in various States as detailed at Annex.-ll.\n(c) No proposal for development of NWs has been received from the State Govt. of\nWest Bengal.\n(d) Does not arise in view of the reply to (c) above.\n\n'') -\nAn nex. J\nLrsr oF 26 NATIONAL WATERWAY?N.!4'9I\nrouruoEaSELe roR ceneo I passeucen uovemeNr\nsl.\nNo NW Details of WaterwaYs\nLength\n(Km) STATES\nI NW1 Ganga-Bhagirathi-Hooghly River System (Haldia\n- Allahabad)\n1620 Uttar\nPradesh,\nBihar,\nJharkhand &\nWest\nBengal\n2 NW2 Brahmaputra River (Dhubri - Sadiya) 891 Assam\n3 NW 16 Barak River (Lakhipur -Tuker Gram) 121 Assam\n4 NW3 West Coast Canal (Kottapuram - Kollam),\nChampakara and Udyogmandal Canals-\n205 Kerala\nR NW4 Krishna River (Vijayawada - Muktyala)\na.) Andhra\nPradesh\n6 NW5 Dhamra-Paradip via Mangalagq!i!9!349pgl-- ZJJ Odisha\n7 NW8 Alappuzha- Changanassery Canal 29 Kerala\n8 NW9 Alappuzha - Kottayam - Athirampuzha Canal\n40 Kerala\n(Alternate\nroute:\n'1 1.5km)\no NW 27 Cumberjua River (Cortalim-Ferry to Sao\nMartiasVidhanParisad)\n17 Goa\n10 NW 68 Mandovi River (Usgoan bridge to Arabian Sea) 41 Goa\n11 NW 86 Rupnarayan River (Pratap Pur to Geonkhali) 72 West\nBengal\n12 NW 97 Sunderbans Wateruay (Namkhana to\nAtharaBanki Khal)\n172 West\nBenqal\nNW 111 Zuari River (Sanvorden bridge to Marmugao\nPort)\n50 Goa\n14 NW 40 Ghaqra River (Faizabad to ManjhiGhat) 345 Bihar & U.P.\n15 NW 1O\nAmba River (Arabian Sea, Dharamtaar Creek to\nNagothane ST Stand)\n45 Maharashtra\nto NW 44 lchamati River (Bridge at Gobra to Bansjhari\nnear Bangladesh Border)\n63 West\nBenqal\n17 NW 52 Kali River (Kodasalli Dam to Sadashivgad\nBridqe, Arabian Sea)\nE1\nKarnataka\n1B NW 57 Kopili River (Banthai Gaon Tinali Bus Stop to\nChandrapur No.2 confluence with Brahmaputra)\n5U Assam\n'19 NW 25 Chapora River (Bridge near Maneri village to\nMoriim, Arabian Sea)\n25 Goa\n20 NW 37 Gandak River (Bhaisalotal Barrage to Hazipur) 296 Bihar & U.P.\n21 NW 28 Dabhol Creek Vasisti River (Arabian Sea at\nDabhol to Bridqe at Pedhe)\n45 Maharashtra\n22 NW 73 Narmada River (Pandhariya to Gulf of\nKhambhat)\n226 Maharashtra\n& Gu jarat\nZJ NW 85 Revadanda Creek - Kundalika River System 31 Maharashtra\n\n.. J-\n(Arabian Sea at Revadanda to Bridge near Roha\n24 NW 94 Sone River (Sone Barrage, Dehri to Confluence\nwith the Ganqa)\n141 Bihar\n25 NW 1OO Tapi River (Hatnur Dam to Gulf of Khambhat) 436 Maharashtra\n& Guiarat\n26 NW 31 Dhansrri River (Morongi T.E. village Bridge to\nN u maliqarh)\n110 Assam\n\n* tl -\nAnnex.ll\nLIST OF 14 NATIONAL WATERWAYS (NWs)\n' ON WHICH DEVELOPMENT ACTIVITIES TAKEN UP\nst.\nNo NW Details of Waterways\nLength\n(Km) STATES\n1 NWl Ganga-Bhagirathi-Hooghly River System (Haldia\n- Allahabad)\n1620 Uttar\nPradesh,\nBihar,\nJharkhand &\nWest Bengal\n2 NW2 Brahmaputra River (Dhubri - Sadiya) 891 Assam\n3 NW,16 Barak River (Lakhipur -Tuker Gram) 121 Assam\n4 NW3 West Coast Canal (Kottapuram - Kollam),\nChampakara and Udyogmandal Canals\n205\nKerala\nE NW4 Krishna River (Vilayawada - Muktyala) 82 Andhra\nPradesh\n6 NW5 Dhamra-Paradip via Mangalagadi to Pankopal a.r a Odisha\n7 NW8 Alappuzha- Chanqanassery Canal 29 Kerala\n8 NW9 Alappuzha - Kottayam - Athirampuzha Canal\n40 Kerala\n(Alternate\nroute:\n11.Skm)\no NW 27 Cumberjua River (Cortalim-Ferry to Sao\nMartiasVidhanParisad)\n17 Goa\n10 NW 68 Mandovi River (Usgoan bridge to Arabian Sea) 41 Goa\n11 NW 86 Rupnarayan River (Pratap Pur to Geonkhali) 72 West Bengal\n12 NW 97 Sunderbans Wateruay (Namkhana to\nAtharaBanki Khal)\n172 West Bengal\n13 NW 111 Zuari River (Sanvorden bridge to Marmugao Port) 50 Goa\n14 NW 40 Ghagra River (Faizabad to ManjhiGhat) 345 Bihar & U.P."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |564", "t": "Anganwadi Centres in Jharkhand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 564 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022 \n  \nANGANWADI CENTRES IN JHARKHAND \n  \n564.  SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  the number of Anganwadi centres in Jharkhand and children enrolled in these \ncentres, district-wise; \n(b)  the remuneration paid to Anganwadi Workers and Anganwadi Helpers along with \nthe proposed increase therein in view of rising pr ices and meagre salaries being \npaid to them, State/UT-wise including Jharkhand; \n(c)  the number of Anganwadi centres having pucca buildings, tapped water supply \nand toilets, State/UT-wise particularly in Jharkhand; \n(d)  the quantity of rice, pulses, cereal s, oil, vegetables, sugar, iodised salt etc. \nprescribed for each child, State/UT-wise including Jharkhand; and \n(e)  the scale and quality of food supplied to each child enrolled in Anganwadi \ncentres along with the steps taken to ensure that good quality fo od is supplied to \nthem, State/UT-wise including Jharkhand? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)   As reported by the State, the district -wise number of Anganwadi Centres  and \nchildren enrolled therein in the State of Jharkhand is at Annexure-I. \n  \n(b)   With respect to honorarium of Anganwadi Workers (AWWs) and Anganwadi \nHelpers (AWHs), Government of India w.e.f. 01.10.2018 has enhanced the honorarium \nof AWWs at main -AWCs f rom ₹3,000/ - to₹ 4,500/ - per month; AWWs at mini -AWCs \nfrom ₹2,250/- to ₹3,500/- per month; AWHs from ₹1,500/ - to ₹2,250/- per month; and \nintroduced performance linked incentive of ₹250/ - per month to AWHs.   Further, \nmodified guidelines have been issued for  performance linked incentive @ ₹ 500/ - per \nmonth to Anganwadi Workers w.e.f. 01.04.2021. In addition, States/UTs are also paying \nadditional monetary incentives/honorarium to these functionaries from their own \nresources. \n \n(c)  State/UT-wise number of AWCs having pucca buildings, drinking water facilities \nand toilet facilities is at Annexure-II. \n\n \n(d)  The Government of India allocates food-grains i.e. Wheat/Fortified Rice/Millets to \nState Governments/UT Administrations at subsidized rates under NFSA for preparation \nof supplementary food under Anganwadi Services. The recipe for the beneficiaries \nunder Supple mentary Nutrition is according to the local food habits and food items \neasily available in the State/UT.  \n \n(e)  Supplementary Nutrition provided to beneficiaries as per nutritional standards \nmentioned in Schedule II of National Food Security Act, 2013, which are as follows:  \n  \nS. No. Category Type of Meal Calories Protein \ni.   Children (6 Months to 3 \nYears) \nTake Home Ration  500 12-15 \nii.   Children (3 to 6 Years) Morning Snack and Hot \nCooked Meal \n500 12-15 \niii.   Children (6 Months to 6 \nYears) who are \nmalnourished \nTake Home Ration 800 20-25 \niv.   Pregnant women and \nLactating mothers \nTake Home Ration 600 18-20 \n  \nTo ensure that good quality food is supplied to beneficiaries by State \nGovernments/UT Administrations, Government of India has issued Streamlined \nGuidelines dated 13.01.2021 on quality assurance, roles and responsibilities of duty \nholders, procedure for procurement, integrating AYUSH concepts and data \nmanagement and monitoring through „Poshan Tracker‟ for transparency, efficiency and \naccountability in delivery  of Supplementary Nutrition. As per these guidelines, State \nGovernments/UT Administrations shall ensure that the quality of Supplementary \nNutrition being provided is with reference to the norms of food safety as well as nutrient \ncomposition. Supplementary Nutrition must conform to prescribed standards laid down \nunder the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 and regulations made there -under to \nensure consistent quality and nutritive value per serving.  \n  \nTake-Home Ration (not raw ration), shall be tested from  FSSAI \nowned/registered/empaneled/NABL accredited laboratory. Random testing must be \nconducted by Anganwadi Services functionaries after receipt of stock at the AWC or at \nthe Block level. Anganwadi Services functionaries i.e. CDPO or Supervisor shall draw \nthe samples, as per the prescribed procedure and send the sample for testing to a \nFSSAI owned/registered/empaneled/NABL accredited laboratory. \n  \nln case of Hot Cooked Meal, it should be ensured that it is prepared in proper \nkitchen sheds having adequate sa nitation and safe drinking water so as to maintain \nhygienic conditions.  \n  \nState/UT wise allocation of food grains for the financial year 2022 -23 (upto 3 rd \nQuarter) is at Annexure-III. \n****** \n  \n\n  \n  \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure Referred In Reply to Part (a) of Lok S abha Unstarred Question No.564 for \nanswer on 09.12.2022 raised by Shri Chandra Prakash Choudhary regarding \n“Anganwadi Centres in Jharkhand” Indicating District -wise Number of Anganwadi \nCentr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |565", "t": "Medicinal Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 565 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09TH DECEMBER 2022 \n \nMEDICINAL PLANTS \n \n565. SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA : \nSHRI SUNIL BABURAO MENDHE : \n \n \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of depleting medicinal plant resources and if so, \nthe reasons therefor and the steps taken by the Government to address this \nproblem; \n(b) the quantity of Medicinal plants and herbs produced in the country particularly in \nMaharashtra during the last three years; \n(c) the financial assistance provided to promote the farming of Medicinal plants and \nherbs during the said period, State/UT-wise; \n(d) the quantity of Medicinal plants exported from Maharashtra during the said \nperiod; \n(e) the steps ta ken to promote production of Ayurvedic and herbal medicines in the \ncountry particularly in Maharashtra; \n(f) whether the Government encourages/proposes to encourage the farmers for \ncultivation of Medicinal plants under any scheme, if so, the details thereof; and \n(g) the steps taken by the Government to promote the emerging nutraceutical \nindustry? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) Yes Sir. The Government is aware of depleting medicinal plant resources  in the country. \nThe reasons for depletion of medicinal plant resources are unsustainable collection / \nharvesting of these resources from the wild and destruction of their natural habitat. \nTo address this problem, the Ministry of Ayush, Government of India under its \n“Central Sector Scheme on Conservation, Development and Sustainable Management of \nMedicinal Plants” is providing project based support to State Forest Department for in-\n\nsitu cons ervation by way of establishing Medicinal Plant Conservation Development \nAreas (MPCDAs) and Resource Augmentation by way of plantation in Forest areas and \nex-situ plantation outside designated forest areas. \n \n(b) Ministry of Ayush, Government of India had implemented “Centrally Sponsored \nScheme” of National Ayush Mission (NAM) to promote the cultivation of medicinal \nplants since 2015 -16 to 2020 -21. Under „Medicinal Plants‟ component of the National \nAyush Mission (NAM)  Scheme, market driven cultivation of 140 prioritized medicinal \nplants in identified clusters/zones was supported and implemented in a mission mode \nthrough selected State Implementing Agencies throughout  the country including the \nState of Maharashtra. As per the scheme guidelines, the support was provided for: \n(i) Cultivation of prioritized medicinal plants on farmer‟s land. \n(ii) Establishment of nurseries with backward linkages for raising and \nsupply of Quality Planting Material. \n(iii) Post-Harvest Management with forward linkages. \n(iv) Primary processing, marketing infrastructure etc. \n \n During last three year, under „Medicinal Plants‟ component of National Ayush Mission \n(NAM), the Ministry of Ayush  had supported 15,150 hectare area for cultivation of \nmedicinal plants throughout the country including State of Maharashtra. The detail is \ngiven at Annexure – I. \n \n(c) The Ministry of Ayush had provided an amount of Rs. 4181.45 lakh to promote the \nfarming of medicinal plants and herbs under „Medicinal Plants‟ component of National \nAyush Mission (NAM) scheme throughout the c ountry. The state -wise and year -wise \ndetail of fund approved/released for cultivation of medicinal plants under National Ayush \nMission (NAM) scheme is given at Annexure – II. \n \n \n(d) As per data received from Directorate General of Commercial Intelligence & Statistics  \n(DGCIS) under Ministry of Commerce & Industry, the State -wise export data are \ncompiled by DGCIS on the basis of State Codes reported by the exporters in the shipping \n\nbills with the custom authority.  The export of Ayush and herbal products from \nMaharashtra during last three years is at Annexure – III. \n \n(e) Ministry of Ayush has implemented Central Sector Scheme AYUSH Oushadhi  Gunvatta \nEvam Uttpadan Samvardhan Yojana (AOGUSY) including Maharashtra. The objectives \nof the Scheme are as under : \ni. To enhance India's manufacturing capabilities and exports of traditional \nmedicines and health promotion products under the initiative of \nAtmanirbhar Bharat. \nii. To facilitate ad equate infrastructural & technological upgradation and \ninstitutional activities in public and private sector for standardization, \nquality manufacturing and analytical testing of Ayush drugs & \nmaterials. \niii. To strengthen regulatory frameworks at Central and St ate level for \neffective quality control, safety monitoring and surveillance of \nmisleading advertisements of Ayush drugs. \niv. To encourage building up synergies, collaborations and convergent \napproaches for promoting standards and quality of Ayush drugs & \nmaterials. \n \nThe components of the AYUSH Oushadhi  Gunvatta Evam Uttpadan Samvardhan \nYojana (AOGUSY) are as under : \nA. Strengthening and up -gradation of Ayush Pharmaci"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |566", "t": "Students Returning to Ukraine", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 566\nTO BE ANSWERED 09.12.2022\nSTUDENTS RETURNING TO UKRAINE\n566. SHRI DAYANIDHI MARAN:\nWill the Minister of External Affairs be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the students who were pursuing medical education in Ukraine\nare  returning  to  Ukraine  for  the  completion  of  their  education  despite\nadvisories warning and if so, the details thereof;\n(b  the  details  of  the  steps  taken  by  the  Government  to  support  these\nstudents on their return to India;\n(c)  whether  the  Government  has  taken  note  of  the  reports  that  these\nstudents had to return to Ukraine are doing so at the risk of their safety since\nthey did not get adequate support to complete their education in India;\n(d)  if so, the steps are being taken by the Government to provide support\nand assure the safety of the Indian students in Ukraine; and\n(e)  the  manner  in  which  the  Government  would  ensure  their  safety  and\nreturn to India in light of increasing hostilities?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(SMT. MEENAKSHI LEKHI)\n(a) Yes Sir . The Indian Mission in Kyiv issued an Advisory on 25 October 2022\nadvising all Indian citizens to immediately leave Ukraine by available means.\nWhile most of the Indian students pursuing medical education in Ukraine have\nreturned to India since the conflict began, around 1100 Indian students are\ncurrently in Ukraine.  \n1\n\n(b) & (c) The National Medical Commission (NMC), vide Public Notice dated\n28.07.22, devised a scheme under which the Indian students who were in the\nlast year of their undergraduate medicine course but  had to leave their foreign\nmedical institute and have subsequently completed their studies with certificate\nof completion of course/degree by their respective institute, on or before 30 th\nJune, 2022, shall be permitted to appear in the Foreign Medical Graduate (FMG)\nExamination. Thereafter , upon qualifying the FMG examination, such foreign\nmedical  graduates  are  required  to  undergo  Compulsory  Rotating  Medical\nInternship for a period of two years to make up for the clinical training which\ncould  not  be  physically  done  by  them  during  the  undergraduate  medicine\ncourse in the foreign institute  and also to familiarize  them with practice of\nmedicine under Indian conditions. \nThe NMC has issued Public Notices in September 2022 conveying its no-\nobjection to the Academic Mobility Program offered by Ukraine i.e. temporary\nrelocation (for the period of conflict) to some other universities, provided that\nother criteria of Screening Test Regulations 2002 are fulfilled. It is also clarified\nthat there were no restrictions on permanent transfer to universities in third\ncountries for medical students covered under Screening Test Regulations,2002.\nMedical Students were allowed to undergo online classes of theory subjects\nwhich should be supplemented by offline practical and clinical training done in\nthe Medical University and /or its affiliated Hospital during their MBBS course.\n  \n(d) & (e)  All Indian citizens in Ukraine have been advised by our Mission in\nKyiv to strictly follow the safety and security guidelines issued by the Ukrainian\nGovernment  and  local  authorities.  The   Mission,  in  its  Advisory,  had  also\nprovided  information  about  available  options  to  Indian  citizens  for  crossing\nborders and it remains in touch with our nationals in Ukraine to provide help,\nwhen required.\n*****\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |567", "t": "Conversion of PHCs into AB-HWCs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 567 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nCONVERSION OF PHCs INTO AB-HWCs \n \n 567. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the upgradation work carried out/proposed to be carried out with regard to the \nstaff, equipment, facilities and funding while transforming the Primary Health Centers an d the \nSub-Health centers into Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centers (AB-HWC); and \n(b) the number and percentage of Primary Health Centers and Sub -Health Centers converted into \nABHWCs in the State of Maharashtra till date? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY \nWELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a):   Under Ayushman Bharat, States/UTs are to transform existing 1,50,000 lakh sub Health \nCenters (SHCs) and rural & urban Primary Health Centers(PHCs) across the country as Ayushman \nBharat Health and Wellness Centers(AB-HWCs) by December 2022.  \nThe detailed norms with respect to the staff, equipment, facilities and funding while \ntransforming the PHCs and SHCs to AB -HWCs is provided to the states/UTs vide Operat ional \nGuidelines on Ayushman Bharat comprehensive Primaty Health care through Health and \nwellness centers issued in 2018. The link for the same is as follows: \n \nhttps://www.nhm.gov.in/New_Updates_2018/NHM_Components/Health_System_Stregthening/\nComprehensive_primary_health_care/letter/Operational_Guidelines_For_CPHC.pdf  \n \n(b): As per the data updated by states on AB-HWC portal , a s on 30 th Nov 2022,  8,32 7 \n(78.02%) SHCs , 1,864 ( 101.36%) PHCs and 58 3 (89.55%) UPHCs in the state have been \noperationalized into Health & Wellness Centre.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |568", "t": "Funds for National Nutrition Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF WOMEN & CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 568 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022 \n  \nFUNDS FOR NATIONAL NUTRITION MISSION \n  \n  \n568.  SHRI CHANDESHWAR PRASAD: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of funds released to the States particularly in Bihar under the National \nNutrition Mission during the last three years, State-wise; \n(b)  the names of the districts of the State identified under the said Mission along with \nthe details of the campaign for publicity and awareness of the same; \n(c)  whether any training programme is being conducted towards ensuring smooth \noperation of the scheme; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a)  The State/UT wise details of funds released under POSHAN Abhiyaan during the \nlast three years including the state of Bihar is at Annexure-I. \n  \n(b)  All the districts of Bihar are covered under the Abhiyaan. The details are \nannexed at Annexure II.   \n  \nWith respect to publicity and awareness, an important focus under Poshan \nAbhiyan has been on ‘Behaviour Change Communication’ for greater awareness on \ngood health and nutrition practices and to address inadequate and inappropriate infant \nand young child feeding practices, nutrition and care during pregnancy and \nadolescence. In order to ensure community mobilization and bolster people’s \nparticipation, every year, the month of September is celebrated as POSHAN Maah and \nthe month of March is celebrated as Poshan Pakhwada across the country. Over the \nyears, themes have included overall nutrition, hygiene, water and sanitation, anemia \nprevention, importance of b reast-feeding, growth monitoring, role of Poshan \nPanchayats, AYUSH for Well -being, ‘Back to Basics – Yoga for Health’, importance of \n\nPoshan Vatikas for cultivation of local vegetables, medicinal plants/herbs and fruits at \nthe community level, Poshan ke Paanch Sutra etc. \n  \nIn the state of Bihar, during Poshan Maah, 2022, around 1.73 crore activities \nwere conducted, whereas, during Poshan Pakhwada 2022, around 31.56 lakh activities \nwere conducted across various themes. \n  \n(c) & (d)  Capacity building of Anganwadi Workers and other front line functionaries \nis one of the core activities under POSHAN Abhiyaan. Till date, approximately 10.22 \nlakh ICDS functionaries have been trained across the country. Capacity building is also \nconducted through digital platform. A pproximately, 6.61 lakh field functionaries have \nundergone training through digital platform. Further, all the Anganwadi workers of the \nState have been trained in different modules under the capacity building component of \nPOSHAN Abhiyaan. Some of the key modules, include identification and care of a weak \nnewborn baby, complementary feeding, diet diversity, preventing anemia in women, \nassessment of growth in children, ensuring exclusive breastfeeding, supporting mothers \nwith issues in breastfeeding etc \n  \n******* \n  \n  \n       \n  \n\n Annexure- I \n \nState-wise statement of funds released under POSHAN Abhiyaan (as on \n31.03.2022) \n          Amount in lakhs \nSl. States/UTs \nCentral Funds \nreleased in FY 2019-\n20 \nCentral Funds \nreleased in FY 2020-\n21 \nCentral Funds released \nin FY 2021-22 \n1 A & N Islands  307.62 100 518.98 \n2 Andhra Pradesh  13296.52 889.71 0 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1006.62 99.6 0 \n4 Assam  14171 987.04 0 \n5 Bihar 25465 1835.48 0 \n6 Chandigarh 353 107.2 122.66 \n7 Chhattisgarh  0 839.58 0 \n8 D & 