thouse\n3. Jaigarh Lighthouse\n4. Ratnagiri Lighthouse\n5. Vengurla Point Lighthouse\nMaharashtra\n1. Oyster Rock Lighthouse\n2. Tadri Lighthouse\n3. Bhatkal Lighthouse\n4. Kaup Lighthouse\n5. Surathkal Lighthouse\nKarnataka\n1. Gopalpur Lighthouse\n2. Puri Lighthouse\n3. Chandrabhaga Lighthouse\n4. Paradip Lighthouse\n5. False Point Lighthouse\n1. Tajpur Lighthouse\n2. Dariyapur Lighthouse\n3. Sagar lsland Lighthouse\nWest Bengal\nNorth Point Lighthouse\nPort Cornwallis Lighthouse\n1.\n2.\nAndaman &\nNicobar lslands\n1. Minicoy Lighthouse\n1. Aguada Lighthouse\nhas been droPPed due to securitY"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |623", "t": "Self Reliance in Manufacturing of Pharmaceuticals", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 623 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 9TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nSelf Reliance in Manufacturing of Pharmaceuticals  \n \n623. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps to make India self -reliant in Active \nPharmaceutical Ingredients (APIs), Drug Intermediates (DIs) and Key Starting Materials \n(KSMs);  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n                                       (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n \n(a) to (c):   Yes, Sir. In order to make the country self-reliant and to reduce import dependence \nin Active Pharmaceutical Ingredients (APIs), Drug Intermediates (DIs) and Key Starting \nMaterials (KSMs), the Department of Pharmaceuticals, amongst other, is implementing the \nfollowing three schemes:  \n \n(i) Production Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for promotion of domestic  \nmanufacturing of critical Key Starting Materials (KSMs)/ Drug Intermediates (DIs) and \nActive Pharmaceutical Ingredients (APIs) in India, with a financial outlay of Rs. 6,940 \ncrores and the tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2029-30, provides for financial incentive \nfor 41 identified products. A total of 51 applications have been selected under the scheme. \nOut of these, 21 projects have already been commissioned with the installed capacity of \n33,895 MT. \n \n(ii) Production Linked Incentive Scheme for Pharmaceuticals, with a financial outlay Rs. \n15,000 crores and the tenure from FY 2020 - 2021 to FY 2028 -29, provides for financial \nincentive to 55 selected applicants for manufacturing of identified products under three \ncategories for a period of six years.  The eligible drugs under this scheme include APIs \namong other categories of pharmaceutical products. A total of about 1,666 products have \nbeen approved for the selected 55 applicants under the scheme. \n \n(iii)Scheme for Promotion of Bulk Drug Parks, with a financial outlay of Rs. 3,000  crores \nand the tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2024-25, provides for financial assistance to three \nStates for establishing Bulk Drug Parks. After evaluation of the proposals received from 13 \nStates, the proposals of the states of Gujarat, Himachal Pradesh and Andhra Pradesh have \nbeen finally approved for providing grants -in-aid for creation of common infrastructure \nfacilities in the proposed Bulk Drug Parks.  \n  \nxxxxx "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |624", "t": "Disposal of Cases through Video Conferencing", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \n(DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. †624 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 09thDECEMBER, 2022 \n \nDisposal of Cases through Video Conferencing \n \n†624. SHRIMATI  DELKARKALABEN MOHANBHAI: \n \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of High Courts/District Courts where the \nproceedings are being conducted through video conferencing, \nState/UT-wise; \n(b) the details of cases disposed by courts through video \nconferencing since its inception upto September , 2022, \nState/UT and court-wise; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to allocate  additional funds \nfor live streaming of the proceedings of district/High Co urts; \nand \n(d) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE \n (SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a): In the following High Courts  along with the District Courts within their \njurisdiction proceedings are being conducted using video conferencing:  \nAllahabad,Andhra Pradesh , Bombay, Calcutta, Chhattisgarh,  Delhi, \nGauhati –(Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Mizoram, Nagaland), Gujarat,  \nHimanchal Pradesh, Jammu and Kashmir, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Kerala, \nMadhya Pradesh,  Madras, Mani pur, Meghalaya, Orissa, Patna,  Punjab \nand Haryana, Rajasthan, Sikkim, Telangana, Tripura, Uttarakhand. \n(b):Number of cases deal t by High Courts and District Courts through \nvideoconferencing upto September 2022is attached at Annexure. \n\n2 \n \n \n \n(c)and (d):eCourts Phase -II is coming to end and phase -III DPR has been \napproved by eCommittee, Supreme Court of India . Provision of funds for Live \nStreaming has been incorporated in DPR of phase III. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3 \n \n \nAnnexure \nStatement referred to in reply of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.†624 for 09/12/2022 \nregarding disposal of cases through video conferencing. The details of proceedings conducted by \ndifferent courts through video conferencing upto September 2022 are as under: \nNumber of cases dealt with (virtual hearings) on video conferencing in High Courts and District \nCourts during the pandemic as on 30 September 2022 \nS.No High Court High Court  District Court  Total  \n1 Allahabad 241321 3639634 3880955 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 380252 1411741 1791993 \n3 Bombay 36849 59084 95933 \n4 Calcutta 135598 79549 215147 \n5 Chhattisgarh 103007 36358 139365 \n6 Delhi 317729 3210946 3528675 \n7 Gauhati - Arunachal Pradesh 2291 8128 10419 \n8 Gauhati - Assam 242335 309766 552101 \n9 Gauhati - Mizoram 3963 13268 17231 \n10 Gauhati - Nagaland 930 650 1580 \n11 Gujarat 388928 191558 580486 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 183904 81755 265659 \n13 \nCommon High Court for Union \nTerritory of Jammu and Kashmir \nand Union Territory of Ladakh 257102 408713 665815 \n14 Jharkhand 218022 635528 853550 \n15 Karnataka 1003946 117422 1121368 \n16 Kerala 158150 520123 678273 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 664985 747388 1412373 \n18 Madras 1424292 327922 1752214 \n19 Manipur 38695 15288 53983 \n20 Meghalaya 2466 21377 23843 \n21 Orissa 276241 234567 510808 \n22 Patna 259819 1991876 2251695 \n23 Punjab and Haryana 585412 792754 1378166 \n24 Rajasthan 232770 217746 450516 \n25 Sikkim 474 56053 56527 \n26 Telangana 299859 190600 490459 \n27 Tripura 10563 11994 22557 \n\n4 \n \n28 Uttarakhand 73686 41151 114837 \nTotal 7543589 15372939 22916528 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |625", "t": "Jan Aushadhi Kendras in West Bengal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 625 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9th December, 2022 \n \nJan Aushadhi Kendras in West Bengal \n \n625. SHRI DILIP GHOSH:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the features and guidelines for setting up Jan Aushadhi Kendras under Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya \nJan Aushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJAP) scheme;  \n(b) the present status of Jan Aushadhi Kendras set up to provide medicines at affordable price in the \nState of West Bengal and the details of such centres including in rural backward areas, district-wise;  \n(c) the details of funds sanctioned, allocated and utilized under PMBJAP so far in West Bengal;  \n(d) the details of the number of medicines being provided under such kendras; and  \n(e) whether the Government proposes to set up more such centres in West Bengal, if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)                                \n \n(a): Under the Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) implemented by the \nPharmaceutical and Medical Devices Bureau of India (PMBI), a registered society under the aegis of \nthe Department of Pharmaceuticals, an individual having D. Pharma/B. Pharma degree holder himself \nor any individual/organization who has employed D. Pharma/B. Pharma degree holder for getting \ndrug license from concerned State Government, is eligible to open Pradhan Mantri Bh artiya \nJanaushadhi Kendra (PMBJK). The requirements for applying to open PMBJK are as under:  \ni. Minimum 120 sq. ft. space duly supported by proper documents. \nii. Registration details of the pharmacist with State Pharmacy Council. \niii. Applicants under category of wom en entrepreneurs, divyang, SC and ST have to submit \nsuitable certificate/proof from respective authorities along with undertaking. \n \nThe incentive provided to the Kendra owners under the scheme has been enhanced from Rs. \n2.50 lakh to up to Rs. 5.00 lakh to be given @ 15% of monthly purchases made, subject to a ceiling \nof Rs. 15,000/- per month. Further, one-time additional incentive of Rs. 2.00 lakh (as reimbursement \nfor IT & infrastructure expenditure) is provided to the PMBJP Kendras  opened in North -Eastern \nStates, Himalayan areas, Island territories and backward areas mentioned as aspirational district by \nNITI Aayog or opened by women entrepreneur, Divyang and SC & ST. \n \n(b): Till 30.11.2022, about 8916 PMBJKs have been opened across the country, of which 222 Kendras \nhave been opened in West Bengal. District-wise list of PMBJKs opened in the state of West Bengal \nis enclosed as Annexure. \n \n\n(c):  The details of the funds allocated and utilized under PMBJP in the entire country during the last \nthree years including current financial year 2022-23 is as under: - \n \nFinancial Year Funds \nSanctioned \n(Rs. In Cr.) \nFunds Allocated \n(Rs. In Cr.) \nFunds Utilized \n(Rs. In Cr.) \n2020-21 65.00 65.00 65.00 \n2021-22 68.50 68.50 68.50 \n2022-23 \n(Till 30.11.2022) \n72.50 \n \n54.37 \n(till 30.11.2022) \n36.68 \n(till 30.11.2022) \n \nNo State/UT-wise specific budget allocation made under the scheme \n \n(d): The product basket of PMBJP presently comprises of 1,759 medicines and 280 surgical devices \nwhich are available for sale through about 8,916 PMBJKs opened across the country. In addition, \nvarious nutraceutical and some AYUSH products have also been added in the product basket of the  \nPariyojana.  \n \n(e): Government has set a target of opening 10,500 PMBJKs by March 2025. The set target will be \nachieved by the PMBI for which efforts are being made to open PMBJKs at block level. Recently, \nPMBI has invited online applications from more tha n 3500 blocks of the country for opening of \nPMBJKs including 263 blocks of 22 districts of West Bengal.  \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nStatement referred to in part (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Q. No. 625 for answer on \n09.12.2022 raised by SHRI DILIP GHOSH regarding Jan Aushadhi Kendras in West Bengal  \n \nDistrict-wise list of PMBJKs opened in West Bengal till 30.11.2022 \nS. NO. Name of District Number of PMBJKs \n1 24 Paraganas North 36 \n2 24 Paraganas South 12 \n3 Alipurduar 5 \n4 Bankura 4 \n5 Birbhum 2 \n6 Coochbehar 10 \n7 Darjeeling 5 \n8 Dinajpur Dakshin 1 \n9 Dinajpur Uttar 8 \n10 Hooghly 26 \n11 Howrah 13 \n12 Jalpaiguri 9 \n13 Jhargram 1 \n14 Kalimpong 1 \n15 Kolkata 34 \n16 Maldah 5 \n17 Medinipur East 8 \n18 Medinipur West 5 \n19 Murshidabad 4 \n20 Nadia 7 \n21 Paschim Bardhaman 11 \n22 Purba Bardhaman 13 \n23 Purulia 2 \n Total  222 \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |626", "t": "Stipend to the MBBS Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 626 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 09TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nSTIPEND TO THE MBBS STUDENTS \n \n626  SHRI RAJESH VERMA: \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has issued any guideline for providing stipend to the students of \nMBBS internship in private medical colleges/institutes of the country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY \nWELFARE \n \n(DR BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (c) As informed by the National Medical Commission (NMC) , the Commission has  \nissued guidelines to all Directorates of Medical Education, State Medical Councils and \nuniversities regarding charge of internship fee and payment of stipend to interns in India. The \namount of stipend to be paid to interns is decided/ fixed by the appropr iate authority \napplicable to the institution/ university or State.  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |627", "t": "Revitalize the Traditional System of Medicine", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.627 \nANSWERED ON 9TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nREVITALIZE THE TRADITIONAL SYSTEM OF MEDICINE \n \n627.   SHRI DULAL CHAND GOSWAMI:  \n   \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) aims to revitalize the traditional system \nof medicine and AYUSH, so as to strengthen the public health system at all levels;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) the action plan formulated by the Government to revitalize traditional system of medicines \nand promote its practitioners? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) to (c): 'Public Health and Hospitals’ being a State subject the primary responsibility to ensure \nhealthcare services lies with the respective State Governments. However, under the National \nHealth Mission (NHM), Government of India provides technical and financial support to \nStates/UTs for strengthening their healthcare systems, including support for engagement of \ndoctors and for mainstreaming of AYUSH through co -located facilities, based on the \nrequirements posed by the States/UTs in their Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs) within \ntheir overall resource envelope.  \nUnder National Health Mission (NHM) of which National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) \nis one of the component, engagement of Ayurveda Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and \nHomoeopathy (AYUSH) doctors/ param edics is being supported, provided they are co -located \nwith existing District Hospitals (DHs), Community Health Centres (CHCs) and Primary Health \nCentres (PHCs) with priority given to remote PHCs and CHCs.   \nAYUSH doctors are also being engaged under Rasht riya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK). \nAyurveda practitioner, trained and certified through IGNOU/other State Public Health/Medical \nUniversities for a set of competencies in delivering public health amd primary care services are \nalso being engaged as Communit y Health Officer (CHO) at Ayushman Bharat Health & \nWellness Centre- Sub Centres (AB-HWC-SCs).  \n\n2 \n \nAs on June, 2022, over 27,606 AYUSH doctors and 4,337 AYUSH Paramedical Staff are \nsupported under NHM at over 14,309 functional co-located facilities under NHM \nFurther, North Eastern Institute of Ayurveda & Folk Medicine Research, located in \nPasighat, Arunachal Pradesh has been established with a mandate to revitalize and promote Folk \nHealing practices & Folk medicine. The institute is engaged in documentation of ethnomedicinal \npractices and scientific validation of folk claims. Furthermore, the institute is also trying to \ndevelop capacity building among the traditional healers and organizing awareness camps for \npopularizing the potentiality of folk healing practic es & folk medicine.  Quality Council of India \n(QCI) is certifying Traditional Community Healthcare Providers (TCHP) under Voluntary \nCertification Scheme for Traditional Community Healthcare Providers (VCSTCHP).  \nWith regard to  quality control mechanism s in teaching institutes  across the country the \nNational Commission for Indian System of Medicine (NCISM) Act, 2020 was enacted on \n21stSeptember, 2020, to replace the existing Indian Medicine Central Council  (IMCC) Act, 1970 \nto introduce competency based/o utcome based Course Curriculum, Sylla bus, Transitional \nCurriculum & U ser Manual for Under Graduate ( UG) Courses. Further, Central Council for \nResearch in Ayurvedic Sciences (CCRAS ), Central Council for Research in Unani Medicine  \n(CCRUM) and Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS)  are autonomous organizations \nunder Ministry of Ayush for  research including medicinal research in Ayurveda, Unani, and \nSiddha system of Medicine  respectively. CCRAS, CCRUM, CCRS are working towards t he \nscientific validation in the concerned system of m edicines through clinical research, drug \nresearch, medicinal plants research, fundamental research, literary research and documentation.  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |628", "t": "Cervical Cancer Vaccine", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 628 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9thDECEMBER, 2021 \n \nCERVICAL CANCER VACCINE \n \n628. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAYBHATT: \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes tomanufacture vaccine for cervical cancer across \ndifferent parts of the country; and \n(b) if so, the details of the steps taken/proposedto be taken by t he Government in this regard \nand ifnot, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND  \nFAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) & (b):The Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation(CDSCO) has granted permission \nfor manufacturing and marketing of Quadrivalent Human Papilloma Virus (Serotypes 6,11, \n16 & 18) Vaccine (Recombinant) (qHPV) to M/s Serum Institiute of India Pvt. Ltd., Pune for \nprevention of the disease caused by Human Papilloma Virus types 6, 11, 16 and 18. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |63", "t": "Textile Park", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n��� ������ �\n�\n����������������������� �\n������������ �����������\n�\n��������������������\n�\n����� ���� ������������ �� ������ ��\n� ���������������������� ��\n�\n����������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ���� ������ ������� ��� �������� ������� ������������� ��� ���� �������� ������ ����� ���� ������� ���\n����������������������������������������� �����\n�\n������ �������� ���� ����������� ���� ���� ��������� ��� ���� ���������� ������������� ��� ���� ��������\n�������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n��������������������� �\n������������������������� �\n�\n������������ ������������������������������������������������\n�\n������\n� �\n\n���������� � �������� � �� ��� � ����� ��� ������ �� �� ��� ���� ��� ���� ������\n�������� ��������� ������ ���� ������� ��� ����������� ���������� �������\n������������� ������ ��� ��������� �������� �� ������ ���� ����� ������\n����������� ��������������������������\n�\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������� ����������� ��������� ������������ ������� �������� ������������ ������� ����������� ���\n������� �������� ��������� ������������� ������� ���������������� ���� ������� ��������� ����\n������������\n�\n������������ ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������\n�\n����� ���� ��������� ������������������� ������� ��� ���������� ������������������������������ ����\n���������������������\n�\n����� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������\n�\n� �\n\n�������� ���\n�\n��������� �� ���������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ����� �� �� ��� ���� ���� ������\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |630", "t": "Indian Investments in Afghanistan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.630\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022\nINDIAN INVESTMENTS IN AFGHANISTAN\n630. KUNWAR DANISH ALI:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) the investments made by the Government of India in Afghanistan during the\nlast three years, project-wise;\n(b) whether the India-Afghanistan strategic partnership agreement was signed in\nthe year 2011 between India and Afghanistan, if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the strategy proposed to be followed in future in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN)\n(a) to (c)  India has been engaged in a development partnership with Afghanistan,\nwhich  includes  more  than  five  hundred  projects  spread  across  each  of  the  34\nprovinces of the country in critical areas of power , water supply, road connectivity,\nhealthcare,  education,  agriculture  and  capacity  building.  The  vast  majority  of  the\nprojects  committed  to  by  the  Government  of  India  in  Afghanistan  have  been\ncompleted and handed over . \nIndia and Afghanistan signed the Strategic Partnership Agreement in October\n2011. The Strategic Partnership Agreement (SPA) between the two sides, provides for\nassistance to help rebuild Afghanistan’s infrastructure and institutions, education and\ntechnical  assistance  to  re-build  indigenous  Afghan  capacity  in  different  areas,\nencouraging investment in Afghanistan’s natural resources and providing duty free\naccess to the Indian market for Afghanistan’s exports.\nIndia  has  historical  and  civilizational  ties  with  Afghanistan.  Our  special\nrelationship with the Afghan people and the UNSC Resolution 2593 would continue to\nguide India’s approach on Afghanistan.  We remain steadfast in our commitment to\nprovide humanitarian assistance to the people of Afghanistan. In this endeavour , we\nhave already dispatched several shipments of humanitarian assistance consisting of\n40,000 MTs of wheat, 50 tons of medicines, 500,000 doses of COVID Vaccine, winter\nclothing and 28 tons of disaster relief assistance.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |631", "t": "Dialogue between Practitioners of Different Medical Treatment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH   \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 631 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022 \n \nDIALOGUE BETWEEN PRACTITIONERS OF DIFFERENT MEDICAL TREATMENT  \n631.   SHRI  RAJENDRA  DHEDYA  GAVIT ;  \n \n          Will the Minister of AYUSH  be pleased to state: \n (a)    whether Government has ever taken any concrete steps to  organize long -term dialogue \nbetween the practitioners of  different systems of medical treatment to lay a path ahead to  \neffectively treat patients suffering with diseases  of serious  nature and if  so, the details \nthereof; \n \n    (b) if not, whether the Government proposes to organize such dialogue with a view to find out \neffective solutions to treat illnesses; and \n \n    (c)      whether Government proposes to set a high-level working group of medical experts and \nrepresentatives of premier research institutions belonging to different systems of \n              medicinal treatment; and  \n \n  (d)     if so, the details thereof ? \n   ANSWER \n         THE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n         (SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) & (b)   Yes, Sir.   All the five Research Councils {Central Council for Research in Ayurvedic \nSciences (CCRAS); Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS); Central Council for \nResearch  in Unani Medicines (CCRUM); Central Council for  Research in Homoeopathy \n(CCRH); and Central Council for Research in Yoga and Naturopathy (CCRYN)}  functioning \nunder the aegis of Ministry of A yush, since their inception, have been undertaking clinical \nresearch studies involving domain experts from modern medicine in the development of research \nprotocols, consultations during the implementation of the study, follow-up of patients enrolled in \nthe studies, as and when required.   Few examples wherein experts from modern medicine were \ninvolved in research studies , are acute encephalitis, cancer, COVID -19, diabetes and its \ncomplications, hypertension, dyslipidemia, trigeminal neuralgia, haemohpilia, acute otitis media, \ndengue, depression, schizophrenia, chronic kidney disease etc.  \n        The Ministry of Ayush has also constituted an inter -disciplinary Ayush Research & \nDevelopment Task Force for initiating, coordinating and monitoring the R&D activities in the \nAyush Sector related to  SARS-COV-2 virus  and COVID-19 disease.  This Task Force consists  \n \nContd…2/-… \n\n-:       2       :- \n \nof eminent scientists,  medical professionals, members and representatives from CSIR, ICMR, \nDBT, Traditional Medicine Unit (WHO), UGC, AIIMS, AIIA, CCRAS, CCRS, CCRUM, \nCCRH and NMPB. The Task Force has formulated guidelines  for Ayush  clinical and \nobservational studies in COVID-19, covering various aspects of clinical trial protocols.  \n                \n(c) & (d)  A high-level working group of Secretaries of Science Research \nDepartments/Ministries, has been formed and  constituted  by the Government very recently in \nAugust, 2022 to ensure better synergy  and increased coordination of the projects for Ayush \nresearch.   This Working Group, namely “National Ayush Inter -Ministerial Research Committee \n(NAIMRC)”, under Chairmanship of Secretary, Ministry of Ayush, has following Members: \n \n(i) Secretary (DST), Department of Science and Technology; \n(ii) Secretary (DBT), Department of Biotechnology; \n(iii) Secretary (DSIR), Department of Scientific and Industrial Research; \n(iv) Secretary (DPIIT), Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade; \n(v) Secretary (DoC), Department of Commerce; \n(vi) Secretary (MoEF&CC), Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change; and  \n(vii) Deputy Director General (Dr. N . Srikanth), Central Council for Research in \nAyurvedic Sciences (CCRAS), as Member Secretary.  \n \n         In addition, a ll the Research Councils have set -up different scientific bodies such as \nScientific Advisory Board, Special Comm ittee for Clinical Research, Sp ecial Committee for \nFundamental Research, Special Committee for Drug Providing and Special Committee for Drug \nStandardization, and Institutional Ethics Committees for authenticating the research protocols \ndeveloped for undertaking research studies.   \n            The experts/members in these Committees guide in the development of research \nproposals.  \n \n…………….. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |632", "t": "Fishermen Harassed by Sri Lankan Personnel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 632\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.12.2022\nFISHERMEN HARASSED BY SRI LANKAN PERSONNEL\n632. MS. S. JOTHIMANI:\n         SHRI S. VENKATESAN:\n         SHRI ADHIR RANJAN CHOWDHURY:\n         DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI \n         THANGAPANDIAN:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether  the  Government  is  aware  of  the  increasing  incidents  of  Indian\nfishermen being apprehended/harassed and their fishing vessels being seized by the\nSri Lankan Navy/authorities during the last three years and the current year;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of Indian fishermen particularly from\nTamil  Nadu  in  captivity  of  Sri  Lankan  Navy/authorities  along  with  the   boats\nseized/destroyed  during  the  said  period,  year-wise  indicating  losses  incurred  by\nfishermen as a result thereof;\n(c) the number of Indian fishermen shot by Sri Lankan personnel during the said\nperiod;\n(d) whether any steps are being taken by the Government to secure the release of\nthe captured fishermen and their fishing boats/vessels;\n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the diplomatic initiatives/steps taken by the\nGovernment to prevent such instances in future and check harassment of Indian\nfishermen; and \n(f) the  financial  assistance  provided  to  the  dependents/families  of  these\nfishermen during the said period?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN)\n(a) Indian fishermen are arrested from time to time by the Sri Lankan authorities\nfor allegedly crossing the International Maritime Boundary Line (IMBL) and fishing in\nSri Lankan waters.\n1\n\n(b) Details are given in the table below:\nYear Number  of\nFishermen  (from\nTamil  Nadu)\narrested\nNumber  of\nFishermen\n(from  Tamil\nNadu) released\nNumber  of\nFishing  boats\n(from  Tamil\nNadu) seized\nNumber  of\nFishing  boats\n(from  Tamil\nNadu) released\n2019 190 All released 39 1\n2020 74 All released 11 1\n2021 143 All released 19 4\n2022 219 198 released 30 Nil\n(c) As per available information, in August 2021, there was an injury to one Indian\nfisherman due to alleged firing by Sri Lankan Navy.  \n(d) to (f) Our Consular officials visit the apprehended Indian fishermen to seek\ntheir welfare and provide them legal assistance along with food and other essential\nitems.  \nIssues relating to Indian fishermen have been raised at the highest levels,\nincluding by our Prime Minister with the Sri Lankan Prime Minister during the Virtual\nBilateral Summit held in September 2020.  In 2021, during his visit to Colombo from\n5-7 January, External Affairs Minister (EAM) had met the Minister of Fisheries of Sri\nLanka and discussed all issues related to Indian fishermen.  On 15 January 2022, EAM\ndiscussed the issue of early release of Indian fishermen in Sri Lankan custody, during\nhis virtual meeting with Finance Minister of Sri Lanka.  The matter was also discussed\nby EAM with the Sri Lankan Minister of Fisheries, during his visit to Sri Lanka in March\n2022.\nGovernment of India attaches the highest priority to the safety, security and\nwelfare of Indian fishermen. Following the 2+2 initiative in November 2016, when the\nForeign and Fisheries Ministers of the two countries met in New Delhi, a bilateral\nJoint Working Group (JWG) mechanism and meeting of the Ministers for Fisheries of\nthe  two  countries  was  institutionalized  to  address  the  fishermen  issues  with  Sri\nLanka.  On  25th March  2022,  5 th Round  of  JWG  was  organized  between  the  two\ngovernments where the entire gamut of issues related to fishermen was discussed.\n*****\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |633", "t": "Quality of Drugs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 633 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 9TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nQuality of Drugs \n \n633.  SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \nSHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has received complaints from countries such as Vietnam, Sri \nLanka, Ghana, Mozambique and Nigeria over the quality of drugs being exported by the Indian \npharmaceutical industry to them;  \n(b) if so, the remedial measures taken to address these complaints;  \n(c) the list of measures taken/likely to be taken by the Government to prevent the export of \nadulterated drugs following the World Health Organisation’s alert; and  \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to improve the quality of drugs manufactured in India?\n   \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n                                       (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n \n(a):   As per information received from Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation \n(CDSCO) under the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare, isolated complaints on quality \nrelated issues of drugs exported by the Indian Pharmaceutical companies have been received \nfrom some foreign countries like Vietnam and Sri Lanka.  \n \n(b) to (d):    As and when complaints are received by CDSCO, the same are investigated in \nconsultation with the State Licensing Authorities (SLAs). Based on the investigations, SLAs \ntake suitable action unde r the provisions of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 & Rules. In \ncase of manufacturing drugs for exports, the manufacturers are required to obtain license from \nthe concerned State Licensing Authority (SLA) and the same are required to be manufactured \nin accordance to the conditions of license under the provisions of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, \n1940 and Rules made thereunder. Further, in case of drugs meant for export, the manufacturers \nare also required to meet the requirements of importing country. CDSCO and the Ministry of \nHealth and Family Welfare have taken various regulatory measures to ensure the quality of \nmedicines in the country. The major reforms are as under:  \n \n(i) On 17-11-2022, the Drugs Rules, 1945 were amended vide G.S.R.823(E) which would \ncome into force on 1st of August, 2023 providing that the manufacturers of drug formulation \nproducts as specified in Schedule H2 shall print or affix Bar Code or Quick Response Code on \nits primary packaging label or, in case of inadequate space in primary p ackage label, on the \nsecondary package label that store data or information legible with software application to \n\nfacilitate authentication. The stored data or information referred to in sub-rule (6) shall include \nthe following particulars, namely: \n \n(a) unique product identification code; \n(b) proper and generic name of the drug; \n            (c) brand name; \n(d) name and address of the manufacturer; \n(e) batch number; \n(f) date of manufacturing; \n(g) date of expiry; and \n(h) Manufacturing licence number. \n \n(ii) On 18.01.2022, the Drugs Rules, 1945 were amended vide G.S.R. 20 (E) providing that \nevery Active Pharmaceutical Ingredient (bulk drug) manufactured or imported in India shall \nbear Quick Response Code on its label at each level of packaging that store data or information \nreadable with software application to facilitate tracking and tracing. The stored data or \ninformation shall include the minimum particulars including Unique product identification \ncode, Batch No, MFG date, expiry date, etc. \n \n(iii) The number of sanctioned posts in CDSCO has been increased from 111 in 2008 to 478 \ntill January 2022 and 220 posts have recently been created on 27-06-2022. \n \n(iv) On 11.02.2020, the Drugs Rules, 1945 were amended vide G.S.R. 101 (E), providing \nthat w.e.f. 01.03.2021 any marketer who sells or distributes any drug shall be responsible for \nquality of that drug as well as other regulatory compliances along with the manufacturer under \nthese rules. \n \n(v) On 6.11.2019, the Drugs Rules, 1945 were amended vide Gazette notifica tion no. \nG.S.R.828(E), providing that in case the applicant intends to market the drug under a brand \nname or trade name, the applicant shall furnish an undertaking in Form 51 to the Licensing \nAuthority to the effect that to the best of his knowledge based on search in trademarks registry, \ncentral data base for brand name or trade name of drugs maintained by Central Drugs Standard \nControl Organisation, literature and reference books on details of drug formulations in India, \nand internet, such or similar brand name or trade name is not already in existence with respect \nto any drug in the country and the proposed brand name or trade name shall not lead to any \nconfusion or deception in the market with effective from 06.11.2019. \n \n(vi) The testing capacities of Ce ntral Drugs Testing Laboratories under CDSCO are being \nconstant"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |634", "t": "Opening Food Testing Laboratories at Block Level", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.634 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9th DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nOPENING FOOD TESTING LABORATORIES AT BLOCK LEVEL \n634. SHRI RATANSINH MAGANSINH RATHOD: \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of food testing laboratories opened in the country particularly in the backward \nareas during the last three years and the current year; \n \n(b) whether the Government has any proposal to open more such laboratories at block level in \nfuture; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n        \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY \nWELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Food Authority has notified 225 food laboratories under Section 43 of the Food \nSafety and Standards Act, 2006 for primary food sample testing and 21 referral laboratories \ntill October, 2022.  Food Safety and Standards Authority of India ( FSSAI) has informed that \nopening of new food testing laboratories is not under the purview of FSSAI. It is \nresponsibility of the States to establish the Food Testing Laboratories in the States. FSSAI \nprovides financial support for procurement of equipment, purchase of consu mables, hiring of \ncontractual manpower for a specified period and capacity building of the lab staff. \n \n………………… \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |635", "t": "Passenger Ferry and Freight Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS. SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 635\nANSWERED ON 0911212022\nPASSENGER FERRY AND FREIGHT SERVICES\n635, SHRI RAVI KISHAN:\nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPP|NG AND WATERWAYS be pteased to state:\nq-f,{, rifd qftfrf,{ 3fr{ wmcFf ci\n(a) the details of the scheme being formulated by the Government to develop the\nwateMays in the country,\n(b) the number of port terminals developed under Waterways Development\nProject, State/UT - wise,\n(c) the details of the waterways on which passenger ferry and fright services\nhave been started so far during the last three years, State/UT-wise; and\n(d) the details of the waterways on which the said services are proposed to be\nstarted during the current year?\nANSWER\nI\\JlINISTER OF PORTS. SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) To promote lnland Water Transport (lWT) in the country, 11 1 wateMays\n(including 5 existing and 106 new) spread over 24 states have been declared as\nNational Waterways under the National WateMays Act, 20i6. Based on the\noutcome of techno-economic feasibility and Detaited project Reports (DpRs) of the\nNWs, 26 NWs have been found viable for cargo/passenger movement as detailed at\nAnnex.-1. Further, Ministry of Ports, Shipping and WateMays has a Central Sector\nScheme under which 100% grant is provided for development of lnland Water\ninfrastructure in North Eastern Stales\n(b) There are 21 permanent terminals/Jetties developed on NWs, details of which is\nat Annex.-2.\n(c) & (d) Travelle