: Ministry of Health &  Family Welfare maintains data on COVID -19 deaths as \nreported by States. A total of 5,30,633 deaths due to COVID -19 have been reported by \nStates/UTs in the country as on 6th December 2022. Details of deaths reported by States/UTs \nis at Annexure. \nHon’ble Su preme Court had issued orders regarding the criteria fixed to register Covid -19 \ndeaths on 30 th June 2021. The total deaths as reported by the State of Kerala as on 30 th June \n2021 was 13093. \nAs per Hon’ble Supreme Court’s directions, NDMA has issued guidelines for Ex -gratia \nassistance to next of kin of the deceased by Covid -19 on 11 th September 2021. States are \naccordingly sanctioning Ex-Gratia to the eligible families. \nThe guidelines issued by NDMA has recommended States/UTs to settle all claims with in \nthirty days of submission of required documents, subject to cause of death certified as \nCOVID-19. \n \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \n \nAnnexure \nCumulative number of deaths due to COVID-19 as, reported by States/UTs.  \n(as on 06.12.22) \n \nS. No. Name of State / UT No. of Deaths \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 129 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 14733 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 296 \n4 Assam 8035 \n5 Bihar 12302 \n6 Chandigarh 1181 \n7 Chhattisgarh 14146 \n8 Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu 4 \n9 Delhi 26519 \n10 Goa 4013 \n11 Gujarat 11043 \n12 Haryana 10714 \n13 Himachal  4213 \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 4785 \n15 Jharkhand 5331 \n16 Karnataka 40303 \n17 Kerala 71504 \n18 Ladakh 231 \n19 Lakshadweep 52 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 10776 \n21 Maharashtra 148407 \n22 Manipur 2149 \n23 Meghalaya 1624 \n24 Mizoram 726 \n25 Nagaland 782 \n26 Odisha 9205 \n27 Puducherry 1975 \n28 Punjab 19289 \n29 Rajasthan 9653 \n30 Sikkim 499 \n31 Tamil Nadu 38049 \n32 Telangana 4111 \n33 Tripura 940 \n34 Uttarakhand 7751 \n35 Uttar Pradesh 23632 \n36 West Bengal 21531 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |659", "t": "Tele-Consultation in AYUSH Institutions", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 659 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nTELE-CONSULTATION IN AYUSH INSTITUTIONS \n \n659.  DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the MINISTER OF AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any tele-consultations has taken place in the institutions under the AYUSH  \nin Bihar; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the percentage of the total consultations held telephonically; \n \n(d) whether there is any initiative and/or plans to b e taken up by the Government to \nincrease the number of such  tele-consultations; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) & (b): There are two separate tele-consultations running i.e.,i) E-sanjeevani OPD and ii) \nAYUSH COVID-19 Counselling Helpline (14443). \nThese tele-consultations have been running at National level and not on State Level. \n \n(c) to (e):  No separate state wise data is maintained, and the increase in strength will depend \non the demand. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |66", "t": "Installation of VSS in Train Coaches", "head": "����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������\n��\n������ �������� ������������������������������ �������������������� �������� �������� �������� ������ �������������� �������� ������������������ ������������������ ������ ��������\n������������������������������������������������