r; \n \n(c) whether there has been sharp rise in various types of crimes committed in \nState of Bihar in recent years; and \n \n(d) whether any stringent action plan has been  chalked out by the \nGovernment in consultation with the State Governments to stop these crimes \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) to (c): National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes \ninformation on crimes in its publication ‘Crime in India’. The published \nreports are available till the year 2021. State -wise details of cases of crime \non theft, dacoity, robbery, rape and child kidnapping, including for the State \nof Bihar, during 2019, 2020 & 2021 are given in Annexure. The data does not \nshow any such trend. \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 973 FOR 13.12.2022 \n \n(d): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule \nto the Constitution of India. The State Governments are responsible for \nprevention, detection, registration and investigation of crime and for \nprosecuting the criminals through their law enforcement agencies. However, \nthe Ministry of Home Affairs have issued advisories to States and UTs, from \ntime to time, to maintain law and order and ensure that any person who \ntakes law into his/her own hand is punished promptly as per law. \n*****\n\nANNEXURE \nL.SUS.Q.NO.973 FOR 13.12.2022 \nState/UT-wise Cases Registered under Rape, Theft, Robbery, Dacoity and Kidnapping & Abduction during 2019, 2020 and 2021 as per NCRB data \n   \nSL State/UT Rape Theft  Robbery Dacoity Kidnapping and Abduction of \nChildren \n2019 2020 2021 2019 2020 2021 2019 2020 2021 2019 2020 2021 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1086 1095 1188 11301 9508 11831 310 237 259 40 39 74 521 487 487 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 63 60 83 489 395 745 76 48 50 15 7 7 61 41 52 \n3 Assam 1773 1657 1733 16478 14391 17351 1944 1577 1885 198 198 188 3742 2312 2704 \n4 Bihar 730 806 786 3497