0 0 0 2 0 1 0 26 6 6 \n30 Chandigarh 112 60 74 908 614 757 33 23 17 4 1 6 153 112 149 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman & Diu+ 4 4 3 87 47 64 9 7 11 0 2 3 32 19 46 \n32 Delhi 1253 997 1250 245985 177439 199981 1956 1963 2333 15 9 26 5537 3748 5053 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir* 223 243 315 1923 2064 2397 73 70 104 1 5 3 285 293 478 \n34 Ladakh - 2 2 - 2 10 - 0 0 - 0 0 - 0 0 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 3 0 19 9 7 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 0 \n36 Puducherry 10 8 2 484 323 408 20 16 12 3 3 4 0 0 34 \n  TOTAL"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |974", "t": "Component Champion Incentive Scheme", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 974 \nANSWERED ON 13.12.2022 \n \nCOMPONENT CHAMPION INCENTIVE SCHEME \n \n974. SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWAR:  \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has initiated a new Component Champion Incentive Scheme in the \ncountry;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the extent to which it will be beneficial;  \n \n(c) whether it is a ‘sales value linked’ scheme, applicable on pre-approved Advanced Automotive \nTechnology components of all vehicles;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the incentive currently being provided under this scheme to Battery Electric Vehicles and \nHydrogen Fuel Cells component? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a): Yes Sir, the Government has approved the Production Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for \nAutomobile and Auto Component Industry in India for enhancing India's Manufacturing \nCapabilities for Advanced Automotive Technology (AAT) products with a budgetary outlay of \n₹25,938 Crore for five years. \n \nThis scheme has two components:  \ni) Champion OEM Incentive Scheme \nii) Component Champion Incentive Scheme \n \n(b): PLI Scheme for Automobile and Auto Component Industry proposes financial incentives \nto boost domestic manufacturing of Advanced Automotive Technology (AAT) products and \nattracts investments in the automotive manufacturing value chain.   Its prime objectives include \novercoming cost disabilities, creating economies of scale and building a robust supply chain in \nareas of AAT products.  It will also generate employment.  The list of AAT products has been \nnotified by MHI vide S.O 4632 (E) dat ed 9 th November 2021.  The list of AAT products is \navailable on the official website of MHI.   \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n-2- \n \n \n(c) & (d): The Component Champion Incentive Scheme is a 'sales value linked' scheme, \napplicable on Advanced Automotive Technology components of vehicles, Completely Knocked \nDown (CKD)/ Semi Knocked Down (SKD) kits, Vehicle aggregates of 2 -Wheelers, 3-Wheelers, \npassenger vehicles, commercial vehicles and tr actors, etc.   Minimum 50% domestic value \naddition will be required to become eligible for incentive under this scheme.  \n \n(e): Incentives of 13% to 18%  is applicable on Battery Electric vehicles and Hydrogen Fuel \nCell vehicles and their AAT components.  \n     ****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |975", "t": "Target Olympic Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.975 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.12.2022 \n \nTarget Olympic Programme \n \n975.  SHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK: \n         DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR:  \n         SHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA):  \n         SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR:  \n         SHRI BHOLA SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has approved foreign training camps for \nOlympic champion along with three other athletes under ‘TOPS’ (Target \nOlympic Podium Scheme);  \n(b) if so, the details of all sportspersons selected under the TOPS during \nthe last one year;  \n(c) the number of sportspersons who applied for, were selected and \nrejected for the financial assistance in foreign training camps during the \nlast one year, State/UT-wise;  \n(d) the details of budget allocation and expenditure of TOPS for the \nfinancial year 2022-23; and  \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government in support of \nsportspersons in the country during the last two years? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a): Yes, Sir. The Government has approved foreign training camps under \nthe Target Olympic Podium Scheme (TOPS) of this Ministry. \n \n\n(b): The details of all sportspersons selected under the TOPS during the \nlast one year are given in Annexure – I. \n \n(c): The details of the number of sportspersons who applied for, were \nselected, and rejected for the financial assistance in foreign training \ncamps during the last one year, State/UT-wise, are given in Annexure – \nII. \n \n(d): Budget of Rs. 31.60 Crore has been allocated for TOPS for the \nfinancial year 2022-23. Expenditure of Rs. 12.88 Crore has been \nsanctioned under TOPS till date. \n \n(e): Sports being a State Subject, the responsibility to support the \nsportspersons in the country rests primarily with the respective \nState/UT Governments. The Union Government supplements their efforts \nby bridging critical gaps. The Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nimplements various schemes supporting sportspersons across the \ncountry, viz.: \n  \n(i) Khelo India Scheme;  \n(ii) Assistance to National Sports Federations;  \n(iii) Special Awards to Winners in International Sports Events and \ntheir Coaches;  \n(iv) National Sports Awards;  \n(v) Pension to Meritorious Sportspersons;  \n(vi) PanditDeendayalUpadhyay National Sports Welfare Fund;  \n(vii) National Sports Development Fund (NSDF); and  \n(viii) Running Sports Training Centres through Sports Authority of \nIndia. \nDetails of the above schemes are available in the public domain on the \nwebsites of this Ministry and the Sports Authority of India. \n \n**** \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXUREREFERREDINREPLYTOPART(B)OFLOKSABHAUNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 975 FOR ANSWER ON 13-12-2022 REGARDING TARGET \nOLYMPIC PROGRAMME ASKED BY SHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK,DR. \nSUKANTA MAJUMDAR, SHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA), SHRI \nVINOD KUMAR SONKAR AND SHRI BHOLA SINGH,MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT \n \nDetails of all sportspersons selected under the TOPS during the last one \nyear for foreign training camps (2021-22): \n \nSno Discipline \n \nAthlete name \n \nNo of Foreign Camps \n \n1 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nFencing \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nC.A. Bhavani Devi 3 \nKaran Singh 3 \nSheetalDalal 1 \nTaniksha Khatri 1 \nChingakhamjetlee \nSingh 1 \nR S Sherjin 1 \nVedikaKhushi 1 \nAbhayShinde 1 \nShreya Gupta 1 \nOinamJubraj 1 \nSN Siva Magesh 1 \nLaishramMoramba 1 \n2 Cycling - 0 \n3 Shooting - 0 \n4 Archery - 0 \n5 Badminton PV Sindhu 1 \nSainaNehwal 1 \n6 Wrestling \nRavi Kumar Dahiya 1 \nDeepak Punia 3 \nBajrangPunia 4 \nVineshPhogat 1 \nSunil Kumar 1 \n7 Weightlifting \nMirabaiChanu 1 \nAchintaSheuli 1 \nBindyarani Devi 1 \n8 Table Tennis Swastika Ghosh 1 \n9 Swimming Sajan Prakash 1 \n\nAryan Nehra 1 \n10 Rowing - 0 \n11 Boxing - 0 \n12 Athletics Neeraj Chopra 5 \nTajinder Singh Toor 1 \n13 Sailing \nVarun Thakkar 1 \nKC Ganapathy 1 \nNethraKumanan 1 \nVishnu Saravanan 1 \n \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXUREREFERREDINREPLYTOPART(C) OFLOKSABHAUNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 975 FOR ANSWER ON 13-12-2022 REGARDING TARGET \nOLYMPIC PROGRAMME ASKED BY SHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK, \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR, SHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA), SHRI \nVINOD KUMAR SONKAR AND SHRI BHOLA SINGH, MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT \n \nNumber of sportspersons who applied for, were selected and rejected \nfor the financial assistance in foreign training camps during the last one \nyear, State/UT-wise \n \nYear Discipline \nNo of \nSportsperson \napplied for \nFinancial \nAssistance for \nForeign \nTraining \ncamps (under \nTOPS) 2021-22 \nSelected Rejected State/UT wise \n2021-22 \nFencing 12 12 0 \n3- Tamil Nadu \n3-Manipur \n2- Haryana \n1- Maharashtra \n1- Rajasthan \n1- Chattisgarh \n1-Jammu \n&kashmir \nSwimming 2 2 0 1- Kerala \n1- Gujarat \nTable Tennis 1 1 0 Maharashtra \nAthletics 2 2 0 1-Haryana \n1-Punjab \nCycling 0 0 0 0 \nSailing 4 4 0 3-Chennai \n1-Maharashtra \nShooting 2 0 2 \n1-UP \n1-Punjab \nWrestling 11 10 1 9 - H"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |976", "t": "Cold Storages in Food Processing Sector", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 976 \nANSWERED ON- 13TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nCOLD SROREGES IN FOOD PROCESSING SECTOR  \n \n976. SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \nSHRI C.N ANNADURAI: \nSHRI SELVAM G.: \nSHRIMATI  MANJULATA MANDAL: \nSHRI GANANAN KIRTIKAR; \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of normal cold storages and multi -purpose cold storages set up to prevent wastage \nof fruits and vegetables during each of the last three years and the current year, State -wise including \nTamil Nadu and Maharashtra; \n(b)  whether the present capacity of cold storages in the food processing sector is insufficient to \nprocess the agricultural produce in the country; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof including capacity utilization, Sector-wise; \n(d) whether majority of cold storages are mainly concentrated in urban areas leaving behind the rural \nareas where there is imperative need for more cold storages; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the number of existing cold storages in rural areas vis -à-vis urban \nareas in the country; and \n(f) the other corrective measures taken by the Government in this regard? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a).  The State-wise list of cold storages sanctioned under NHB (National  Horticulture Board) and \nMIDH (Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture) are given in Annexure. \n \n(b) & (c). The study, instituted by the National Centre for Cold Chain Development (NCCD) under the \nMinistry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare on All India Cold -chain Infrastructure Capacity \n(Assessment of Status & Gap) - 2015 and conducted by NABCONS, had reported that 35 million metric \ntons (MMT) cold storage capacity is req uired against the existing capacity of 32 million metric tons \n(MMT). However, the food processing units are mainly having storage capacity commensurate with \ntheir processing capacities. \n \n(d) & (e). As the schemes being implemented by the Government for sup porting setting up of cold \nstorage facilities are demand driven, the records are maintained based on individual/ entrepreneur \ncoordinates. \n \n(f). The Government does not set up cold storage on its own. However, Ministry of  Agriculture & \nFamers Welfare is implementing schemes namely “Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture \n(MIDH)” under which assistance is provided for development of Post -Harvest Management (PHM) \nincluding establishment of cold storages; and “Capital Investment Subsidy for \nConstruction/Expansion/Modernization of Cold Storages and storages” for Horticulture Produce” \nthrough National Horticulture  Board. Besides, the Ministry of Food Processing Industries is \nimplementing Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY) through setting up food processing \nfacilities with associated storage facilities. \n \n***** \n\n  \nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART  (a) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.976 FOR ANSWER ON 13 TH DECEMBER 2022  REGARDING “COLD \nSROREGES IN FOOD PROCESSING SECTOR” \nState wise and year wise details of Cold Storages \nState  2019-20  2020-21  2021-22  2022-23  \n  Number  Capacity \n(MT)  \nNumber  Capacity \n(MT)  \nNumber  Capacity \n(MT)  \nNumber  Capacity \n(MT)  \nAndhra \nPradesh  \n5  57164  -  -  11  55000  2  18789  \nAssam  1  10000  1  9000  -  -  1  5500  \nGujarat  12  33964  1  3452  8  37816  9  18882  \nHaryana  -  -  -  -  2  16063.7  4  6741  \nHimachal \nPradesh  \n-  -  1  6368  -  -  3  1231  \nJammu & \nKashmir  \n5  24981  7  32163  7  33574  4  19000  \nJharkhand  -  -  -  -  -  -  1  5974.67  \nKarnataka  6  50488  -  -  1  7300  4  21320  \nMadhya \nPradesh  \n1  5653  -  -  1  10000  1  126.99  \nMaharashtra  -  -  1  5600  4  22133  2  9504  \nPunjab  5  28945  6  31632  14  60414  2  4925  \nRajasthan  1  5800  4  28405  -  -  1  4654.88  \nTamil Nadu  3  23260  -  -  1  5386  -  -  \nTelangana  5  47320  -  -  -  -  -  -  \nUttar Pradesh  7  59325  17  97424  1  100  15  85871.9  \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |977", "t": "Funds allocated under PMGSY Phase-III", "head": " O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 977 \n  ANSWERED ON 13/12/2022 \n \nFUNDS ALLOCATED UNDER PMGSY PHASE-III \n \n977.  SHRI DILIP SAIKIA: \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \nDR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nSHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \nSHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR: \nSHRI BALAK NATH: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the proposals which have been approved and funds allocated under \nPradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), Phase-III alongwith the total funds \nsanctioned and allocated under such Yojana, State and district-wise including \nRajasthan, Assam, Jharkhand and Maharashtra; \n(b)  the current status of the proposals approved under such yojana, State-wise, \nparticularly for Rajasthan in Sikar, Bharatpur, Karauli, Dhaulpur and Alwar and for \nAssam, Jharkhand and Maharashtra; \n(c)  whether the Government has proposed to connect one village to another through ring \nroad in the Phase-III of PMGSY; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b) Pradhan Mantri Gram SadakYojana (PMGSY) -III was launched in the year 2019 \nfor consolidation of 1,25,000 Km Through Routes and Major Rural Links connecting \nhabitations, inter-alia, to Gramin Agricultural Markets (GrAMs), Higher Secondary Schools \nand Ho spitals at an estimated cost of Rs. 80,250 crore. Against the allocated target of \n1,25,000 Km, 91,371 Km and 1,290 bridges have already been sanctioned to 21 States/UTs at \nan estimated cost of Rs. 63,385.79 crore as on 8th December, 2022. The   State/UT-wise and \ndistrict-wise details of proposals approved, alongwith the outlay of the sanctioned projects \nunder PMGSY-III may be accessed at Programme website: www.omms.nic.in => progress \nmonitoring => Monthly Progress Reports (MPR)=>MPR1.  The details of projects  \nsanctioned in the States of Rajasthan, Assam, Jharkhand and Maharashtra under PMGSY -III \nare as under:- \n \n Road length in Km \nName of the State Projects sanctioned  Value of projects (Rs. in \ncrore) Road length Bridges \nRajasthan 6,156 6 3,307.72 \nAssam 3,756 56 2,681.13 \nJharkhand 979 0 629.58 \nMaharashtra 2,926 0 2,177.44 \n\n  \nThe details of proposals approved in Sikar, Bharatpur, Karauli, Dhaulpur and Alwar in the \nState of Rajasthan are as under:- \n  \nName of the District Projects sanctioned  Value of projects (Rs. in \ncrore) Road length Bridges \nSikar 236 0 137.92 \nBharatpur 128 0 89.43 \nKarauli 100 0 55.15 \nDhaulpur 194 0 87.56 \nAlwar 179 0 87.48 \n  \n(c) & (d) PMGSY -III envisages consolidation of the existing Through Routes and Major \nRural Links, as per the programme guidelines. A road connecting various habitations might \nbe taken up under such stipulation, but there is no express provision to connect one v illage to \nanother through ring road under PMGSY-III. \n  \n****** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |979", "t": "PMGSY in West Bengal", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 979 \n  ANSWERED ON 13/12/2022 \n \nPMGSY IN WEST BENGAL \n \n979.  SHRI DIBYENDU ADHIKARI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of total length of roads sanctioned and constructed in West Bengal in  \nphases under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY); \n(b)  the total number of pending projects in West Bengal under PMGSY and the time by \nwhich the said proposals are likely to be sanctioned; and \n(c)  the measures taken to ensure the proper maintenance of the road constructed under \nPMGSY? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b) The details of road length sanctioned and constructed in West Bengal under various \nphases of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) are given below:- \n  \nPhase Road \nlength sanctioned \n(in Km) \nRoad length  \nconstructed  \n(in Km) \nBalance road \nlength  \n(in Km) \nPMGSY-I 34,524 33,975 245* \nPMGSY-II 2,518 2,466 39* \nPMGSY-III 857 0 857 \n* Balance road length is less than the difference of sanctioned and completed length due to \nthe reasons that some projects were completed with less than the sanctioned length due to \nreduction in road length, change in alignment, construction of part length by other agencies, \netc.  \nThe state has been sanctioned all the projects under PMGSY -I & II. The State of West \nBengal has been allocated a target of 6,288 Km of road length under  PMGSY-III, out of \nwhich 857 Km has been sanctioned to the state. Receipt of proposals from the State \nGovernments and their examination and consideration, as per guidelines, is a continuous \nprocess. The implementation period of PMGSY -III is up to March 2025. The state has been \nasked to expedite the submission of the rest of the proposals under PMGSY-III. \n(c)   'Rural road' is a State subject and as per the programme guidelines of PMGSY, \nmaintenance of roads constructed under the programme is the responsibility of the State \nGovernments and all road works are covered by initial five -year maintenance contracts to be \nentered into along with the construction contract, with the same contractor, as per the \nstandard bidding document. Maintenance funds to service the contract are required  to be \nbudgeted by the State Governments and placed at the disposal of the State Rural Roads \nDevelopment Agencies in a separate maintenance account. On expiry of the 5 -year post \nconstruction maintenance, PMGSY roads are required to be placed under zonal ma intenance \n\ncontracts consisting of 5-year maintenance including renewal as per maintenance cycle, from \ntime to time, which are also financed by the State Governments.  \nNational Quality Monitors appointed by the Ministry are also deputed to check the quality  of \nmaintenance work on PMGSY roads. If any work is found having unsatisfactory grading, the \nconcerned State Government is informed to get it rectified/ repaired. \nTo further enhance the  focus on maintenance of roads during the defect liability period and \nalso streamlining the delivery of routine maintenance of PMGSY roads, electronic \nMaintenance of Rural Roads (eMARG)- an e-governance solution, has been introduced.  \n \n \n********** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |98", "t": "Cyber Crime against Women", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *98 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 13TH DECEMBER, 2022/ AGRAHAYANA 22, 1944 \n(SAKA) \n \nCOLLECTION OF CENSUS DATA \n \n*98.      SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any  initiatives for collection of \ncensus data for this decade census and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether any changes in the format or  proforma have been suggested \nfor collection of  additional information for various socio -economic \nparameters and preparation of detailed category -wise database and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to include  digital census and self -\nenumeration of citizens, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether there is a demand for conducting caste-wise census also along \nwith the data; and \n \n(e) if so, whether the Government has taken any action in this regard and if \nnot, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n \n(a) to (e):    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.S.Q.NO.*98 FOR 13.12.2022 \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *98 FOR  \n13.12.2022 REGARDING “COLLECTION OF CENSUS DATA” \n \n(a):  The intent of the Government for conducting Census 2021 was \nnotified in Gazette of India on 28th March, 2019. However, due to outbreak \nof COVID-19 pandemic, the conduct of Census 2021 and the related field \nactivities have been postponed until further orders. \n \n(b):  In Census, data is collected on demographic and various socio -\neconomic parameters like education, SC/ST, religion, language, marriage, \nfertility, disability, occupation and migration of the individuals. The \nquestionnaire for the first phase of Census was notified in the Gazette of \nIndia. \n \n(c):  The forthcoming Census is to be the first digital Census and there is \na provision for self -enumeration. Mobile App for collection of data and a \nCensus Portal for management and monitoring of various Census related \nactivities have been developed. \n \n(d) & (e):  Three State Governments, namely, Bihar, Maharashtra and \nOdisha and a few organisations have requested to collect caste details in \nthe forthcoming C ensus.  In Census, the castes and tribes which are \nspecifically notified as Scheduled Castes (SCs) and Scheduled Tribes (STs) \nas per the Constitution (Scheduled  Castes) Order, 1950 and the \nConstitution (Scheduled Tribes) Order, 1950, as amended from time t o \ntime, are enumerated.  The Government of India has not enumerated caste \nwise population other than SCs and STs in Census since independence. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |980", "t": "Kisan Rail", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 980 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 13TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nKISAN RAIL \n \n980. SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \nSHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE: \nDR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE:   \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government had formed a Committee under the Ministry of Agriculture \nand Farmers Welfare to work out the modalities of Kisan Rail;  \n \n(b) whether the aforesaid Committee has submitted its report;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) the number of private players who have shown interest in Kisan Rail through the \npublic-private partnership (PPP) mode for a cold supply chain to transport perishable \ngoods; and  \n \n(e) the present status of the PPP mode of Transport of perishable goods? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (e): To implement the budget announcement 2020 -21, a Committee was constituted \non Kisan Rail and Krishi Udan. The Committee constituted a sub -committee for detailed \ndeliberation and preparati on of concept note under convenership of Executive Director, \nRailway Board.  \n \nMinistry of Railways after due deliberation with Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers \nWelfare has started operating Kisan Special Trains for transportation of perishable \nhorticulture / agriculture products, dairy, fisheries etc. These trains are multi -commodity, \nmulti-consignor/consignee services, running in a time -tabled manner with stoppages at all \nmajor stations. \n \nThe first Kisan Rail was flagged off from Devlali (Maharashtra) to Danapur (Bihar) \nby Shri Narendra Singh Tomar, Hon'ble Minister of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare and \n\nShri Piyush Goyal, then Hon'ble Minister of Railways, Commerce & Industry on 7th August \n2020, for transportation of perishable agriculture/horticulture products, dairy, fisheries etc.  \n \nThe 100th service of Kisan Rail was flagged off by Hon'ble Prime Minister Shri \nNarendra Modi for the route from Sangola (Maharashtra) to Shalimar (West Bengal) on \n28th December 2020.  \n \nCurrently, a total of 2359 trips of Kisan  Rail trains have been operated on 167 \nroutes, wherein nearly 7.9 lakh tones of consignments have been transported.  \n \nThe routes for Kisan Rail are decided on demand received from farmers and other \nstakeholders. Whenever a demand for Kisan Rail for a parti cular route is received, \narrangements are made by the concerned Zonal Railway for supply for rakes and \noperation of Kisan Rail train, duly considering the operational feasibility of the proposed \nroute. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |981", "t": "Value Addition of Food Grains", "head": "GOVERNMENTOF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 981 \n ANSWERED ON. 13TH DECEMBER, 2022 \n \nVALUE ADDITION OF FOOD GRAINS \n \n†981. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the percentage of food grains produced in the country that are processed for value addition; \n \n(b) whether the percentage of the said food grains has increased during the last three years; and \n \n(c) if so, the roadmap for increasing this percentage over the next five years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (c). The  Ministry of Food Processing Industries implement  schemes to augment post harvest \ninfrastructure and processing facilit ies so as to reduce post -harvest losses and augment processing levels of \nvarious agriculture produce.  As per a study commissioned by the Ministry, the level of processing of food \ngrains has increased over the years, as indicated below: \n \n               \n     \n \n \nSource: Study to determine the level of Food Processing in India Submitted by M/s. Deloitte (2021) \n \n The Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI)  implements a Central Sector umbrella scheme, the \nPradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY) since 2016 -17 for overall growth and development of the \nsector with the aim to augment preservation and processing capacity so as to reduce post -harvest losses, create \noff f arm employment , and enhancing of value addition  & exports of processed food  with components, \nnamely,(i) Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure, (ii) Infrastructure for Agro -Processing \nClusters, (iii) Creation/ Expansion of Food Processing & Preservation Capacities, (iv) Food Safety and Quality \nAssurance Infrastructure, (v) Human Resources and Institutions –Research & Development  and (vi) Operation \nGreens. Earlier Mega Food Park and Creation of Backward and Fo rward Linkages schemes were also part of \nPMKSY during the 14th Finance Commission period. \n \n 679 completed projects under PMKSY has resulted in creation of approximately 204.49 LMT of \npreservation and processing capacity till 30.11.2022.  \n \n The Ministry is also implementing the Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro Food Processing \nEnterprises (PM -FME) scheme since 2020 -21 for providing financial, technical and business support for \nupgradation / setting up of two lakh micro enterprises based on One District O ne Product approach. A \nProduction Linked Incentive (PLI) scheme  is being implemented from 2021 -22 to 2026 -27 in order  to support \nprocessed fruits & vegetables, marine products, Ready to Eat  (RTE)/ Ready to Cook (RTC) products including \nmillet based products and mozzarella cheese segments of the food processing sector. Innovative /organic \nproducts by SME segment is also covered under the scheme . Promoting Indian food brands abroad, m arketing \nand branding support are also provided for under the scheme. \n \n***** \nCommodity Level of Processing (in %) \nFoodgrains 2010-11 2015-16 2018-19 \n49.3 63.2 68.2 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |982", "t": "Construction of Roads under Phases of PMGSY", "head": " O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 982 \n  ANSWERED ON 13/12/2022 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF ROADS UNDER PHASES OF PMGSY \n \n982.  SHRI AJAY KUMAR MANDAL: \nSHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \nSHRIMATI RAMA DEVI: \nMS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \nSHRI SUNIL BABURAO MENDHE: \nSHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the total length of roads in various Phases approved and constructed \nunder Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), Statewise including Sonipat of \nHaryana, Bhagalpur, Sheohar, Sitamarhi, East Champaran and Supaul of Bihar, West \nBengal and Maharashtra; \n(b)  whether several proposals are pending for approval under PMGSY Phase-I, II and III \nseparately of Maharashtra and Sonipat, Bhagalpur, Sheohar, Sitamarhi, East \nChamparan, Supaul and Hugli districts under PMGSY; \n(c)  if so, the reasons therefor along with the time by which the said proposals are likely to \nbe approved; and \n(d)  the measures taken for ensuring the proper maintenance of roads constructed under \nPMGSY? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)  The details of total length of roads sanctioned and constructed under various ongoing \ninterventions/verticals of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), State/UT -wise are \ngiven at Annexure.  The details of road length sanctioned and completed in Sonipat District \nof Haryana and Bhagalpur, Sheohar, Sitamarhi, East Champaran and Supaul districts of Bihar \nunder PMGSY as on 8th December, 2022 are as under:- \nRoad length in Km \nName of the State Name of the district Sanctioned road \nlength \nCompleted road \nlength \nHaryana Sonipat 492 474 \nBihar Bhagalpur 1,470 1,323 \nSheohar 396 309 \nSitamarhi 1,964 1,737 \nEast Champaran 2,971 2,569 \nSupaul 843 685 \n  \n(b) and (c) All the projects under PMGSY -I and II have already been sanctioned to the \nStates/UTs.  Under PMGSY -III, Ministry has allocated the target length to the States/UTs. \n\nThe district-wise allocation of the target within the overall target allocated t o the State under \nPMGSY-III is done by the respective State Governments. \n  \nThe State of Haryana has already been accorded all the sanctions under PMGSY -I, II and III, \nas per the allocated targets to the State. There is no proposal of Bihar and West Bengal \npending in the Ministry as of now for approval. Proposal in respect of State of Maharashtra \nfor 548 road works of 3,334 Km under PMGSY -III was considered in the meeting of the \nEmpowered Committee held on 16 th November, 2022. The observations of the Empower ed \nCommittee have been communicated to the State Government for submitting compliance \nreport.  \n  \n(d) As per the programme guidelines of PMGSY, maintenance of roads constructed under the \nprogramme is the responsibility of the State Governments and all road works are covered by \ninitial five-year maintenance contracts to be entered into along with the construction contract, \nwith the same contractor, as per the standard bidding document. Maintenance funds to service \nthe contract are required to be budgeted by t he State Governments and placed at the disposal \nof the State Rural Roads Development Agencies (SRRDAs) in a separate maintenance \naccount. On expiry of the 5 -year post construction maintenance, PMGSY roads are required \nto be placed under zonal maintenance c ontracts consisting of 5 -year maintenance including \nrenewal as per maintenance cycle, from time to time, which are also financed by the State \ngovernments.  \nFurther, in order to ensure that states provide required maintenance funds during the design \nlife of the road being constructed under PMGSY-III, States have to enter into a Memorandum \nof Understanding with the Ministry of Rural Development, Government of India before \nlaunching of the scheme in the concerned state for providing adequate funds for the \nmaintenance of roads constructed/ upgraded under PMGSY for initial five -years routine \nmaintenance, and for further five -year routine maintenance including periodic renewal as per \nrequirement.  \nAs a measure of enhancing the focus on maintenance of roads during the defect liability \nperiod (five years from the date of completion of road) and streamlining the delivery of \nroutine maintenance of PMGSY roads on the basis of performance -based maintenance \ncontract, Electronic Maintenance of PMGSY roads (eMARG) has been implemented in all \nthe states.   \n  \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.982 for \n13.12.2022 \nTotal road length sanctioned and completed under various interventions/verticals of \nPMGSY, State-wise \nRoad length in Km \nS.No. State Name Road length Sanctioned Road Length Completed \n1 Andaman And Nicobar 200 50 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 18,973 16,866 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 14,384 13,109 \n4 Assam 32,420 30,759 \n5 Bihar 65,576 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |984", "t": "Nirbhaya Fund", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 984 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 13TH DECEMBER, 2022/ AGRAHAYANA 22, 1944 \n(SAKA) \n \nNIRBHAYA FUND \n \n984. SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the quantum of funds disbursed by the Government from the Nirbhaya \nFund to set up Anti Human Trafficking Units (AHTUs) across the country; \n \n(b) whether the Government has allocated financial resources for the \nintelligence gathering and capacity building of such AHTUs for scientific \nand timely investigation and charge-sheeting; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA)  \n (a) to (c): The Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) has provided financial \nassistance to the tune of Rs 98.86 crores under “Nirbhaya Fund” to all \nStates and Union Territories (UTs) during the financial years 2019-20 and \n2020-21 for strengthening the existing Anti Human Trafficking Units \n(AHTUs) and for establishing new AHTUs covering all districts of the \nStates and UTs. \n‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are ‘State-List’ subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule  to  the  Constitution  of  India.  Therefore,  the  responsibility   of  \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 984 FOR 13.12.2022 \nintelligence gathering, investigation and prosecution vests with respective \nState Governments who are competent to deal with such matters under \nthe extant provisions of law.  \nThe Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) provides regular financial assistance \nto the States and UTs for holding Judicial Colloquiums and State Level \nConferences from time to time, which are aimed at the capacity building of \nlaw officers, police officers and other officials concerned with the issue of \npreventing and countering human trafficking. MHA had also issued an \nAdvisory to the States and UTs, inter-alia, advising them that ‘intelligence’ \nand ‘surveillance’ mechanisms may be established for identifying the \npersons and gangs involved in human trafficking and for gathering \ninformation about their affiliations, modus operandi, etc. The States/UTs \nhave also been advised that Police officers, especially those handling \ncases of human trafficking, should be trained and sensitized at regular \nintervals and they should be imparted knowledge of procedural laws, court \nrulings, administrative procedures, skills in child friendly investigation, \nincluding interviewing, interrogation, scientific data collection, facilitating \nvictims/witness protection programmes etc. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |985", "t": "Amendment to Cinematograph Act, 1952", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 985 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.12.2022 \n \nAMENDMENT TO CINEMATOGRAPH ACT, 1952 \n \n985: SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of numerous feature films depicting \ndetailed scenes that glorify and normalize the usage of \nMethylenedioxymethamphetamine (MDMA) especially in Malayalam movies \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is intending to make mandatory requirements of \nstatutory warnings in such scenes to educate the audience of the \nconsequences of narcotic drugs; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government would further amend provisions of the \nCinematograph Act, 1952 to ensure compliance; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a): The Central Board of Film Certification (CBFC) performs the \nstatutory function of certifying films for public exhibition in India in \naccordance with the Cinematograph Act, 1952, the Cinematograph \n(Certification) Rules, 1983 and Guidelines for certification of films \nnotified by the Government.  \n \nUnder Guideline 2(vi), the CBFC shall ensure that “scenes tending to \nencourage, justify or glamorise drug addiction are not shown”. While \n\ncertifying films, including Malayalam films, having scenes depicting \ndrug usage, it is ensured that the aforesaid guideline is strictly \nadhered to. Also, during examination of a film, CBFC ensures that no \ninstances of promotion of drugs of any sort, including MDMA, are \nthere. If certain scenes are found to be violating these Guidelines, \nthey are directed to be removed or modified accordingly under \nSection 4(1)(iii) of the Cinematograph Act, 1952. However, if such \nscenes are germane to the narrative of the film, they are \nrecommended to be modified suitably or reduced to a minimum, \nwithout being shown in detail. \n \n(b) & (c): No such proposal is under consideration by the Ministry. \n \n (d) & (e): Does not arise. \n \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |986", "t": "Empowering Cooperative Structure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 986 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13/12/2022 \n \nEmpowering Cooperative Structure \n \n986.  SHRIMATI SARMISTHA SETHI:  \n         DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is taking any steps to empower/strengthen cooperative \nsocieties/structure in the country especially in the States of Jharkhand and Bihar, if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) whether any amount has been allocated for strengthening the cooperative structure in \nabove mentioned States and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the manner in which the Government intends to function without diluting the \nconstitutional mandate of cooperative federalism; and \n(d) the details in this regard and the proposed framework, if any? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n \n(a) Yes, sir. In order to revitalize the cooperative sector, empower cooperative societies and \ndeepening its reach to the grassroots in all States, including the States of Jharkhand and Bihar, \nthe Ministry of Cooperation has taken various initiatives such as: \ni. Project for Computerization of 63,000 functional Primary Agriculture Credit Societies \n(PACS), in next three years, with a total financial outlay of ₹2,516 Crore has been \napproved by the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs vide its decision dated 29th \nJune, 2022. This project entails bringing all the functional PACS onto an ERP (Enterprise \nResource Planning) based common software, linking them with NABARD through State \nCooperative Banks (StCBs) and District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs).  \nii. Model bye laws for PACS have been prepared after detailed stakeholder consultations, \nincluding all the States/ Union Territories and Ministries concerned, for adoption by \nPACS as per their respective State Cooperatives Societies Act. These Model Byelaws of \nPACS will enable them to undertake more than 25 business activities like dairy, fishery, \nfloriculture, setting up of godowns, procurement of foodgrains, fertilizers, seeds, LPG/ \nPetrol/ Green energy distribution agency, banking correspondents, common service \ncentres, etc. \niii. On 1st June, 2022, Union Cabinet has taken a decision allowing cooperatives to register \nas ‘buyer’ on Government e-Marketing platform, enabling them to procure goods and \nservices from nearly 40 lakh vendors registered on the GeM portal throughout the \ncountry. This will help cooperatives in making savings and improving transparency in \ntheir procurement system. \n \n\niv. Surcharge on Co-operative societies was reduced from 12 % to 7% for those co-operative \nsocieties having a total income of more than Rs. 1 Cr. and up to Rs. 10 Cr. to enhance the \nincome of Co-operative societies and its members. \nv. Minimum Alternate Tax (MAT) rate reduced for the Co-operatives from 18.5% to 15% to \nprovide them a level playing field with Corporates. \nvi. In order to provide adequate, affordable and timely credit to the co-operative institutions \nto give a boost to the co-operative based economic development model, non-scheduled \nUrban Co-operative Banks, State Co-operative Banks and District Central Co-operative \nBanks has been notified as Member Lending Institutions in Guarantee Fund Trust \n(CGTMSE) Scheme vide circular dated 3rd February, 2022. \nvii. Government vide its notification dated 25th October, 2021 offered a major relief to co-\noperative sugar mills by clarifying that sugar co-operative mills shall not be subjected to \nadditional income tax for paying higher sugarcane prices to farmers upto to the Fair and \nRemunerative Price (FRP) or State Advised Price (SAP), as the case may be. \nviii. National Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC), a statutory organization under \nthe Ministry of Cooperation, provides financial assistance to the cooperative sector and \nundertakes various activities such as strengthening share capital base of primary/ District \ncooperative marketing societies, setting up of processing centres, storage facilities, \nestablishing and modernizing cold chain, creation of cooperative banking units, \nagricultural services, Integrated Cooperative Development Projects, assistance for \ncomputerization of cooperatives, ‘Yuva-Sahkar’ for cooperative enterprise support and \ninnovation, ‘Ayushman Sahkar’ covering healthcare sector, ‘Nandini Sahkar’ to support \nwomen cooperatives etc.  \n \n(b) All initiatives being taken by the Ministry are oriented towards revitalizing the cooperatives \nin all sectors at all levels and in all States/ Union Territories, including the states of \nJharkhand and Bihar keeping the Constitutional provisions in mind. NCDC has provided \nfinancial assistance to the States of Jharkhand and Bihar under the Central Sector Integrated \nScheme on Agricultural Cooperation (CSISAC). The details of financial assistance released \nby  National Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC) as on 07.12.20"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |987", "t": "Rename of Schemes", "head": "   \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 987 \n  ANSWERED ON 13/12/2022 \n \nRENAME OF SCHEMES \n \n987.  SHRI NAKUL KAMAL NATH: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of schemes of the Ministry which have been renamed by the Union \nGovernment after 2014; and \n(b)  the details of allocation of budget to these schemes after 2014? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)  The schemes of the Ministry of Rural Development which have been renamed by the \nUnion Government after 2014 are as under:  \ni. The erstwhile Rural Housing Scheme Indira Awas Yojana (IAY) has been \nrestructured into Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G) with effect from \n1st April, 2016.  \nii. The Centrally Sponsored Scheme National Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM) was \nrenamed as Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana - National Rural Livelihoods Mission \n(DAY-NRLM) in  2015. Its skill component has be en renamed as Deen Dayal \nUpadhyaya-Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDUGKY) in 2014. \n(b) The year-wise details of Budget Estimate and Revised Estimate from 2015-16 to \n2022-23 is at Annexure. \n****** \n  \n  \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.987 for reply          on 13.12.2022 \n  \n            (i) The year-wise details of Budget Estimate and Revised Estimate under PMAY-G: \n                                                                                                                        (Rs. in crore) \nFinancial \nYear \nBudget Estimate (BE) Revised Estimate (RE) \n2015-16 10025.00 10025.00 \n2016-17 15000.00 16078.60 \n2017-18 23000.00 22832.31 \n2018-19 21000.00 19600.00 \n2019-20 19000.00 18455.20 \n2020-21 19500.00 19500.00 \n2021-22 30057.15 30056.87 \n2022-23 20000.00 - \n  \n          (ii) Budget Allocations Under DAY-NRLM since 2015-16 are as under: \n                                             \n                                                                                      (Rs. in crore) \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n               \n \n            \n* including Startup Village Entrepreneurship Programme (SVEP) \n  \nFinancial  \nYear Budget Estimate (BE) Revised  Estimate (RE) \n2015-16* 2705 2705 \n2016-17 3000 3168.15 \n2017-18 4500 4350.83 \n2018-19 5750 5783.5 \n2019-20 9024 9024 \n2020-21 9210.04 9209.53 \n2021-22 13677.61 10813.89 \n2022-23 13336.42  - "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|10|UNSTARRED |988", "t": "Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund (DIDF) to NDDB", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 988 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13THDECEMBER 2022 \nDAIRY PROCESSING AND INFRASTRUCTURE DEVELOPMENT FUND (DIDF) TO \nNDDB \n988. SHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the number of villages where milk producers are getting benefited from Dairy Processing \nand Infrastructure Development Fund, State-wise including Bijnor Constituency; \n(b) the amount of fund in the first installment of Rs. 440 crore to National Dairy Developmen t \nBoard (NDDB) under the Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund which has \nbeen spent to double the income of dairy farmers and the number of farmers benefited from \nit, State and district-wise; and \n(c) the details of the amount of financial assist ance being given/ has been given as loan under \nthe scheme and the percentage of interest subsidy being given to the milk institutions under \nthis scheme, State and district-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PAR